WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.630
- Welcome everybody to
the public board meeting

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.583
of NHS England.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.700
Can I first of all just note
the apologies for absence

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.950
that we've received a
number of our colleagues

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.710
who are with us this
morning in the private board

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.410
who the board will know we're
dialling in to the meeting

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.270
who are unable to dial into this meeting,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.970
is simply given the logistics,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.880
but we've received apologies
for this afternoon's meeting

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.800
from David Behan and Jeremy
Townsend, from Wol Kolade,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.913
Simon Wesley, Jacqui Rock,
and Helen Stokes-Lampard.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.260
Are there any declarations of interest

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.660
that members of the board
needs to declare now

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.633
that are new since your last registration?

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.030
Thank you. And can I
take the board's support

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.170
and approval of the minutes

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.663
of the meeting held on the 28th of March?

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.423
Thank you very much.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.370
Let me just make a few comments

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.310
just as chair at the start of this meeting

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.960
on some sort of organisational matters.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.670
First of all, and we
commented this morning,

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.310
and David's not here,
David Behan, as we know,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.350
has been appointed as the chair

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.250
for King's College
Hospital Foundation Trust.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:28.050
And that appointment plus
actually that in normal time flow

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:33.050
in terms of directors terms of office,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.930
we are about to launch
a recruitment programme,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.090
probably looking to recruit two

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.830
or three new non-executive directors.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.840
The processes of public
sector appointment process

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.910
does tend to take some time.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.240
So we're just launching that programme now

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.110
or that process now

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.420
in anticipation that it
may take several months

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.430
and therefore we do need to start now

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.410
essentially to ensure the board

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.290
maintains the right skill
set and mix of skills

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.560
as we see colleagues stepping away.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.550
In terms of appointments,

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:09.240
we have actually made a
very recent new appointment

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.790
as chair of cybersecurity for NHS England.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.250
So as you'll know, John
Noble is stepping down

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.130
again in June or July,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.310
and we've appointed Jamie
Saunders to take over from him

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:25.291
and he's now in the
process of his induction

00:02:25.291 --> 00:02:26.430
and that I think is a
very good appointment,

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.090
although will be sorry to
see John Noble leave us

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.030
and can I just welcome Suresh Viswanathan

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.253
to your first public board.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.500
In terms of meetings

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.690
and engagements that I've
had since the last board,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.510
let me just share just
a few of those with you.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:50.310
In terms of meetings, we
had a very good series

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.680
of calls with ICB chairs

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.690
and there's a particular
group of ICB chairs

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:55.860
and trust chairs who act

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.350
as an advisory group to me.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.200
We had a very good discussion yesterday

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.340
and that was actually thinking about

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.200
strategic communications

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.670
and how we think about the messaging

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.540
of NHS England's strategy

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.423
as we go forward over
the next months and year.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.670
In the area of innovation
and life sciences,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.050
I've had a couple of meetings actually

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.820
with the Tony Blair Institute

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.430
and I know other colleagues
have also done that,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.367
and that's on topics of
primary care transformation

00:03:26.367 --> 00:03:28.890
and also on data

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.440
and that's been a really,
really helpful engagement.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.670
I also had a very good session
with Lord James O'shaughnessy

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:37.050
and that again on innovation

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.223
and clinical trials in
particular was a good engagement.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.540
On Monday I attended as a board member,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.840
the Genomics England
Limited Strategy Board

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.420
and it was an all day event.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.450
There is a very good working relationship

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.320
between NHS England and
Genomics England Limited.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.320
And as we collaborate well and
work well together actually,

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.470
one can see really good strategic momentum

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.960
in the work that Genomics England is doing

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.480
where we are mutually
supportive to each other

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.620
and with Sue Hill who leads
to us here as the executive,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.530
that relationship she working
with the newly appointed CO

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.010
of Genomics England, Rich Scott

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.670
is proving evermore more fruitful.

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.760
And then we also had
about three weeks ago,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.760
we had the Genomics England
broader board meeting

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.610
and that does include
Genomics England Limited

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.230
as one of its constituent members,

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.630
but also captures our own genomic.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.230
We have seven genomics
labs around the NHS,

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:48.230
and the NHS does about 90% of
the genomics testing actually

00:04:48.605 --> 00:04:51.780
that's done across the health system

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.736
and Genomics England with
its whole genome sequencing

00:04:54.736 --> 00:04:56.220
does around around 8% of it.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.970
So actually we gather
the broader operations

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:00.803
of genomics together

00:05:00.803 --> 00:05:02.760
and we make sure that we are coordinating

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.920
and we had, again, a very good

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.590
forward looking board meeting.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.270
And then lastly, I had the great pleasure

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.700
of attending a life
sciences industry dinner,

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.950
actually, which was
one, a very good dinner

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.590
and two actually some
very interesting people

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.540
and a good conversation.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.913
And Vin joined me there.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.690
In terms of meetings with public bodies

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.643
if I had described them
as that with Amanda,

00:05:27.643 --> 00:05:29.460
a very good meeting with
Dr Jayne Chidgey-Clark,

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.560
so the National Guardian's Office.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.580
Separately a meeting
with the chief executive

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:35.820
of the National Audit Office.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.760
Again, a very good discussion.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:39.960
I've had a chair to chair meeting

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.870
with the NHS Business Service Authority

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:46.710
and also chair to chair meeting with CQC,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.470
and then with patient groups meetings

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.496
with Cancer Research UK
with Michelle Mitchell,

00:05:52.496 --> 00:05:54.210
which was very good.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.030
And also with the UK Sepsis Trust.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:59.220
And then I attended for NHS England

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.990
and with Chris Hopson, we
both attended the Health ALBs

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:07.803
senior Leaders meeting
actually hosted by the DHSC.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:15.510
In terms of other meetings,
if I just choose a meeting

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:19.083
with Lord Markham in
the department actually,

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:25.620
there's a very regular meeting
where we review the NHS app

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.810
and the increase in its functionality

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.240
and the plans and the
development of the NHS app.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.890
And so, I now attend those

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.900
and they're virtually on a weekly basis.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.780
And again, Vin is often
also at those meetings.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.240
And then separately on the
new hospitals programme

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.940
led here for NHS England by Julian.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:45.840
Again, a good exchange with Lord Markham

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.990
and, the team both in
the Department of Health

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:52.050
and also from within NHS England.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.300
And then in terms of visits,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.150
again, I made this comment this morning.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.220
They are quite close to us physically,

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.450
the London Ambulance Service.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.670
So again, they had the
pleasure of my company.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.980
Perhaps that's not quite
the right description,

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.710
but I went again to see
the London ambulance.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.110
So it was actually in a
tactical operations centre

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.100
and that was really helpful
to me and instructive.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.360
A number of events,

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.900
I made a keynote speech at
the London Business School

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.490
with Chris Hopson.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:24.600
We hosted a breakfast round
table on productivity.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.070
Again, I did a keynote speech

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:28.620
at the pharmacy clinical leadership event

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.743
and also for the Seacole Group AGM.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.220
If I stop giving you my diary

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.370
and reflect instead on today's meeting,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:43.370
I think we've got a really
very good set of agenda items,

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.720
some really important discussion points.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.660
So as well as hearing from
Amanda, and then from Emily

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.330
and from Julian on so
that on the CEO's update

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:52.710
and operational performance

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.450
and on financial performance,

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.640
we are going to have a
conversation about productivity,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:58.710
which I think is really good.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:07:59.640
We can have an update

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.220
on the delivery of the
first year of the maternity

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.260
and neonatal three year delivery plan

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.313
and reflect more broadly,
I think upon maternity.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.280
We are going to have a conversation,

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.800
an agenda item here on
research in the NHS,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:18.800
a truly important constituent
of what the NHS does.

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:23.520
And then we're also going to
reflect on a point of closure,

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.960
which is actually we are
closing an oversight committee

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.830
attaching to what's called
the new NHS England programme.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:35.220
And I'll talk a little bit more
just about that in a moment.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.530
So the agenda today is
probably going to be

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:38.550
at something of a gallup.

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:42.600
It's quite a full agenda,
but it's also a meaty agenda.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.703
I think in the topics covered,

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:49.410
if I just just reflect back
on productivity for a moment

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:54.410
and how we should be able to
deliver more for patients,

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.400
I just want to reflect on
some current performance that

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.620
we should just reflect
on how much NHS staff

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.470
are delivering in the here and now,

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:13.980
and remind everybody that we
are clearly not standing still,

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.470
and that improvements are
constantly being made.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.030
The most recent monthly data,

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:21.240
and Emily, I'm sure would
draw some of this out

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:22.860
and better than I will,

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:24.210
but the most recent monthly data

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:25.770
shows that for the second month in a row

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.680
we've met the faster diagnosis standard,

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.900
77% of people getting all clear

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.180
or a definitive diagnosis
within four weeks.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.060
That's almost 200,000 people in a month.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.330
And an earlier diagnosis, as
we all know is truly important

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:43.410
as the sooner we're able
to catch the cancer,

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.450
then it's easier to treat.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.160
GP appointments.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.500
There were absolute record levels.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.920
In February, there were
30 million GP appointments

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.860
and that compares with
24 million appointments

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.630
in the same month pre pandemic,

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.540
a 23% increase in the
number of appointments

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:04.770
that GPs are that are
offering to patients.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.200
And on elective activity
over the past year,

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.710
so from April '23 to March '24,

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.590
almost 17 and a half million appointments

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.540
and procedures were delivered.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.580
And that was up by almost a quarter

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.910
of a million on the 12
months pre the pandemic.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.550
And whilst we know that
there's still much to do

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.980
and particularly on
urgent and emergency care

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.440
and that there is significant pressure

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.740
last year for our ANE
performance did improve

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.793
for the first time
outside of the pandemic.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.340
And we'll be publishing some

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:44.400
new analysis today just
touching on a range of things.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.130
But one of the things
we'll touch on is actually

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.590
the impact of virtual wards,

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.770
and virtual wards where we
have nearly 12,000 of them.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:54.210
I remember when that target was set

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:56.490
and we thought, can we get
to 10,000 virtual wards?

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.643
And I think we got to
11,717, something like that.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.460
And so we beat the target

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:04.860
and we were sceptical
that we'd hit the target.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.330
And now as we look into this next year,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:07.980
we know that we are
going to work really hard

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:12.720
to improve the occupancy
levels in those virtual wards.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.430
But in the southeast alone,

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:19.260
in the past year 9,000 hospital
admissions were avoided

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.510
because of what is a
world leading initiative,

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:23.850
which is these virtual ward beds.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:25.650
And that's very good news for the patients

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.711
who can be treated from the
comfort of their own homes,

00:11:28.711 --> 00:11:33.711
but it also helps alleviate
clearly their conditions.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:34.890
And it's one example of

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.770
how technology can improve productivity.

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.360
And we know that there are many, many ways

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.543
that we can transform care
for patients for the better.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.573
Staying on that technology point,

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.660
and I referenced the
meetings with Lord Markham

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:51.690
and with Vin on the NHS app,
it's one of the big bets

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:53.220
that we are making right now

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.500
and that's the increasing functionality

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.237
being applied through the NHS app.

00:11:57.237 --> 00:11:58.687
And it really is, if we think

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.130
how it might continue to develop.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.830
When we look back in five
years time, we look back,

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.260
I think it'd be one of the
things where people say

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:09.420
that was absolutely transformational

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:14.010
for the way society engages
with the health service,

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.350
but it also brings a cost saving benefit.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:18.767
And over the last financial year,

00:12:18.767 --> 00:12:22.260
over 22 million messages
was sent to patients

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.240
through the NHS app.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.490
And they might otherwise
have been sent as a letter

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:29.490
or perhaps a text message.

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:33.333
And that's a saving of
over 1 million pounds.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.120
So I often use this phrase

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:41.820
that we can't be pollyannaish

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.640
and we know that we suffer
very real pressures,

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:49.640
very real pressures from
ongoing strong growth in demand.

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.080
But I think it's important
to recognise that

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.730
through the hard work

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:56.757
and the efforts of our workforce actually,

00:12:56.757 --> 00:12:59.610
that the NHS today is
providing absolutely record

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.500
levels of healthcare.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:03.540
So we provide record levels of healthcare

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:06.180
through the very good
work of our workforce

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:07.887
and I know we've got lots more to do

00:13:07.887 --> 00:13:10.863
and we need to keep up
with growing demand.

00:13:13.496 --> 00:13:15.270
One of the other items
we're going to talk about

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:16.950
outside productivity by the way,

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:20.340
that long narrative was a
link to to productivity,

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:24.270
but is actually what's called
here the Change Programme.

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:27.570
That's this new NHS England committee.

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:29.460
And I would just like
to start this meeting

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:32.040
by thanking everybody involved

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.140
in what has been a very
important piece of work

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:35.910
over the last two years.

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:38.160
So to Steve Russell for
the executives thank you,

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:42.030
but also Amanda for your
leadership and engagement in it.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.170
And he's not here but to Wol Kolade

00:13:45.397 --> 00:13:46.830
who led and chaired
the oversight committee

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:48.690
of this piece of work.

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:52.530
It hasn't been easy and it
hasn't been straightforward,

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:57.450
but it is one of the largest
public sector transformations

00:13:57.450 --> 00:13:59.010
in this country.

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:02.550
And the restructure of NHS England

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:07.550
absorbing NHS Improvement,
NHS Digital, X and HEE

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.483
has created a new organisation,

00:14:11.483 --> 00:14:16.350
still expert, but over a third smaller.

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:19.616
And I'd also like to thank
not just Wol and Steve

00:14:19.616 --> 00:14:22.200
and Amanda, but all
these senior executives

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:25.710
because actually the stress on
you as you manage this merger

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:29.190
and this reshaping of the
organisation was continuing

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:33.840
to provide the role that you
do provide into the NHS system.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:35.040
Thank you for that.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:39.150
And I should also thank actually
clearly all of our staff

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:40.590
for the way they've engaged

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:43.320
with what has been a
very demanding programme

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:46.260
with often actually
real personal challenge

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:48.576
to individuals amongst them.

00:14:48.576 --> 00:14:52.990
You know, we have reduced
our headcount by over a third

00:14:54.616 --> 00:14:58.140
and in doing that actually
we'll shift funding

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.380
to the front line and
we'll reduce bureaucracy,

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:06.270
and we'll still retain
that head office expertise.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.640
So it's been a really important initiative

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:10.050
and actually today we mark

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.210
the closure of that oversight committee

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:13.920
and now we need to make sure we continue

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.133
to run the new model very effectively.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:19.950
I do need, I think just to comment Amanda,

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:23.220
very briefly on maternity,

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:26.130
we have the topic coming up today.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:29.280
About a month ago, three
weeks or a month ago,

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.830
the board had a deep dive
board call discussion

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:38.880
and narrative and a
discussion around maternity.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.600
But we've all seen the
distressing testimonies

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:45.540
from patients who
experienced maternity care

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:48.180
that was simply not good enough

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:51.420
and not what the NHS wants
or expects for patients.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:54.330
And we are working with
local leaders at all trusts

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:58.410
to build and nurture a culture
where women are listened to

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:00.600
and their choices are respected,

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:05.520
and that care is personable
and equitable and safe.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.278
We're now a year into our

00:16:08.278 --> 00:16:11.236
three year maternity delivery plans,

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:13.680
we're going to get an update from Ruth

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:15.840
and it does feel to all of us I think,

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:18.719
we had that conversation
say three or four weeks ago,

00:16:18.719 --> 00:16:21.480
that it's the right time again
to take stock of progress

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:24.840
and where we need to go further, faster.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:28.410
But this work also reiterates
the importance of listening,

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:31.830
and also our freedom to
speak up responsibilities.

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:34.320
But certainly, the
importance of listening.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:37.890
And it's incumbent on us as
members of the NHS England board

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:40.230
to listen to patients
and to listen to staff

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.820
who raise concerns,

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:43.773
but not just to listen but then to act,

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:46.681
which is why you know,

00:16:46.681 --> 00:16:49.500
we are updating our speaking up policies,

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:52.800
it's why actually we as leaders
should send the right tone

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:55.260
from the top in an area
of great importance.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:57.360
It's sort of an intangible cultural cement

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:59.190
about the way an
organisation should behave

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.530
in the way it should signal.

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:04.920
But going back to where
that that theme started,

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.941
the maternity conversation today

00:17:06.941 --> 00:17:09.030
is a really important conversation,

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:11.490
and I look forward to that.

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:13.860
So let me finish there Amanda,

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:16.350
I've gone on for longer than
I thought, so I apologise.

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:17.800
I may have made some comments

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:21.341
that you might have been
going to make going to make,

00:17:21.341 --> 00:17:22.650
but can I hand over to you for
the chief executives report?

00:17:22.650 --> 00:17:23.910
- Yeah, of course Richard, thank you.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:26.760
And some of the things
I want to say do overlap

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:27.870
but that sort of as it should be

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:29.490
because these I think are the big issues

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:34.110
that we've been working on
and are thinking about today.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.540
So first thing I wanted to do

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:39.060
is just update colleagues
on planning for '24/'25.

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:41.400
Again, we're going to touch on that more

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:43.860
in the course of today's meeting.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:44.790
Colleagues will remember that

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.010
we were able to start the planning round

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:48.960
with the publication of the priorities

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.340
and operational planning guidance

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:52.170
just before the last board.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:54.270
That was the 27th of March.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.070
And the focus of planning
guidance this year

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:58.980
was really about continued
improvement on recovery

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:00.990
just as Richard has said,

00:18:00.990 --> 00:18:03.510
focusing on delivering the
long-term workforce plan.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.780
And that includes of
course a really important

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:09.180
focus on looking after our staff,

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:11.790
and also a focus on
building for the future.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:16.590
So as we did last year, we've
started meeting with the ICBs

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:20.310
and providers in each system
to discuss their draught plans.

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:23.220
So far I think we have covered 20 systems

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:26.790
and that's been in the
Midlands and southwest London

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:29.220
and a couple in the southeast.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:31.920
I suspect Emily and Julian
may well want to add

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:33.510
their reflections from those visits.

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.970
But I have to say mine would be

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:39.600
that whilst it is clearly
really challenging,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.580
I have been really impressed by

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:43.500
how hard colleagues are working

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:47.040
to ensure that we do have
robust plans for the year ahead

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:49.863
whilst also getting on with delivery now.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:54.150
Talking about planning
and some of the unknowns,

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:58.140
certainly we have had a year

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:01.500
where one of the unknowns
that we had to deal with was

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:03.360
around industrial action.

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:06.750
So I thought it was just
worth taking a moment

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:09.420
to welcome the news last
night that the government

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.400
and junior doctors committee

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:15.420
are taking forward
independently mediated talks.

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:19.950
I know we all hope that
this does take, you know,

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.230
everybody a step further
towards a settlement

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:25.950
which allows the NHS to
have a clear run at recovery

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:27.750
in the year ahead.

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:29.310
On the less welcome side,

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:31.860
while we have been
meeting today as a board,

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:33.270
the GPC has announced

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.490
that it is balloting GP contract holders

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:37.350
on a form of collective action,

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:39.270
although we won't see the result of that

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.643
until the end of July at the earliest.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.780
Richard, you've just spent
a moment talking about

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:48.723
productivity and also technology.

00:19:48.723 --> 00:19:51.240
So I just wanted again to pick up on that

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:54.060
because that has been
another really big focus

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:55.320
for the last few weeks

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:58.020
because we've been
working on taking forward

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.270
the planning work to determine now

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:02.790
how we make the most of the capital

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.950
made available through the spring budget,

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:07.920
which includes a big
investment in technology

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:09.740
starting from next year.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:11.790
So we had a really good,

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:15.360
and I'd say very energising

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.610
and thoughtful discussion with ICB

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:20.460
and trust chief executives
earlier this month,

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.650
which was particularly
focused on productivity

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:24.840
as a theme for the whole day.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:26.490
That included technology,

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:30.240
but it also included how
we embrace the opportunity

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:34.890
that NHS Impact offers to
embed continuous improvement

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:36.840
as the way that we deliver operational

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:37.890
and clinical excellence.

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:39.540
And we'll discuss all of that more

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:42.033
Julian, when we come
to your paper later on.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:44.460
Colleagues will also remember

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:48.060
that at the last public
board we discussed ADHD

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:51.600
and the intention to create
a new cross-sector task force

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:53.070
to gain a better understanding

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.650
of the challenges
affecting those with ADHD

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:57.840
and what we can
collectively do in response

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:00.510
really recognising it
was a much broader issue

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:02.730
than just the NHS.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:04.890
So I'm really pleased
that we have now appointed

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:09.000
the co-chairs of that task
force Professor Anita Thapar,

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.550
a child psychiatrist
and clinician scientist

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:12.990
and she is an advisor

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:14.970
to the Welsh government on these issues.

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:17.160
And Joanna Killian who's
the chief executive

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:19.020
of the local government association

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:21.150
and that obviously importantly recognising

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.010
the crucial role that local councils play.

00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:25.890
So just want to note my thanks

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:27.420
and no doubt our thanks to them

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:30.183
for taking on this
incredibly important work.

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:33.780
You've mentioned it already Richard,

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:36.690
but as we'll discuss later,

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:39.930
the last stages of creating
the new NHS England

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:42.180
has occupied a lot of colleagues time

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:43.770
over the past few weeks.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.470
And I would also like to add
my thanks to yours Richard

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:49.620
and thank Steve in particular, Wol,

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:51.960
but also all of the other colleagues

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:54.270
who have played such an important part

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:59.270
in this very complex programme of work.

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:01.260
We're going to talk
about it in detail later.

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:03.150
But I think it's worth saying that whilst,

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:05.700
and you've said it, but
I want to add to it,

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:08.250
while the benefits of the
programme are significant

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:10.620
and are going to be significant,

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:13.020
I really do want to just acknowledge

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:17.190
the impact that that has had
on staff within NHS England

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:20.010
and to note my thanks for how committed

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:24.870
so many people have been
to doing really good work

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:26.880
despite that disruption.

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:29.340
We know now the big challenge for us

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:32.970
is to focus on how as a single
streamlined organisation

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:36.060
we really do best deliver
on our responsibilities

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:39.402
to the wider NHS, to
patients and to the public

00:22:39.402 --> 00:22:41.460
and that is our opportunity now.

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:42.573
Few more things from me.

00:22:42.573 --> 00:22:44.823
I've just got three more
if that's all right.

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:53.897
Just three things I wanted
to talk about all timely.

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:56.220
The first relates

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.800
to delivery of the long
term workforce plan.

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:01.440
We have said, in our planning guidance,

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.530
I've already mentioned
that that is an absolutely

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:07.980
key priority for us in the year ahead.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:09.030
And that one of the things

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:10.380
that we're particularly focused on

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:11.580
is looking after our staff.

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:14.730
So that is the retention part of the plan

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:18.420
alongside of course reform and train.

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:22.440
So in that spirit, we have just published

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:24.240
or just recently published

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:26.880
a letter to the NHS on
improving the working lives

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:28.500
of doctors in training,

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:30.901
I'm sure colleagues saw that.

00:23:30.901 --> 00:23:31.830
And that was really recognising

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:34.440
that there are some very specific things

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.080
that affect doctors in
training which are different

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:38.880
to the things that affect
the rest of our staff,

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:42.780
particularly to do with the
rotational nature of training.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:45.456
But, we also,

00:23:45.456 --> 00:23:48.930
we never forget the needs
of all of our other staff.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:50.910
Were able to announce yesterday

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:54.000
a partnership with NHS charities together,

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:57.540
which is again a new partnership
with match funding from us

00:23:57.540 --> 00:24:01.320
to enable colleagues to work with staff

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:03.960
to understand what those additional

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:05.253
sort of smaller things are

00:24:05.253 --> 00:24:07.320
that we know really do make a difference

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:09.270
to how people feel about
their working environment

00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:10.740
and their working lives.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:14.460
So that was just another important step

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.280
in delivering on our commitments.

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:19.470
I just wanted to say as well though,

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:21.660
that we did hear a very
clear message from doctors

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:24.450
a few weeks ago on the
importance of health

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:28.830
and wellbeing support in
particular, psychological support.

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:31.590
So as we said, we would in
the long-term workforce plan,

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:35.280
we are working to
finalise a terms of review

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:37.920
to make sure that all
staff, not just doctors,

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:40.380
all staff, get the support they need

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:44.100
on the psychological side
as well as more widely.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:45.600
But we do know we got it wrong

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:47.730
on the practitioner health contract,

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:49.320
and that is why we said sorry,

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:52.170
and that is why we took urgent
action with their support

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:54.520
to make sure that that
contract could continue.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:57.900
Richard, you've talked about maternity,

00:24:57.900 --> 00:24:59.790
I just wanted to add my voice to that

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:01.530
and again, I know colleagues
are going to talk about it

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:03.420
in more detail later,

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.030
and it is timely that
we've got a paper today

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:08.940
on progress against our
three year delivery plan,

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:10.260
which is about improving quality

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:13.830
and safety and maternity and
neonatal care across England.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:15.630
And it's timely because of what you said,

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:17.430
which is that we have just seen on Monday

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:22.430
the publication of the APPG
Report on Birth Trauma.

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:27.450
And that did contain some
really powerful testimonies

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:30.800
from women and families who
have had a bad experience

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:32.880
of services previously.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:35.220
And whilst it is difficult reading,

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:37.906
I know colleagues will have done so

00:25:37.906 --> 00:25:40.980
and will have taken the time
to read the report as I have.

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:42.843
Much of what that report says,

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.710
the recommendations do chime strongly

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:48.330
with what we know we need to do

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:50.430
and what is in the plan already.

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:52.830
Things like perinatal
mental health services,

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:55.980
pelvic health services, GP checks

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:57.300
and we have seen progress on them.

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:59.310
We will talk about that later.

00:25:59.310 --> 00:26:02.070
But just to say, Richard,
you're so right when you say

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:05.160
we know there is still so much more to do

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:07.470
and so much work ahead.

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:09.120
A very strong theme of the report

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:11.220
was that women didn't feel listened to,

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:13.230
and they didn't feel that
they were always shown

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:15.000
kindness and compassion.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:17.100
And that is a theme we have heard before

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:20.043
and it's a focus of our
three year delivery plan too.

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:23.130
The APPG suggestion for cross government.

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:26.490
And that means not just NHS
England, and not just the NHS

00:26:26.490 --> 00:26:30.000
but a genuinely cross
government strategy on maternity

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.430
is something that I and
the Secretary of State

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:34.290
absolutely support.

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:37.800
We really welcome that and we
will be taking that forward.

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:42.656
So final thing to end on a
positive, I just want to,

00:26:42.656 --> 00:26:43.489
'cause we didn't always talk about this

00:26:43.489 --> 00:26:45.240
but I thought it was worth just saying,

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:49.230
I really want to congratulate
the greener NHS team

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:50.063
for the support

00:26:50.063 --> 00:26:53.190
that they have been giving
to NHS organisations

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:56.343
to access the public sector
decarbonization scheme.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:59.400
200 million has been announced this week

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:03.390
for 20 trusts to support
energy and cost saving efforts.

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:06.300
And what that means is, more
money for frontline care,

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:08.430
less spend on overheads

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:11.190
and that takes the total
investment awarded to the NHS

00:27:11.190 --> 00:27:14.280
through PSDS to over a billion pounds

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.950
across over a hundred projects.

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:18.270
So that is a real achievement

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:21.000
and that is thanks to
our having a clear vision

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:23.940
and a proven ability to
deliver capital improvements

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:25.470
when we get the right support.

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:26.613
So thanks to them.

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:31.980
- Thank you Amanda. I won't at this stage

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:33.960
ask colleagues if they want
to react to anything I said

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:37.380
or Amanda said, but
we'll pause after Emily

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:41.010
and then Julian have done the
operational performance update

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:42.540
and then Julian the financial update

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:43.980
that's the case. So Emily to you first.

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:45.300
- Thank you.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:47.885
And I should add that Sarah-Jayne

00:27:47.885 --> 00:27:49.008
will also give an update

00:27:49.008 --> 00:27:50.269
on the year two of the UEC plan briefly.

00:27:50.269 --> 00:27:52.320
- [Richard] Yes, good point.
- As part of my update chair.

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:55.800
So I said the last public board that

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:57.450
I thought the story of the last

00:27:57.450 --> 00:27:59.260
of this winter was effectively

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:04.560
an extraordinary effort
from staff across the NHS

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:06.900
to deliver some really challenging targets

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:11.310
and still a long way to go in
terms of delivering exactly

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:13.620
as we would hope every day.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.443
And I think that will be
the theme today as well.

00:28:16.443 --> 00:28:18.870
Some absolutely brilliant improvements

00:28:18.870 --> 00:28:22.530
that I want to mention and
equally lots of ambition

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:25.380
for this year in terms of how
we can continue to improve.

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:29.820
And just to take the
theme that Amanda started

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:32.130
of reflections on the ICB

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:33.240
planning meetings that we've been doing.

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:36.093
We've done 20 out of 42 so far.

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:41.760
I think that's been
reflected in those meetings

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:44.610
where we've set some
extremely challenging targets

00:28:44.610 --> 00:28:47.163
in the planning guidance,
and we've had some very,

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:50.970
about to say robust, but I
think that work can be overused

00:28:50.970 --> 00:28:54.005
but I'll say it

00:28:54.005 --> 00:28:55.020
'cause I can't think of
what the word is now,

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:58.530
but some robust discussions
which just makes it clear

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:00.510
that the plans that have been submitted

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.850
have been really well thought through.

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:07.850
There are not sort of
ambitious unrealistic plans

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:08.850
on those pages.

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:10.366
There are plans

00:29:10.366 --> 00:29:11.430
that have been thought
through very carefully

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:13.380
to make the most of our resources

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:15.900
to deliver for patients every day

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:18.540
and some brilliant
examples of system working

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:20.490
to make sure we can keep
on meeting these really

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:21.330
stretching targets.

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:22.920
That doesn't mean we will be exactly

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:24.660
always where we want to be, but,

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:27.480
I'm incredibly encouraged by those efforts

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.780
and by the analysis
that's underpinning them.

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:32.430
I'll come back to UEC at the end

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:34.471
because I'm going to
hand over to Sarah-Jane

00:29:34.471 --> 00:29:35.996
for some of that.

00:29:35.996 --> 00:29:38.010
But if I just talk briefly about elective,

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:39.900
which obviously is a huge focus

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:41.880
in terms of bringing down long waits

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:43.500
and making sure we can treat patients

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:44.853
as speedily as possible.

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:49.560
I'll refer to the March published figures

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.560
which were in the super stats
that were published last week

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:57.453
and what we saw in March
'24 compared to February '24

00:29:57.453 --> 00:30:00.150
was that the total weighting
list decreased very marginally

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:05.150
by 0.01%, 916 people

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:09.330
and stood at the end of
March at 7.54 million,

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:13.620
104 week waits decreased by 7.94%,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:18.620
78 week waits decreased
significantly by 52%,

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:21.210
and is now at the lowest
since was at the end of March,

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:23.670
the lowest since reporting
started in April '21

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:25.890
against this particular metric.

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:30.890
65 week weights also decreased
significantly by 34.71%

00:30:31.020 --> 00:30:34.503
and at the end of March we're 48.15% lower

00:30:34.503 --> 00:30:36.780
than in March, 2023.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:38.400
So there's been a tremendous effort

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:40.650
through the year despite industrial action

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:43.890
and definite hits to capacity
to do the right thing

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:46.080
and bring those long waits down.

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:47.490
The 52 week waiting list

00:30:47.490 --> 00:30:50.081
however shows that we
still have plenty to do

00:30:50.081 --> 00:30:51.270
and that was the only long wait metric

00:30:51.270 --> 00:30:54.150
that increased in March
compared to February.

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:58.833
It increased by 1.39% to 309,300.

00:31:00.390 --> 00:31:04.410
I wanted to dwell a bit
on the cancer targets

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:07.860
because I think that's been
an extraordinary example

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:11.320
of setting extremely ambitious targets

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:16.080
and the whole system
pulling together to improve.

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:20.220
So with clinical leadership
we've done everything

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:24.060
from increasing capacity through
complete pathway redesign

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:28.320
to creative use of
capacity across weekends

00:31:28.320 --> 00:31:29.800
and evening sessions

00:31:31.117 --> 00:31:36.117
and the building of additional capacity.

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:38.400
And we've seen that in the results

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:41.280
in that we met the 28 day
faster diagnosis target

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:43.200
for cancer in both February and March

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:46.450
for the first time since the
standard was introduced in 2021

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:49.380
and perhaps most importantly

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:51.780
that improvement was
universal across the country

00:31:51.780 --> 00:31:54.900
rather than concentrated
in a small number of areas.

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:57.660
So every region met the
target in both months

00:31:57.660 --> 00:32:00.150
and national performance has
been consistently running

00:32:00.150 --> 00:32:02.790
at two to 4% above the
same month last year

00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:04.290
for 10 months now.

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:06.540
And Richard's already
given the number about

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:08.610
how many more patients have
therefore had their diagnosis

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:09.873
within 28 years.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:15.150
We also showed at the end of March that

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:18.240
patients who've been
waiting longer than 62 days

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:20.430
otherwise known as the 62 day backlog

00:32:20.430 --> 00:32:22.950
ended the year at 14,916,

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:25.433
which is the lowest it's
been since April, 2020,

00:32:26.407 --> 00:32:28.020
A 20,000 reduction on the peak

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:30.300
of the backlog in September '22,

00:32:30.300 --> 00:32:32.730
and therefore a full
recovery from the expansion

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:35.160
of the waiting list over
the course of the pandemic.

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:36.210
And as with FDS,

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:39.573
every single region met their
population adjusted target.

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:42.840
Given where we started the year,

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:45.450
which was a 40% gap
between the best and worst

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:48.060
and we've reduced that to a 22% gap.

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:50.670
This is huge progress
in particular in meeting

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:52.263
the challenge of variation,

00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:55.980
and the number of systems that
reached the ambition increase

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:58.290
from 16 out of 42 at the
beginning of the year

00:32:58.290 --> 00:33:01.860
to 35 out of 42 at the end of the year.

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:03.930
The maximum distance any individual system

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:08.063
has been from the target
reduced from a 100% to just 12%.

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:10.050
This means that the NHS

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:12.300
has met both headline
performance expectations

00:33:12.300 --> 00:33:14.280
for cancer into '23/'24,

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:15.660
and puts us in a strong position

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:16.860
to make further improvements

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:18.720
back towards constitutional standards

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:21.810
as we move into '24/'25.

00:33:21.810 --> 00:33:23.760
Given the relatively strong performance

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:26.460
for the faster diagnosis
standard, we are confident

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:29.340
that we'll continue to meet
those targets through the year.

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:31.440
We expect 62 day performance

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:35.010
to be the key operational
focus in the coming year.

00:33:35.010 --> 00:33:37.022
So if we look at it like that,

00:33:37.022 --> 00:33:39.000
68.7% of people treated

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:41.130
began their first definitive
treatment for cancer

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:44.580
within 62 days of an urgent
suspected cancer referral,

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:47.640
breast symptomatic referral,
urgent screening referral

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:49.290
or consultant upgrade.

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:51.450
That's 1.2 points up from last year

00:33:51.450 --> 00:33:54.840
and the highest it's been since April '22.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:56.190
But there is clearly more to do

00:33:56.190 --> 00:33:58.830
and that will be our focus
over the next 10 months.

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:00.900
The planning guidance
sets a clear expectation

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:05.073
that performance will reach
70% nationally by March, 2025.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:09.480
Having met with the 20 out of
42 ICSs as I mentioned so far

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:11.340
as part of the planning process,

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:13.437
I know that some systems
also plan to exceed that

00:34:13.437 --> 00:34:16.140
and they can see how they can
do that within their existing

00:34:16.140 --> 00:34:18.720
or the new planned capacity

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:20.770
and obviously I'm keen to encourage that.

00:34:22.410 --> 00:34:25.080
A couple of quick headlines on diagnostics

00:34:25.080 --> 00:34:26.640
and mental health.

00:34:26.640 --> 00:34:28.800
So the published statistics
show we're continuing

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:32.280
to deliver high level of activity 113%

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:34.293
of pre pandemic levels in March,

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:40.160
and each month in '23/'24
has seen higher activity

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:42.540
than the same month in any previous year.

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:44.760
Although I want to acknowledge on my point

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:46.710
that there's a long way
to go on some of these.

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:48.120
Partly due to growth in demand,

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:51.720
there's still need for ongoing improvement

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:54.090
and efficiency and use of capacity

00:34:54.090 --> 00:34:57.000
as in March we still had 21.8% of patients

00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:58.650
who'd waited more than six weeks,

00:34:58.650 --> 00:35:01.350
which obviously is not what we would want.

00:35:01.350 --> 00:35:03.570
In terms of mental health,

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:05.220
I wanted to call out the improvement

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:06.810
in the dementia diagnosis rate,

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:10.350
which was at 64.8% at March '24,

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:13.350
against the national ambition of 66.7%

00:35:13.350 --> 00:35:16.210
and has shown steady
improvements since January '23

00:35:17.118 --> 00:35:20.430
and we've asked for that to
be a continued focus this year

00:35:20.430 --> 00:35:22.290
and recognising that across all

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:25.540
of our mental health targets
there's extremely high demand

00:35:26.670 --> 00:35:28.530
which is reflected

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:30.810
in the fact that out of area placements

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:32.520
where ambition would be zero also

00:35:32.520 --> 00:35:34.920
remains high at this point.

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:36.690
An indication of that volume is children,

00:35:36.690 --> 00:35:40.290
young people accessing services
is continuing to increase

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:44.420
with 780,963 accessing services

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:47.310
in the 12 months to February '24.

00:35:47.310 --> 00:35:51.480
So, plenty to do to keep on improving.

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:54.510
If I talk about UEC performance

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:58.410
where we have the April numbers
that have been published.

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:01.110
We've seen pressure remain high on UEC

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:05.075
with ED attendances
9.9% higher in April '24

00:36:05.075 --> 00:36:07.140
than in 2023 in the same month,

00:36:07.140 --> 00:36:10.380
and ambulance services
answering 3% more calls.

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:14.795
Emergency admissions also
continue to rise with April 10.9%

00:36:14.795 --> 00:36:16.260
above April '23.

00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:18.210
We did however see a further improvement

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:21.430
in four hour performance up 0.2% to 74.4%,

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:27.150
but acknowledging that is
down from 74.6% in April '23.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:29.520
There was also an improvement
in cat two performance,

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.470
which was down to 30 minutes in April,

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:34.050
which is almost four minutes
improvement from March.

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:37.230
But above the 28 minutes
and 33 seconds in April '23,

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:39.873
we've really seen quite a
different April this year.

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:43.797
One of our priorities for this year

00:36:43.797 --> 00:36:45.750
are the challenges with
the number of patients

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:48.030
who are waiting 12 hours
or more to be admitted,

00:36:48.030 --> 00:36:50.010
transferred or discharged.

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:54.510
And in April we saw 58% more
12 hour delays than April '23.

00:36:54.510 --> 00:36:56.340
Our planning guidance is set out in detail

00:36:56.340 --> 00:36:58.080
how we'll work with systems to reduce

00:36:58.080 --> 00:36:59.610
the proportion of long waits in ANE

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:02.430
as well as improve across
all of these metrics

00:37:02.430 --> 00:37:04.380
and continuing the support

00:37:04.380 --> 00:37:06.150
and the joint working across the system

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:10.470
that's seen such tremendous
improvements in the last year.

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:13.080
And Sarah-Jane, tell us about

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:15.510
what you've laid out in the plan.

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:16.343
- Thanks Emily.

00:37:16.343 --> 00:37:18.600
So the letter that we have shared today

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:21.180
is really about how we
can build on the learning

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:23.635
that we've got from '23/'24.

00:37:23.635 --> 00:37:26.850
The UEC recovery plan was
clearly a two year document

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:29.460
and it's really important
that the plan is the plan.

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:30.840
We still believe in the content of that

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:33.600
and that is absolutely
the right things to do.

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:35.880
But we've learned a a lot along the way.

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:37.860
We really wanted to have the main emphasis

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:39.540
was to share that learning,

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:42.090
and help and support
systems with the next part

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:43.980
of the journey of improvement.

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:46.980
So just in terms of just
the high level points,

00:37:46.980 --> 00:37:49.320
I'm sure colleagues
would've read the letter

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:51.360
and supporting plans.

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:53.670
So, we have made a lot of progress.

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:58.290
It's really important that we
continue on that trajectory.

00:37:58.290 --> 00:38:00.090
Our targets for this year are,

00:38:00.090 --> 00:38:03.000
the cat two response
time is the same standard

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:05.070
as it was last year but
obviously we were some way

00:38:05.070 --> 00:38:06.660
six minutes adrift of meeting it.

00:38:06.660 --> 00:38:10.800
So, absolutely recommitment
to achieving that target.

00:38:10.800 --> 00:38:11.880
And then we've got the stretch,

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:16.880
further stretch target on
the A&amp;E four hours to 78%.

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:21.497
So we are just making it
really clear in the letter

00:38:21.497 --> 00:38:24.300
that that's what we are
committed to to doing.

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:28.140
In terms of the three areas for priority,

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:30.270
the first is to maintain the capacity

00:38:30.270 --> 00:38:32.250
that we put in in '23/'24.

00:38:32.250 --> 00:38:36.150
We know that made a huge
difference over the winter period.

00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:39.540
Not just in terms of
the general acute beds

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:42.720
but also the virtual wards,
chair that you mentioned earlier

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:45.360
the additional ambulance
hours on the road,

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:48.117
the money that went into
discharge in particular.

00:38:48.117 --> 00:38:50.310
So it's really important
that we maintain that

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:52.290
but we've got to make the best use

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:54.630
of all of our capacity as well.

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:56.310
We know that some of the ways of working

00:38:56.310 --> 00:38:58.770
the way that capacity is joined up,

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:00.540
the way that some of our processes work

00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:03.030
between our inside organisations

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:05.640
can allow us to use every
bit of that more effectively.

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:08.100
So things like a focus on length of stay

00:39:08.100 --> 00:39:10.997
is really important going into this year.

00:39:10.997 --> 00:39:12.690
And then the third priority area

00:39:12.690 --> 00:39:15.990
is about what more we can
do to really have a focus

00:39:15.990 --> 00:39:17.550
on patients in the community

00:39:17.550 --> 00:39:19.500
to avoid them being brought to hospital

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:20.850
and potentially being admitted

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:22.860
due to a lack of alternatives.

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:25.170
It's really obvious that
some of our patients

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:28.260
who stay in hospital for
long periods of time,

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:31.560
we could have put
alternatives in the community

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:33.360
before patients arrived.

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:35.490
So we share in today our evidence

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:37.440
about what works in each of those areas.

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:40.350
And we've got some fantastic
case studies around the country

00:39:40.350 --> 00:39:42.810
that we would encourage people to look at.

00:39:42.810 --> 00:39:44.550
Obviously we're not doing this on our own.

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:46.170
It's really important we are doing it

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:49.020
in conjunction with our
colleagues in local government

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:50.340
and there's quite a lot of sharing

00:39:50.340 --> 00:39:52.440
of best practise examples about

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:54.000
work between health and social care,

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:57.420
particularly around transfer
of care and discharge.

00:39:57.420 --> 00:40:01.320
It's not just a what
piece of correspondence,

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:03.810
it's also quite a lot in there around how.

00:40:03.810 --> 00:40:07.230
So we've learned a lot around
delivery support this year,

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:10.500
the impact of our improvement
offers and tiering,

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:13.440
and we're setting out today
what more we will do to help

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:17.400
and support systems taking
the best of what we know works

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:19.590
with our clinical teams on the ground.

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:22.470
We're also committing to
continue to measure those things

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:24.510
that matter, not necessarily the things

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:26.520
that we have always measured.

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:30.450
So things like our discharge delay data

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:32.370
really looking at that granularity now

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:33.780
of discharge ready date

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:36.810
and the time it takes for
people to leave hospital

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:38.730
once they're ready to leave.

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:40.980
Also recommitting again

00:40:40.980 --> 00:40:43.110
to the importance of the 12 hour standard

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:45.753
and the measurement and
publication of that data.

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:50.850
And then finally, we are
releasing much earlier this year

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:54.420
the details of our
capital incentive scheme.

00:40:54.420 --> 00:40:57.240
We know that organisations
are focused on this anyway,

00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:59.370
but I think we were able
to demonstrate last year

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:02.130
that we can use it to
corral our clinical teams

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:05.280
and our systems to think about
what further improvements

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:08.790
that they can make to in
turn get some capital support

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:10.680
that can help to reinvest in services.

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:12.960
So we've changed our
approach slightly this year

00:41:12.960 --> 00:41:14.400
in a couple of ways.

00:41:14.400 --> 00:41:16.650
The first of that is it's more expansive.

00:41:16.650 --> 00:41:19.020
So we've included category
two response times

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:21.450
and the 12 hours as part of that,

00:41:21.450 --> 00:41:22.620
which I think is important.

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:25.020
Listening to people's
feedback from last year,

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:27.300
we've also made it more inclusive

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:28.950
in that aspects of the incentive,

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:31.920
our system based rather than
individual provider base,

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:33.750
listening to what some of our colleagues

00:41:33.750 --> 00:41:35.130
are fed back particularly in community

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:36.600
and mental health services.

00:41:36.600 --> 00:41:38.730
And then finally the measurement period

00:41:38.730 --> 00:41:42.000
for part of the incentive is
over a longer period of time

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:44.100
what we are really
encouraging everyone to do

00:41:44.100 --> 00:41:47.850
and I know it's easy said and
it's really challenging to do,

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:49.830
but we need to make as much improvement

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:52.590
as we possibly can again over
the spring and summer period.

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:54.330
Now we know that winter's
a really difficult

00:41:54.330 --> 00:41:55.730
time to do that.

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:57.690
So it's really encouraging everybody

00:41:57.690 --> 00:42:01.830
to think about those steps and
key milestones along the way.

00:42:01.830 --> 00:42:04.020
So we've worked extensively
with stakeholders.

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:06.120
I'm really confident that there
won't be anything in there

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:07.530
that's a surprise.

00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:08.970
The only differences really

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:11.160
are in points of emphasis and focus,

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:13.170
but the plan was the plan last year.

00:42:13.170 --> 00:42:16.530
We demonstrated we could make
it work together collectively

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:18.570
last year and I'm confident
we can do that again

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:21.060
as we go into this year.

00:42:21.060 --> 00:42:22.701
Thanks Emily and thank you chair.

00:42:22.701 --> 00:42:24.480
- Emily thank you. Sarah-Jane, thank you.

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:26.400
As I said going to move on Julian

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:28.380
pick up on the financial
performance update

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:30.543
and then we'll pause and see

00:42:30.543 --> 00:42:32.117
if colleagues have any reactions

00:42:32.117 --> 00:42:33.322
to any of the reports so far.

00:42:33.322 --> 00:42:34.500
- Yeah, I really won't say much.

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:38.580
I think it's in the paper for '23/'24

00:42:38.580 --> 00:42:42.090
we will have underspent
the revenue NHSE mandate

00:42:42.090 --> 00:42:44.280
by 30 million pounds.

00:42:44.280 --> 00:42:46.203
So a fraction of a decimal point.

00:42:47.610 --> 00:42:50.100
I mean it shows both how
hard people have worked,

00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:52.380
this will be the second year

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:55.013
in which we have spent less in real terms

00:42:55.013 --> 00:42:57.720
on like for like basis

00:42:57.720 --> 00:42:59.310
whilst we have continued

00:42:59.310 --> 00:43:00.720
to actually deliver the improvements

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:03.573
that Emily has just talked through.

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:07.800
The only other thing
to raise is just a flag

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:10.020
is that on capital as well

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:12.963
for the money we deployed to the NHS,

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:16.380
we underspent by 4 million pounds

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:18.870
on a 7.3 billion pound budget.

00:43:18.870 --> 00:43:21.840
So when the money is given,
the NHS do really use it

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:23.700
to good effect.

00:43:23.700 --> 00:43:25.050
The one area we did underspend

00:43:25.050 --> 00:43:27.720
was about a hundred million
pounds on a ring fence budget

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:30.120
we are given by HM treasury,

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:33.360
which is used basically to
cover the cost of the change

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:36.150
to IFRS 16 and capitalising leases.

00:43:36.150 --> 00:43:38.250
And I'll just say that
is one of the areas where

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:40.743
actually both forecasting
is is really hard.

00:43:42.368 --> 00:43:47.368
So you know, I think the NHS
overall have done a good job

00:43:47.850 --> 00:43:50.043
of living within the money in aggregate.

00:43:51.120 --> 00:43:53.700
- Thank you. So should we
just pause for a moment

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:55.980
to see if any colleagues
who have any reactions

00:43:55.980 --> 00:43:59.760
to Julian, you gave us a
very good report actually

00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:01.860
this morning as well on the private board,

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:07.560
but any reactions on the
operational performance report

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:09.330
or update or Amanda's comments?

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:10.730
Andrew, your hand's gone up.

00:44:12.360 --> 00:44:14.550
- Thank you. So I've been on this board

00:44:14.550 --> 00:44:15.600
for a number of years,

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:17.550
and this month's report

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:22.410
is probably the best we've
seen in terms of the turnaround

00:44:22.410 --> 00:44:25.740
that you guys are
delivering in performance

00:44:25.740 --> 00:44:29.670
and you know, on cancer,
it's a remarkable story

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:32.430
considering where we were
a couple of years ago.

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:34.830
So, you know, well done
for all the efforts

00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:37.650
that you've put into this
'cause it is really fantastic

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:40.560
and it is making a big
difference to people's lives

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:43.650
and experience of the NHS.

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:47.820
So I'm heartened by what's happening here.

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:50.430
I suppose the big challenge going forward

00:44:50.430 --> 00:44:53.250
is around keeping up the momentum,

00:44:53.250 --> 00:44:57.810
and coming up with ideas and proposals

00:44:57.810 --> 00:45:00.900
that the whole kind of NHS will adopt.

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:04.770
So I've still got concerns
about 12 hours for example,

00:45:04.770 --> 00:45:08.820
and how we've managed
to manage the front door

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:11.730
much, much better with cat two performance

00:45:11.730 --> 00:45:13.170
is a reflection of that.

00:45:13.170 --> 00:45:15.720
But once you're in a hospital,

00:45:15.720 --> 00:45:18.270
can you be sorted relatively quickly

00:45:18.270 --> 00:45:21.060
and then move discharged
as quickly as possible.

00:45:21.060 --> 00:45:23.100
And I think that's the
next big challenge for us

00:45:23.100 --> 00:45:25.350
as we approach the coming winter.

00:45:25.350 --> 00:45:29.760
So I'm really heartened to
hear your plans going forward,

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.500
Sarah-Jane about, you know,

00:45:31.500 --> 00:45:33.720
how we can really address this,

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:36.900
but it's just trying to win
hearts and minds within systems.

00:45:36.900 --> 00:45:39.720
'Cause I think it does
require a system approach.

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:42.300
It can't be just down
to the acute provider

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:44.130
to discharge people

00:45:44.130 --> 00:45:48.750
and I'd be interested to
know at a future meeting

00:45:48.750 --> 00:45:51.930
all the thoughts that
systems have come back with

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:54.630
on how we can actually
get hold of that metric

00:45:54.630 --> 00:45:56.220
and really, really improve it.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:58.260
'Cause you know, you go back a decade,

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:00.450
chief executives used to get dismissed

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.090
on the basis of 12 hour performance

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:04.593
and you know,

00:46:04.593 --> 00:46:06.450
clearly we don't want to be
in that kind of territory.

00:46:06.450 --> 00:46:10.170
But you know, it is a
marker of experience,

00:46:10.170 --> 00:46:11.850
patient experience and
I think it's a really

00:46:11.850 --> 00:46:13.263
important thing to tackle.

00:46:14.374 --> 00:46:16.380
- Thank you Jane.
- Thank you Chair.

00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:18.810
And thank you to the
executives the report.

00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:22.380
Just wanted to note with interest,

00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:23.850
the very encouraging data

00:46:23.850 --> 00:46:26.250
in the operational performance report

00:46:26.250 --> 00:46:28.170
perhaps doesn't attract
quite so many headlines

00:46:28.170 --> 00:46:32.790
as some of the things
Emily mentioned in detail.

00:46:32.790 --> 00:46:35.040
But paragraphs 24 and five,

00:46:35.040 --> 00:46:37.950
the work around the diabetes
prevention programme

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:40.170
and the path to remission programme,

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:42.420
I thought the results
were hugely encouraging

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:45.990
and I just wondered if you wanted

00:46:45.990 --> 00:46:47.730
somebody wanted to comment
a bit further on those

00:46:47.730 --> 00:46:50.160
and also if plans to expand

00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:51.990
referrals into those programmes

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:55.800
were very much sort of on
the radar going forward.

00:46:55.800 --> 00:46:58.590
- Emily, do you want
to pick that one up or?

00:46:58.590 --> 00:47:00.030
- Shall I do one and then the other?

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:01.320
- Yeah, yeah, of course.

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:03.840
- I just wanted to pick
up on Andrew's points

00:47:03.840 --> 00:47:05.040
first of all, thank you.

00:47:05.970 --> 00:47:07.710
But I was going to reflect particularly

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:11.280
what you said about
cancer quickly in that,

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:13.470
I think all of the recovery programmes

00:47:13.470 --> 00:47:16.230
have demonstrated tremendous

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:18.270
working with the system in
order to get stuff done.

00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:21.300
And I wanted to call three
things out in particular,

00:47:21.300 --> 00:47:22.320
I think fits all of them,

00:47:22.320 --> 00:47:24.000
but particularly effecting
on the cancer one

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:25.080
we've focused on today.

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:26.850
The first is the combination of clinical

00:47:26.850 --> 00:47:29.190
and operational leadership together

00:47:29.190 --> 00:47:32.160
so that the policies, the
approaches, the solutions,

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:35.340
the working with the system
has very much recognised

00:47:35.340 --> 00:47:37.470
that we can't do one without the other.

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:39.450
And so the leadership
of the cancer programme

00:47:39.450 --> 00:47:41.820
of Ed Rose on the operational
side and Kelly Palmer

00:47:41.820 --> 00:47:44.730
and Peter Johnson as the
national clinical director means

00:47:44.730 --> 00:47:46.800
that everything is has been
done from that perspective.

00:47:46.800 --> 00:47:49.600
And the same in the UEC programme,
in the elective programme

00:47:50.730 --> 00:47:52.470
and in primary care as well.

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:56.940
The second is about doing
things with not to the system.

00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:59.190
And I think all of the
programmes have been absolutely

00:47:59.190 --> 00:48:01.950
rigorous at even the
way the tiering system

00:48:01.950 --> 00:48:04.710
could have worked, which
would've been sort of directive

00:48:04.710 --> 00:48:06.120
NHS England tells you
you've got to do this.

00:48:06.120 --> 00:48:08.130
That is not how it's been experienced

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:10.200
in terms of the feedback that I've heard.

00:48:10.200 --> 00:48:12.960
Recognising that we can look
across all of the systems

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:15.720
and say, you know, we
think this is the approach

00:48:15.720 --> 00:48:17.610
that demonstrably is more effective,

00:48:17.610 --> 00:48:19.500
but what that looks like
when it's implemented

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:21.720
in any individual system
will look somewhat different.

00:48:21.720 --> 00:48:24.210
And systems need to have
that ability to influence

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:26.010
and own those solutions.

00:48:26.010 --> 00:48:28.170
And the third is making the
investment where it's needed.

00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:30.180
There are places where we
just didn't have the capacity,

00:48:30.180 --> 00:48:32.370
we didn't have the space,
we didn't have the staff

00:48:32.370 --> 00:48:33.300
in order to deliver.

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:36.900
And the combination of
those things in concert

00:48:36.900 --> 00:48:38.430
using the creative problem solving

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:39.600
and the improvement techniques

00:48:39.600 --> 00:48:40.890
that we're going to talk about later,

00:48:40.890 --> 00:48:42.900
I think has been really impressive.

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:44.608
And obviously partly want to mention it,

00:48:44.608 --> 00:48:45.728
as I said in the private board

00:48:45.728 --> 00:48:48.300
in that it's Ed Rose's
last day was yesterday

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:50.670
and we've all benefited from
the three years he spent

00:48:50.670 --> 00:48:52.980
leading the cancer programme
and want to recognise that

00:48:52.980 --> 00:48:54.930
and say good luck and please come back.

00:48:56.640 --> 00:48:57.473
Not that we don't have lots of

00:48:57.473 --> 00:48:58.306
other brilliant people as well,

00:48:58.306 --> 00:48:59.993
but Ed has been particularly brilliant.

00:49:00.870 --> 00:49:05.490
On the diabetes point, so
thank you for calling that out.

00:49:05.490 --> 00:49:09.010
And I think one of the things
that we spoke about earlier

00:49:10.020 --> 00:49:13.390
is the how important the
evaluation of this has been

00:49:14.280 --> 00:49:17.190
and what we found looking across

00:49:17.190 --> 00:49:20.010
all of our initiatives is that
we are better in evaluation

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:22.110
in some places than others

00:49:22.110 --> 00:49:26.610
because we want to make sure
what we're doing is robust

00:49:26.610 --> 00:49:27.720
but we are much better off

00:49:27.720 --> 00:49:29.760
when we can look at particularly
something like diabetes

00:49:29.760 --> 00:49:31.740
where we need to look at
long-term health outcomes,

00:49:31.740 --> 00:49:34.440
having designed the evaluation upfront,

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:36.300
embedded it in the way
the program's working

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:37.230
so that we can come back and say,

00:49:37.230 --> 00:49:39.120
no, this is much better for patients

00:49:39.120 --> 00:49:41.793
and genuinely good value
for money at the same time.

00:49:42.660 --> 00:49:46.590
So we are, you know, excited
at the opportunity to extend

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:50.220
that now that the evaluation
has come back so positively,

00:49:50.220 --> 00:49:52.813
you know, the statistic
in paragraph 25 that

00:49:52.813 --> 00:49:57.270
in around 50% of cases,

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:59.940
the type two diabetes was in remission

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:03.093
at the end of the experience
is as you say very exciting.

00:50:04.050 --> 00:50:06.180
And it speaks to the conversations

00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:07.590
we've been having internally on the

00:50:07.590 --> 00:50:09.360
medium term strategy more broadly

00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:11.160
about how do we create the right balance

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:13.620
between fixing things we
can see right in front of us

00:50:13.620 --> 00:50:14.790
of operationally

00:50:14.790 --> 00:50:17.280
and the need to make sure
we're building in, you know,

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:20.583
strategic in investment in
prevention in particular.

00:50:22.200 --> 00:50:24.190
- Thank you. Any other? Mark.

00:50:26.403 --> 00:50:28.140
- It's an extraordinary
financial tour to force

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:32.940
to come in on a 1 billion
budget with 30 million under.

00:50:32.940 --> 00:50:35.100
But of course as you point out Julian,

00:50:35.100 --> 00:50:37.050
it has been achieved to some extent

00:50:37.050 --> 00:50:39.060
with an overspend in the systems

00:50:39.060 --> 00:50:41.730
and an underspend of almost a
billion on the transformation

00:50:41.730 --> 00:50:44.190
and reserves.

00:50:44.190 --> 00:50:45.690
And some of that transformation

00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:48.990
is the digital
infrastructure of the system.

00:50:48.990 --> 00:50:53.790
And so the question is how
long one can go on avoiding,

00:50:53.790 --> 00:50:57.480
as it were per force the
necessary transformation.

00:50:57.480 --> 00:50:59.190
Although I accept that an enormous amount

00:50:59.190 --> 00:51:00.750
of what has been described

00:51:00.750 --> 00:51:03.510
in terms of improvement
forms is transformation.

00:51:03.510 --> 00:51:05.460
But I mean there is some
physical transformation

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:08.343
that probably is a problem.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:10.103
- Julian, do you want...

00:51:10.103 --> 00:51:12.160
- I would just say that

00:51:13.170 --> 00:51:14.520
I mean all sorts of
decisions have been made

00:51:14.520 --> 00:51:16.470
around prioritisation as
we've gone through the year,

00:51:16.470 --> 00:51:18.750
but last year we would've
spent pretty much

00:51:18.750 --> 00:51:21.690
over 2 billion pounds in aggregate

00:51:21.690 --> 00:51:23.910
on the kind of technology
transformation programme,

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:25.440
which is actually a significant increase

00:51:25.440 --> 00:51:26.490
on the previous year.

00:51:26.490 --> 00:51:29.430
So notwithstanding all the pressures

00:51:29.430 --> 00:51:32.220
even within this, we
continue to make progress

00:51:32.220 --> 00:51:36.630
on the things we need
to do for the long term

00:51:36.630 --> 00:51:39.480
on, you know, technology enabled change,

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:42.780
the long term workforce plan,

00:51:42.780 --> 00:51:47.340
actually the diabetes prevention
programme and other areas.

00:51:47.340 --> 00:51:50.640
Of course at times we would
like to go further faster,

00:51:50.640 --> 00:51:53.610
but you know, we have continued

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:56.370
to make progress on our long-term goals.

00:51:56.370 --> 00:51:58.620
- Yeah, I mean I do think the,

00:52:00.450 --> 00:52:02.130
I always go back to the NHS app

00:52:02.130 --> 00:52:03.840
and I go back to actually
like in last year

00:52:03.840 --> 00:52:07.704
we awarded the FDP, which is very good.

00:52:07.704 --> 00:52:08.537
And now we've got the agreement

00:52:08.537 --> 00:52:11.310
with the support of the
chancellor and the department

00:52:11.310 --> 00:52:13.140
and the prime minister clearly for this,

00:52:13.140 --> 00:52:16.080
the 3.4 billion technology budget.

00:52:16.080 --> 00:52:18.150
It doesn't come till '25, '26,

00:52:18.150 --> 00:52:20.400
but actually the detailed
work that's going on,

00:52:20.400 --> 00:52:22.920
Julian with you and Vin
and others around actually,

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:26.010
how do we deploy that across our systems

00:52:26.010 --> 00:52:28.650
to give us a better chance of
investing behind technology

00:52:28.650 --> 00:52:31.324
to drive the productivity uplift.

00:52:31.324 --> 00:52:36.090
But that was a really good
thing to have received,

00:52:36.090 --> 00:52:37.950
a good amount of funding to have received.

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:39.450
Looking forward to that
further transformation.

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:40.800
The point you make is absolutely right,

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:42.930
we need to keep investing and
perhaps investing even more

00:52:42.930 --> 00:52:46.083
but keep investing behind
our digital estate.

00:52:47.310 --> 00:52:48.810
Mary did you want to?

00:52:48.810 --> 00:52:49.643
- Thank you.

00:52:49.643 --> 00:52:54.643
Mine was just to congratulate
the element of spend

00:52:54.990 --> 00:52:58.050
on the community diagnostic centres

00:52:58.050 --> 00:53:02.290
and in particular, the eradication of

00:53:03.600 --> 00:53:06.390
9% of mental health dormitories

00:53:06.390 --> 00:53:10.950
into better environments for patients,

00:53:10.950 --> 00:53:15.300
which I also think will enhance retention

00:53:15.300 --> 00:53:20.130
in mental health because it
will make life much easier.

00:53:20.130 --> 00:53:23.820
So I think it's easy to miss
a relatively small point

00:53:23.820 --> 00:53:27.300
that really is improving care.

00:53:27.300 --> 00:53:29.790
- Thank you. So Vin
and then Robert I think

00:53:29.790 --> 00:53:32.700
and then I might need to
move us on given time. Vin.

00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:34.259
- Thanks chair.

00:53:34.259 --> 00:53:36.600
I just wanted to highlight I
think something to certainly

00:53:36.600 --> 00:53:38.100
as someone who's got experience

00:53:38.100 --> 00:53:40.890
as a consultant in the NHS for 30 years,

00:53:40.890 --> 00:53:42.960
something that genuinely galvanises staff

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:44.490
is the opportunity to really help

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:47.160
those who experience
the greatest inequality

00:53:47.160 --> 00:53:48.090
in our communities.

00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:49.950
And so something that you
don't always bring out

00:53:49.950 --> 00:53:52.950
in the performance reports,
for example is in cancer,

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:55.140
our targeted lung health checks programmes

00:53:55.140 --> 00:53:58.680
had a disproportionate impact
on those who experienced that.

00:53:58.680 --> 00:54:00.350
The National Diabetes
Prevention programme,

00:54:00.350 --> 00:54:03.810
we know that diabetes
particularly affect people like me

00:54:03.810 --> 00:54:05.160
from South Asian backgrounds

00:54:05.160 --> 00:54:06.990
and those experiencing inequality.

00:54:06.990 --> 00:54:08.940
We've got evidence showing that helps.

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:10.620
And then to pick up Mary's point,

00:54:10.620 --> 00:54:12.090
the community diagnostic hubs

00:54:12.090 --> 00:54:14.310
were specifically planned to be placed in

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:16.830
areas which had the poorest outcomes

00:54:16.830 --> 00:54:18.150
and therefore the best access.

00:54:18.150 --> 00:54:20.828
And I just think it's
important for us to note that

00:54:20.828 --> 00:54:23.580
and also note the way
in which that genuinely

00:54:23.580 --> 00:54:26.850
incentivizes staff, I
think and galvanises them.

00:54:26.850 --> 00:54:28.030
- Thank you. Robert.

00:54:29.190 --> 00:54:32.250
- So just to reinforce the congratulations

00:54:32.250 --> 00:54:36.060
that have been given for
remarkable performance and year,

00:54:36.060 --> 00:54:38.200
just picking up on the diabetes point

00:54:39.480 --> 00:54:41.760
that was highlighted by Jane,

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:44.280
and related also to cancer,

00:54:44.280 --> 00:54:46.960
obesity we're all aware is a huge issue

00:54:48.060 --> 00:54:50.670
and is a contributory factor
for diabetes of course,

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:52.710
and a risk factor for cancer.

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:54.120
And we agreed this morning

00:54:54.120 --> 00:54:58.260
that we'd have a fresh look
at our diabetes strategy

00:54:58.260 --> 00:55:01.410
in light of these drugs that have emerged

00:55:01.410 --> 00:55:02.640
that control appetite,

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:05.760
but also have more immediate benefits

00:55:05.760 --> 00:55:08.640
in terms of cardiovascular
risk and insulin resistance.

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:11.910
So I think that'll be
an important discussion.

00:55:11.910 --> 00:55:16.080
- Yeah, very good. Reflects a
very good conversation we had

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:18.000
in the private board.

00:55:18.000 --> 00:55:23.000
So let's draw that sort of
operational performance reporting

00:55:24.540 --> 00:55:26.580
and financial reporting to a close.

00:55:26.580 --> 00:55:28.110
As I do that, I just want to

00:55:28.110 --> 00:55:30.780
reiterate something I said at the start.

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:35.700
And we recognise we
face lots of challenges

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:39.300
and we know we can do and
we're focused on doing better

00:55:39.300 --> 00:55:42.990
and improving performance,

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:45.990
but we also recognise the
intense demand pressure

00:55:45.990 --> 00:55:48.060
that we're seeing and
whether you pick it out

00:55:48.060 --> 00:55:52.080
in mental health, or in
other areas actually,

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:56.100
but we see particular lift
in mental health presentation

00:55:56.100 --> 00:55:57.813
in the last year or so.

00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:02.250
It's also reflecting on Julian's comments

00:56:02.250 --> 00:56:04.260
and again at the private board as well

00:56:04.260 --> 00:56:06.770
as just very brief
comments he's made today

00:56:06.770 --> 00:56:08.460
that I think we are deploying resources

00:56:08.460 --> 00:56:10.893
Julian led by you with
really careful thought.

00:56:12.600 --> 00:56:13.847
And the engagement,

00:56:13.847 --> 00:56:18.270
so the feedback I've had
from the systems working,

00:56:18.270 --> 00:56:20.040
so Amanda, and Julian and Emily,

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:21.540
and others engage with the systems

00:56:21.540 --> 00:56:24.150
around this year's planning

00:56:24.150 --> 00:56:26.250
actually is showing a
really good collaboration

00:56:26.250 --> 00:56:27.450
across the systems actually.

00:56:27.450 --> 00:56:29.880
So the ICBs working
well with the providers.

00:56:29.880 --> 00:56:31.620
Actually, they're working together

00:56:31.620 --> 00:56:33.570
and they're wrestling with the problem of

00:56:33.570 --> 00:56:35.400
how do we manage these resources

00:56:35.400 --> 00:56:40.400
with a very low increase 0.2%
I think increase in resources

00:56:41.970 --> 00:56:43.230
and yet much higher demand

00:56:43.230 --> 00:56:46.110
and they're working really
well together in general

00:56:46.110 --> 00:56:48.180
collaborating on a solution.

00:56:48.180 --> 00:56:52.080
And as I said earlier,
thanks to the huge efforts

00:56:52.080 --> 00:56:55.080
and hard work of staff across the NHS,

00:56:55.080 --> 00:56:57.393
we are providing record
levels of healthcare.

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:00.540
And we touched on it I think

00:57:00.540 --> 00:57:01.980
at the last public board meeting.

00:57:01.980 --> 00:57:02.813
I'm sure we did.

00:57:02.813 --> 00:57:06.330
And it was just one example of the lift

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:09.780
in the provision of healthcare.

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:11.610
So primary care

00:57:11.610 --> 00:57:14.460
and our GP, our general practitioners

00:57:14.460 --> 00:57:18.600
and our primary care, other health staff,

00:57:18.600 --> 00:57:20.610
whether they're physios or practise nurses

00:57:20.610 --> 00:57:22.320
or district nurses or so on.

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:27.320
And to think that they're
doing 23% more appointments

00:57:27.960 --> 00:57:30.750
every year than they
were doing in the year

00:57:30.750 --> 00:57:32.624
prior to the pandemic.

00:57:32.624 --> 00:57:33.780
And more than one in 10 of the population

00:57:33.780 --> 00:57:37.260
has an appointment in England every week.

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:38.820
More than one in 10 of the population

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:41.610
has an appointment in
primary care every week.

00:57:41.610 --> 00:57:44.610
And so actually it's quite
a remarkable performance.

00:57:44.610 --> 00:57:47.040
And they're an example
actually of what we see

00:57:47.040 --> 00:57:50.070
across everywhere else in the system.

00:57:50.070 --> 00:57:53.160
Whether it's in secondary
trusts, or across ICBs,

00:57:53.160 --> 00:57:56.040
or other areas of provision.

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:58.680
So let's move on. Yes.

00:57:58.680 --> 00:58:00.900
- Sorry Richard, I know
you've just done a summary,

00:58:00.900 --> 00:58:03.330
but you might just...
- What if you correct me

00:58:03.330 --> 00:58:05.610
- No, no, no, it's not that at all.

00:58:05.610 --> 00:58:07.680
I bet. Just before we do leave this item,

00:58:07.680 --> 00:58:09.120
'cause I did want to thank colleagues

00:58:09.120 --> 00:58:10.736
for what you have done,

00:58:10.736 --> 00:58:12.780
which is recognised that the
year that we've just finished

00:58:12.780 --> 00:58:17.780
has been a year of actually
real and meaningful improvement.

00:58:18.390 --> 00:58:22.140
But also just to say that
of course that does follow

00:58:22.140 --> 00:58:25.740
what was the most massive
disruption from Covid.

00:58:25.740 --> 00:58:29.400
So this is a recovery
journey that we are on

00:58:29.400 --> 00:58:32.790
and we have a lot further to go.

00:58:32.790 --> 00:58:34.470
And I think your point
Richard about demand

00:58:34.470 --> 00:58:36.900
is really important 'cause
we have an ageing population

00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:38.970
and we have an increasingly
sick population

00:58:38.970 --> 00:58:41.730
and the demands on the NHS are
only going in one direction

00:58:41.730 --> 00:58:43.140
as a consequence.

00:58:43.140 --> 00:58:44.730
So this is really important.

00:58:44.730 --> 00:58:48.750
It's really important
that the NHS can show that

00:58:48.750 --> 00:58:51.180
despite all of those demand side pressures

00:58:51.180 --> 00:58:53.580
and as Julian has said
within a financial context,

00:58:53.580 --> 00:58:56.070
we have made real and
meaningful improvement,

00:58:56.070 --> 00:58:58.282
which we can now build on.

00:58:58.282 --> 00:59:00.270
But the thing I did want to just say is,

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:04.650
it is because our colleagues
across the NHS have worked

00:59:04.650 --> 00:59:07.740
unbelievably hard over the past year

00:59:07.740 --> 00:59:12.740
and against that backdrop,
it feels pretty relentless.

00:59:13.590 --> 00:59:16.680
So I think it's just to say
for all of the acknowledgement

00:59:16.680 --> 00:59:18.030
that's happened in this meeting,

00:59:18.030 --> 00:59:20.400
actually the acknowledgement
has to be for our colleagues

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:22.437
because they're the ones
that really do the work

00:59:22.437 --> 00:59:24.630
and they do it because every day

00:59:24.630 --> 00:59:26.250
they get up and do
their best for patients.

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:29.040
And that is one thing about
the NHS that hasn't changed.

00:59:29.040 --> 00:59:30.863
- Thank you for saying that. Very good.

00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:34.770
So we're going to to item,
I think it's number six

00:59:34.770 --> 00:59:39.000
on my agenda, which is NHS productivity.

00:59:39.000 --> 00:59:40.920
Julian, I think you are leading in on this

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:42.300
I think, aren't you?

00:59:42.300 --> 00:59:45.210
- Well I think what Amanda
said is almost a perfect segue

00:59:45.210 --> 00:59:47.373
into actually this item.

00:59:48.360 --> 00:59:52.680
There's been lots of
discussion on NHS productivity

00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:57.333
over recent months and the last year.

00:59:58.710 --> 01:00:03.710
As Amanda has just said in the context is

01:00:04.740 --> 01:00:08.610
over the 10 and 15 years
before the pandemic,

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:13.610
the NHS significantly
increased productivity,

01:00:13.710 --> 01:00:16.410
indeed keeping pace
with the wider economy,

01:00:16.410 --> 01:00:18.280
if not slightly outpacing it

01:00:19.170 --> 01:00:23.160
very much bucking the kind
of wider public sector trend.

01:00:23.160 --> 01:00:28.160
But Covid was clearly a
massive shock to the system,

01:00:28.470 --> 01:00:31.170
hugely disruptive.

01:00:31.170 --> 01:00:36.150
And in the ONS published data,
you can see in the year 2021,

01:00:36.150 --> 01:00:39.840
I mean a significant dip in productivity,

01:00:39.840 --> 01:00:42.480
both reflecting increased in costs

01:00:42.480 --> 01:00:45.303
and the disruption to broader services.

01:00:46.380 --> 01:00:47.910
The ONS published data,

01:00:47.910 --> 01:00:50.400
which is the sort of most reliable source

01:00:50.400 --> 01:00:53.160
I think on sort of
aggregate NHS productivity

01:00:53.160 --> 01:00:56.910
shows a sharp bounce back in '21/'22,

01:00:56.910 --> 01:01:00.960
such that aggregate NHS productivity

01:01:00.960 --> 01:01:05.960
came from being about
20% below to 6% below

01:01:06.060 --> 01:01:09.090
where we were before the pandemic.

01:01:09.090 --> 01:01:11.340
Now that data's two years old,

01:01:11.340 --> 01:01:13.540
we would anticipate there has been further

01:01:15.330 --> 01:01:17.460
recovery at the sort
of aggregate NHS level,

01:01:17.460 --> 01:01:19.950
but we wait for, you know,

01:01:19.950 --> 01:01:24.543
what the ONS data says on '22/'23.

01:01:27.510 --> 01:01:30.470
So, you know, that is sort
of what you would expect

01:01:30.470 --> 01:01:33.000
on a recovery journey,

01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:35.400
which we are definitely on.

01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:38.100
Nonetheless, there's been
a real focus on, you know,

01:01:38.100 --> 01:01:43.100
in particular what has gone
in on in the acute sector.

01:01:43.140 --> 01:01:46.740
Now, understanding sort
of in more real time

01:01:46.740 --> 01:01:49.620
what's happened on productivity's hard.

01:01:49.620 --> 01:01:53.010
It's hard because as with
almost any statistical data set

01:01:53.010 --> 01:01:56.040
time brings more data,

01:01:56.040 --> 01:01:59.670
and actually in particular
it's hard to track in real time

01:01:59.670 --> 01:02:01.350
some of the things that
are actually going on

01:02:01.350 --> 01:02:04.530
on quality and outcomes.

01:02:04.530 --> 01:02:06.870
It is harder in real time

01:02:06.870 --> 01:02:08.940
just because of where our data is at

01:02:08.940 --> 01:02:12.780
to separate out if you
are an integrated acute

01:02:12.780 --> 01:02:15.690
and community trust, what's,
you know, the sort of

01:02:15.690 --> 01:02:17.700
what has happened on community services

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:20.010
versus acute services.

01:02:20.010 --> 01:02:22.800
And as Steve would remind me,

01:02:22.800 --> 01:02:24.930
sometimes it's also hard in real time

01:02:24.930 --> 01:02:27.610
to track what is going on
on sort of research income

01:02:28.569 --> 01:02:30.180
and cost and how that is

01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:32.523
sort of playing into the total picture.

01:02:33.450 --> 01:02:38.450
Nonetheless, we have done
work to try and understand,

01:02:41.385 --> 01:02:45.130
what is happening in the acute sector

01:02:46.110 --> 01:02:49.860
and at least to find out
in broad terms where we are

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:54.540
and where our focus of effort
needs to be as we can both,

01:02:54.540 --> 01:02:58.260
you know, help support
providers and systems

01:02:58.260 --> 01:03:01.290
to continue on that journey of recovery

01:03:01.290 --> 01:03:03.453
that Amanda has just referred to.

01:03:04.860 --> 01:03:09.860
Our own data suggests that if we allow for

01:03:13.320 --> 01:03:18.320
things that often aren't
immediately counted in activity,

01:03:19.290 --> 01:03:21.750
including where we have
really changed pathways

01:03:21.750 --> 01:03:25.410
over the last few years and our
accounting and coding hasn't

01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:27.240
yet properly caught up.

01:03:27.240 --> 01:03:32.240
Things like same day
emergency care, virtual wards,

01:03:33.930 --> 01:03:36.630
you know, a whole series
of things of that ilk.

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:38.340
And if we properly take account of things

01:03:38.340 --> 01:03:39.990
like changes in critical care

01:03:39.990 --> 01:03:42.810
and actually what has
happened in maternity

01:03:42.810 --> 01:03:44.940
where clearly we have put more investment

01:03:44.940 --> 01:03:49.503
in order to improve
quality and patient safety.

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:52.950
And if we properly allow for

01:03:52.950 --> 01:03:57.950
what is genuinely an increase
in sort of acuity i.e.,

01:03:58.099 --> 01:04:03.099
the patients we are dealing
with are basically sicker

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:09.570
and frailer then you know at first blush

01:04:09.570 --> 01:04:13.560
it looks like we have
a sort of a 10 to 11%

01:04:13.560 --> 01:04:17.520
drop in productivity in the acute sector.

01:04:17.520 --> 01:04:22.350
Now again in 2023, '24 you
also have to allow for the fact

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:24.060
we've had industrial action

01:04:24.060 --> 01:04:26.430
and industrial action
has cost us more money

01:04:26.430 --> 01:04:29.880
and cost us time and lost us activity

01:04:29.880 --> 01:04:33.630
and our estimate of
that impact is about 3%

01:04:33.630 --> 01:04:36.780
in the year 2023, '24.

01:04:36.780 --> 01:04:40.830
So you know, that drops
it again to a position

01:04:40.830 --> 01:04:43.500
where it looks like you've
had a drop in productivity

01:04:43.500 --> 01:04:45.750
of about 7%.

01:04:45.750 --> 01:04:49.620
Now again, within that, we
can see we've spent about

01:04:49.620 --> 01:04:51.330
a percentage point

01:04:51.330 --> 01:04:55.050
relatively speaking
more on high cost drugs.

01:04:55.050 --> 01:04:57.300
Now those give us better outcomes.

01:04:57.300 --> 01:04:59.280
Again that's not captured

01:04:59.280 --> 01:05:02.640
in the sort of immediate short term data.

01:05:02.640 --> 01:05:06.750
And we know that as we are
recapitalizing the NHS,

01:05:06.750 --> 01:05:11.750
the investment in technology,
community diagnostic centres,

01:05:12.600 --> 01:05:15.870
elective capacity in any given year

01:05:15.870 --> 01:05:17.910
there's like a depreciation cost

01:05:17.910 --> 01:05:20.400
which you don't really start to recover

01:05:20.400 --> 01:05:21.840
until the year after,

01:05:21.840 --> 01:05:24.270
or indeed the two or
three years after that.

01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:26.120
And again, that probably accounts for

01:05:26.972 --> 01:05:29.580
around a percentage point
of that productivity here

01:05:29.580 --> 01:05:34.580
that still leaves a gap and
when we analyse that gap,

01:05:34.740 --> 01:05:37.140
even allowing for case mix, which means,

01:05:37.140 --> 01:05:39.990
we end up with patients
in hospital for longer

01:05:39.990 --> 01:05:42.330
because they just need
more care and treatment

01:05:42.330 --> 01:05:46.567
and time for reablement about half of that

01:05:46.567 --> 01:05:49.050
residual gap is probably

01:05:49.050 --> 01:05:52.083
still an increase in length of stay.

01:05:53.190 --> 01:05:57.480
We know again that we have more work to do

01:05:57.480 --> 01:06:00.630
and Sarah-Jane just did described

01:06:00.630 --> 01:06:04.470
the things we as an NHS can still do

01:06:04.470 --> 01:06:09.360
to improve that position and
recover from the disruption

01:06:09.360 --> 01:06:11.610
we experienced in Covid.

01:06:11.610 --> 01:06:14.400
But we know also that some of that

01:06:14.400 --> 01:06:18.630
is driven by the pressures
that local authorities

01:06:18.630 --> 01:06:20.610
and social care has been under.

01:06:20.610 --> 01:06:23.400
I mean in 2022, we did
see a significant dip

01:06:23.400 --> 01:06:25.950
in domiciliary care capacity.

01:06:25.950 --> 01:06:28.140
I mean local authorities and government

01:06:28.140 --> 01:06:30.450
have done a lot to recover that

01:06:30.450 --> 01:06:33.480
position over the last 12 and 18 months.

01:06:33.480 --> 01:06:35.940
Nonetheless, I think
we are still recovering

01:06:35.940 --> 01:06:38.760
from that disruption and we can see

01:06:38.760 --> 01:06:41.980
the pressures those services
are under going into

01:06:43.140 --> 01:06:44.850
this year coming.

01:06:44.850 --> 01:06:47.010
And we know in mental health for example,

01:06:47.010 --> 01:06:49.980
we are seeing mental health services

01:06:49.980 --> 01:06:53.040
who are spending NHS meant
mental health services

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:56.460
who are spending millions of pounds a year

01:06:56.460 --> 01:06:59.310
dealing with the homelessness issue

01:06:59.310 --> 01:07:03.600
and just getting homeless
people out of our hospital beds.

01:07:03.600 --> 01:07:05.310
So there's a series of factors

01:07:05.310 --> 01:07:08.880
but some of which remain in the NHS's gift

01:07:08.880 --> 01:07:09.960
and as Sarah Jane has said,

01:07:09.960 --> 01:07:12.690
that is a real focus for improvement

01:07:12.690 --> 01:07:14.640
over this coming 12 months.

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:18.210
That still leaves a
gap that we cannot sort

01:07:18.210 --> 01:07:20.340
of analytically explain in the data

01:07:20.340 --> 01:07:23.670
that is probably worth
two to 3% of the gap.

01:07:23.670 --> 01:07:27.633
Now factors that will
influence that, are definitely,

01:07:28.560 --> 01:07:32.610
you know, there is strong at
least qualitative evidence

01:07:32.610 --> 01:07:36.120
of a reduction in staff
discretionary efforts.

01:07:36.120 --> 01:07:39.120
Now, that is not that surprising

01:07:39.120 --> 01:07:41.020
if you look at the level of sort of

01:07:42.660 --> 01:07:47.400
industrial unrest over the
last year and you know,

01:07:47.400 --> 01:07:49.560
we can point to examples

01:07:49.560 --> 01:07:54.420
where people have changed their
working patterns post Covid.

01:07:54.420 --> 01:07:59.420
We know that, you know,
as the data says we have

01:07:59.520 --> 01:08:03.840
significantly increased staffing
over the last four years.

01:08:03.840 --> 01:08:05.700
By the way, I think that's a good thing.

01:08:05.700 --> 01:08:09.210
Four years ago we were really
worried about staffing levels

01:08:09.210 --> 01:08:13.050
where you know, where were
these staff going to come from,

01:08:13.050 --> 01:08:15.510
but nonetheless, that
significantly increases

01:08:15.510 --> 01:08:19.800
the ratio of newer staff
to experienced staff

01:08:19.800 --> 01:08:21.330
and we have definitely seen turnover

01:08:21.330 --> 01:08:23.700
of some of our most experienced staff

01:08:23.700 --> 01:08:26.220
as we came out of the pandemic

01:08:26.220 --> 01:08:28.890
and undoubtedly that just takes time

01:08:28.890 --> 01:08:31.600
for people to sort of
get up the learning curve

01:08:32.550 --> 01:08:34.380
and as people get up the learning curve

01:08:34.380 --> 01:08:38.670
productivity improves.

01:08:38.670 --> 01:08:41.580
And you know, in that
context is great news

01:08:41.580 --> 01:08:44.850
that we've seen a significant improvement

01:08:44.850 --> 01:08:45.840
in the retention rate,

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:48.030
and we have seen an
improvement in staff engagement

01:08:48.030 --> 01:08:49.020
over the last year.

01:08:49.020 --> 01:08:51.810
And again we know
improved staff engagement

01:08:51.810 --> 01:08:55.620
is correlated with improved productivity.

01:08:55.620 --> 01:08:58.740
So it shows there's
definitely still work to do

01:08:58.740 --> 01:09:00.570
and I don't think this is just, you know,

01:09:00.570 --> 01:09:03.930
about us working harder, it's
just about how we continue

01:09:03.930 --> 01:09:06.660
to work in some cases smarter

01:09:06.660 --> 01:09:08.730
and where we can identify the waste

01:09:08.730 --> 01:09:10.620
and where we can sort of
take away the obstacles

01:09:10.620 --> 01:09:12.600
and barriers to enabling people

01:09:12.600 --> 01:09:16.497
to do the best job that they can.

01:09:16.497 --> 01:09:18.970
And we see we are making progress

01:09:20.610 --> 01:09:25.610
for example, high cost agency staffing

01:09:27.330 --> 01:09:30.090
in '22/'23 we spent three
and a half billion pounds,

01:09:30.090 --> 01:09:34.410
in '23/'24 we will spend
closer to 3 billion pounds.

01:09:34.410 --> 01:09:37.080
So as substantive staffing has increased,

01:09:37.080 --> 01:09:39.420
people are realising they can take out

01:09:39.420 --> 01:09:41.700
the temporary staffing.

01:09:41.700 --> 01:09:44.490
We know we are more productive
when we have a higher ratio

01:09:44.490 --> 01:09:47.370
of substantive to temporary staffing.

01:09:47.370 --> 01:09:52.370
And I think we will see that
continue strongly into '24/'25.

01:09:53.520 --> 01:09:56.520
We know we have improved day case rates,

01:09:56.520 --> 01:10:00.300
theatre productivity, you know,

01:10:00.300 --> 01:10:05.300
much higher use of dealing
with patients in non-admitted

01:10:06.360 --> 01:10:08.850
rather than admitted settings.

01:10:08.850 --> 01:10:13.530
Length of stay in '23/'24
improved by about 3%.

01:10:13.530 --> 01:10:17.520
So that shows, you know, we
are on that improvement journey

01:10:17.520 --> 01:10:20.343
that I described.

01:10:22.350 --> 01:10:26.280
But again, work still to do.

01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:30.540
So, you know, our plan is very much

01:10:30.540 --> 01:10:34.750
a focus on how do we support our leaders,

01:10:35.700 --> 01:10:39.330
managers, and staff to
have the improvement skills

01:10:39.330 --> 01:10:43.119
that they need that they can, you know,

01:10:43.119 --> 01:10:43.952
seeing what needs to be done,

01:10:43.952 --> 01:10:46.980
have the tools to deliver
change on the ground.

01:10:46.980 --> 01:10:51.600
And this year we will put new
investment behind NHS impact

01:10:51.600 --> 01:10:55.173
and the improvement regime,

01:10:56.085 --> 01:10:59.430
and some of this is how we
relearn the lessons of things

01:10:59.430 --> 01:11:03.453
that were done well in the early 2000s.

01:11:04.943 --> 01:11:06.270
And I think very much over the next year,

01:11:06.270 --> 01:11:08.970
year and a half, it is
focused on how do we do

01:11:08.970 --> 01:11:10.290
operational improvement

01:11:10.290 --> 01:11:13.890
and then clearly we're already
investing in technology

01:11:13.890 --> 01:11:16.530
in enable change and
we'll set out more details

01:11:16.530 --> 01:11:19.740
of that kind of longer term programme

01:11:19.740 --> 01:11:23.010
to look at how we can sort of double

01:11:23.010 --> 01:11:25.830
the annual rate of
productivity improvement

01:11:25.830 --> 01:11:28.777
over the next five and six years

01:11:28.777 --> 01:11:30.690
and we will look to do that in the summer.

01:11:30.690 --> 01:11:33.120
So I think, you know, that
is our best understanding

01:11:33.120 --> 01:11:37.440
of where we are, more work to do

01:11:37.440 --> 01:11:41.190
and no doubt, we are incredibly
grateful for the support

01:11:41.190 --> 01:11:45.180
the government has given us,

01:11:45.180 --> 01:11:48.750
and the investment for the future.

01:11:48.750 --> 01:11:53.020
And we will also continue
to talk with them

01:11:54.120 --> 01:11:56.490
about how we continue to
find indeed the investment

01:11:56.490 --> 01:11:58.470
we will also need to put into the estate.

01:11:58.470 --> 01:12:01.710
Because definitely one of the
things that still hinders us

01:12:01.710 --> 01:12:03.660
is, I mean, you know, the kind of growth

01:12:03.660 --> 01:12:06.210
and critical backlog maintenance,

01:12:06.210 --> 01:12:10.200
and the fact that every day
we will be losing a theatre,

01:12:10.200 --> 01:12:13.650
a ward block, a pathology department

01:12:13.650 --> 01:12:15.390
and if we can sort that out,

01:12:15.390 --> 01:12:18.360
again that will just take
away one of the obstacles

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:23.250
to enabling our staff to do
the best job that they can.

01:12:23.250 --> 01:12:25.680
- Thank you. So I'm going to
open up just for comments,

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:27.230
questions Mark, I know you
want to ask the question

01:12:27.230 --> 01:12:29.640
as Steve, there's going to
be a whole flood actually

01:12:29.640 --> 01:12:30.900
so we'll we'll probably batch

01:12:30.900 --> 01:12:32.700
them Julian if that's all right.

01:12:32.700 --> 01:12:34.680
So Mark and then Steve first.

01:12:34.680 --> 01:12:36.828
- So thank you very much indeed.

01:12:36.828 --> 01:12:38.490
I mean it's incredibly helpful analysis

01:12:38.490 --> 01:12:40.672
and I think one of the
challenges of the ONS figure

01:12:40.672 --> 01:12:41.970
is that it's, you know, one number

01:12:41.970 --> 01:12:44.370
for an incredibly complex organisation,

01:12:44.370 --> 01:12:46.740
you know roughly 10%
of the British economy.

01:12:46.740 --> 01:12:48.750
And so the note it needs

01:12:48.750 --> 01:12:50.973
is about sort of three
pages to explain it.

01:12:52.170 --> 01:12:53.610
But I mean the other striking figure

01:12:53.610 --> 01:12:56.760
and you alluded to it in
a way was just comparing

01:12:56.760 --> 01:12:57.938
in one of the the ONS papers,

01:12:57.938 --> 01:13:00.480
you referenced the rate of
public service productivity

01:13:00.480 --> 01:13:02.130
growth by service area.

01:13:02.130 --> 01:13:04.830
One of our challenges is that
if you look at both adult

01:13:04.830 --> 01:13:07.560
and children's social
care, their productivity

01:13:07.560 --> 01:13:12.330
is well below where it was
in 1997, and that's a problem

01:13:12.330 --> 01:13:16.110
because it inevitably has a
consequences for us on length

01:13:16.110 --> 01:13:18.870
of stay that are quite
difficult to deal with.

01:13:18.870 --> 01:13:22.560
- So Julian, can you
stall these in your memory

01:13:22.560 --> 01:13:23.880
for response then Steve?

01:13:23.880 --> 01:13:25.459
- Yes, thanks Julian.

01:13:25.459 --> 01:13:27.750
So one comment and two related questions.

01:13:27.750 --> 01:13:29.133
So the comment is,

01:13:30.510 --> 01:13:32.460
although I am absolutely sure

01:13:32.460 --> 01:13:34.557
that clinicians understand the benefit

01:13:34.557 --> 01:13:37.320
and the need to deliver
the highest possible

01:13:37.320 --> 01:13:39.900
quality of care at the
lowest possible cost,

01:13:39.900 --> 01:13:43.830
the term productivity isn't
one that they often embrace.

01:13:43.830 --> 01:13:45.480
And that's why I think
it's really important

01:13:45.480 --> 01:13:49.050
as noted in paragraph
17 that this is framed

01:13:49.050 --> 01:13:51.930
in terms of programmes of
continuous improvement,

01:13:51.930 --> 01:13:54.810
value-based healthcare is
something that is familiar

01:13:54.810 --> 01:13:57.900
to many clinicians and
embraces productivity.

01:13:57.900 --> 01:14:00.940
And so the more we can do this work within

01:14:02.190 --> 01:14:03.540
the scope of NHS Impact,

01:14:03.540 --> 01:14:06.840
which is based on the concept
of continuous improvement,

01:14:06.840 --> 01:14:10.110
the better, and of equally
the getting it first

01:14:10.110 --> 01:14:12.660
getting it right first time
programme, GIRFT, which again,

01:14:12.660 --> 01:14:15.840
I know we all know from visits around

01:14:15.840 --> 01:14:18.180
the system is embraced by many clinicians.

01:14:18.180 --> 01:14:20.883
So I think that framing
is really important.

01:14:21.992 --> 01:14:23.070
And then the two questions
which are related,

01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:27.840
so clearly ONS do make quality adjustments

01:14:27.840 --> 01:14:30.060
in terms of their measurements,

01:14:30.060 --> 01:14:32.670
but do you think that that is sufficient

01:14:32.670 --> 01:14:35.700
to recognise high quality care?

01:14:35.700 --> 01:14:38.640
So quality of care, not
just quantity of care.

01:14:38.640 --> 01:14:42.030
And secondly, as again noted in the paper,

01:14:42.030 --> 01:14:43.950
we all want to do more on prevention

01:14:43.950 --> 01:14:46.650
and of course prevention
is a resource expended

01:14:46.650 --> 01:14:48.780
to lead to new activity hopefully,

01:14:48.780 --> 01:14:50.610
or a reduction in activity.

01:14:50.610 --> 01:14:52.350
And have you had discussions,

01:14:52.350 --> 01:14:54.720
is there a chance of
having discussions with ONS

01:14:54.720 --> 01:14:56.253
about how that is captured?

01:14:57.180 --> 01:15:00.480
- Should we try one more
question for your batch?

01:15:00.480 --> 01:15:01.740
So vin.

01:15:01.740 --> 01:15:03.780
- Yeah so it's a comment
rather than a question chair.

01:15:03.780 --> 01:15:05.820
Thank you. So first I think
the technology investment's

01:15:05.820 --> 01:15:08.646
genuinely really exciting and we can be

01:15:08.646 --> 01:15:11.940
and will be really ambitious
about what we can achieve

01:15:11.940 --> 01:15:13.800
with that investment.

01:15:13.800 --> 01:15:15.330
The second bit which is I think the link

01:15:15.330 --> 01:15:17.550
to productivity reflects on the comments

01:15:17.550 --> 01:15:19.380
that Steve's just made.

01:15:19.380 --> 01:15:21.240
So I used to work for Sarah-Jane

01:15:21.240 --> 01:15:23.366
in Birmingham Children's Hospital.

01:15:23.366 --> 01:15:24.870
I used to stamp round the wards

01:15:24.870 --> 01:15:27.087
saying that if any of the computers worked

01:15:27.087 --> 01:15:28.710
I could be at least 10% more productive.

01:15:28.710 --> 01:15:30.900
And actually the investment
in the core infrastructure

01:15:30.900 --> 01:15:32.400
and technology is really important

01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:33.900
and Birmingham Children's is actually

01:15:33.900 --> 01:15:36.780
implementing an electronic
patient record now

01:15:36.780 --> 01:15:38.310
and some of our early evidence,

01:15:38.310 --> 01:15:40.590
'cause we have digitised now 90% of trust

01:15:40.590 --> 01:15:43.170
so we've not been standing
still over the last few years

01:15:43.170 --> 01:15:45.660
is that there's a five to 10% reduction

01:15:45.660 --> 01:15:47.220
in non-elective length of stay

01:15:47.220 --> 01:15:49.530
if you've put an electronic
patient record in.

01:15:49.530 --> 01:15:51.360
So you can get, and
there's an evidence base

01:15:51.360 --> 01:15:52.830
that there'll be measurable benefits

01:15:52.830 --> 01:15:54.630
that will be better for patients.

01:15:54.630 --> 01:15:56.670
And similarly for the app,

01:15:56.670 --> 01:15:59.400
being able to do your repeat
prescription on the app

01:15:59.400 --> 01:16:02.730
doesn't just save GPs time
which is much better for them,

01:16:02.730 --> 01:16:05.340
but it also saves each patient 18 minutes

01:16:05.340 --> 01:16:07.170
in being able to order
a repeat prescription

01:16:07.170 --> 01:16:10.530
so you get a better experience
for patients as well.

01:16:10.530 --> 01:16:12.480
And just the final bit I
think on the technology,

01:16:12.480 --> 01:16:14.970
which again relates to Steve's point,

01:16:14.970 --> 01:16:17.670
is that I think we know
from years of experience now

01:16:17.670 --> 01:16:20.040
that where technology
investment goes wrong

01:16:20.040 --> 01:16:21.968
is where you don't engage

01:16:21.968 --> 01:16:23.730
and work with your frontline staff

01:16:23.730 --> 01:16:25.200
putting in the technology.

01:16:25.200 --> 01:16:27.930
So in the next few weeks,
in the next few months,

01:16:27.930 --> 01:16:30.840
we will be engaging in a
heavy process of engagement

01:16:30.840 --> 01:16:32.700
with staff.

01:16:32.700 --> 01:16:35.040
We won't just be doing
that through round tables

01:16:35.040 --> 01:16:36.630
and visiting trusts.

01:16:36.630 --> 01:16:39.540
We are also engaging with industries

01:16:39.540 --> 01:16:41.880
so we're getting industry's take on it.

01:16:41.880 --> 01:16:43.770
And then finally we are agreeing

01:16:43.770 --> 01:16:47.460
with government the composition
of an advisory panel

01:16:47.460 --> 01:16:49.500
which will include not
just industry experts

01:16:49.500 --> 01:16:52.440
who've led tech transformation
in their own industries,

01:16:52.440 --> 01:16:55.979
but also frontline NHS staff

01:16:55.979 --> 01:16:58.080
who've done tech transformation
to we get that right balance

01:16:58.080 --> 01:16:59.910
between challenge from outside

01:16:59.910 --> 01:17:02.070
but also challenge from
within the organisation.

01:17:02.070 --> 01:17:04.320
So I must say with the tech
transformation I'm feeling

01:17:04.320 --> 01:17:06.600
really positive about
what we can achieve once

01:17:06.600 --> 01:17:08.800
that money starts flowing
through from 2025.

01:17:09.990 --> 01:17:13.734
- So Julian do you want to
either react to any of those

01:17:13.734 --> 01:17:15.990
and then answer I'm going to
have to move us on quite soon.

01:17:16.825 --> 01:17:21.090
- I'll try and be quick and
just pick up on a few things.

01:17:21.090 --> 01:17:22.470
So firstly on local authorities,

01:17:22.470 --> 01:17:25.380
I mean I can't speak in detail
about like the productivity

01:17:25.380 --> 01:17:27.330
in the social care sector.

01:17:27.330 --> 01:17:29.640
What I would say is
we've learned quite a lot

01:17:29.640 --> 01:17:32.220
about how we and local
authorities can work effectively

01:17:32.220 --> 01:17:34.980
take together over the
last year 18 months.

01:17:34.980 --> 01:17:36.840
And I think actually
we've learned a lot about

01:17:36.840 --> 01:17:38.760
what we in the NHS can do,

01:17:38.760 --> 01:17:43.560
even in the way we are sort
of helping assess patients

01:17:43.560 --> 01:17:47.122
and getting sort of a common understanding

01:17:47.122 --> 01:17:49.160
with local authorities about
what is the level of care

01:17:49.160 --> 01:17:52.780
that's actually needed
so that we aren't almost

01:17:53.981 --> 01:17:58.110
sort of over prescribing care
and if we can get it right,

01:17:58.110 --> 01:18:01.170
we can really help them and
they can really help us.

01:18:01.170 --> 01:18:04.560
And clearly we in

01:18:04.560 --> 01:18:06.630
actually aspects of mental
health learning disability

01:18:06.630 --> 01:18:09.150
are seeing exactly the
same demand pressures

01:18:09.150 --> 01:18:10.560
that they are seeing,

01:18:10.560 --> 01:18:13.710
which is actually a quite
significant increase in demand

01:18:13.710 --> 01:18:15.780
and complexity of cases.

01:18:15.780 --> 01:18:18.150
So as I said, I can't speak in detail

01:18:18.150 --> 01:18:19.950
but I think they are seeing pressures

01:18:19.950 --> 01:18:21.540
that we are also experiencing

01:18:21.540 --> 01:18:23.100
and we are learning a lot

01:18:23.100 --> 01:18:27.003
actually about how we can
help and support each other.

01:18:27.870 --> 01:18:30.510
The second thing just on the ONS,

01:18:30.510 --> 01:18:32.643
I mean actually May is here,

01:18:34.110 --> 01:18:35.730
who is leading this work for us

01:18:35.730 --> 01:18:39.520
and we are in active
discussions with the ONS

01:18:40.920 --> 01:18:43.800
on how we are measuring productivity.

01:18:43.800 --> 01:18:45.600
Now there's always the challenge,

01:18:45.600 --> 01:18:48.120
can we improve the the data
around quality and outcomes?

01:18:48.120 --> 01:18:50.360
And no doubt that will form part of it

01:18:50.360 --> 01:18:52.500
but one of the things we're
actively discussing with them

01:18:52.500 --> 01:18:55.410
is how do you take account of

01:18:55.410 --> 01:18:58.380
actions which prevent activity.

01:18:58.380 --> 01:18:59.880
Now I suspect what we'll have to do

01:18:59.880 --> 01:19:03.037
is work out how you count that activity

01:19:03.037 --> 01:19:04.260
and how you put a value on it basically,

01:19:04.260 --> 01:19:07.113
which is the value of what you've avoided.

01:19:08.160 --> 01:19:10.080
Now that is not easy to do,

01:19:10.080 --> 01:19:12.933
it'll be very hard to do in
real time I guarantee it.

01:19:13.890 --> 01:19:15.990
But we are an active discussion with them

01:19:15.990 --> 01:19:18.240
because clearly that does form part of

01:19:18.240 --> 01:19:20.313
actually how we double NHS productivity

01:19:20.313 --> 01:19:21.963
over the next five and six years.

01:19:23.310 --> 01:19:25.860
- Thank you. Is the
board okay if I move on,

01:19:25.860 --> 01:19:28.848
I'm just conscious of time.

01:19:28.848 --> 01:19:32.310
Julian, thank you for very
cogent actually presentation

01:19:32.310 --> 01:19:35.550
of productivity as we measure it.

01:19:35.550 --> 01:19:37.890
And actually the various
initiatives we have

01:19:37.890 --> 01:19:39.973
that actually we know we'll be confident

01:19:39.973 --> 01:19:42.093
that we continue to to improve.

01:19:43.230 --> 01:19:45.090
So let's go to item seven.

01:19:45.090 --> 01:19:47.607
So it's an update on the first
year of the the maternity

01:19:47.607 --> 01:19:49.830
and neonatal three plan.

01:19:49.830 --> 01:19:51.775
Ruth, are you going to lead in?

01:19:51.775 --> 01:19:53.040
- Yes I am but we'll
be briefer than normal

01:19:53.040 --> 01:19:54.540
to allow more conversation

01:19:54.540 --> 01:19:56.267
- [Richard] Please that
would be very helpful.

01:19:56.267 --> 01:19:59.550
- But as you said in your opening remarks

01:19:59.550 --> 01:20:04.020
and Amanda did as well,
we have all seen and heard

01:20:04.020 --> 01:20:06.990
and read the tragic
stories of the birth trauma

01:20:06.990 --> 01:20:08.670
shared by women and families

01:20:08.670 --> 01:20:13.670
as part of the APPG group
on birth trauma this week.

01:20:14.610 --> 01:20:16.650
The testimonies of those women

01:20:16.650 --> 01:20:19.830
who have experienced
further the traumatic birth

01:20:19.830 --> 01:20:23.550
and makes extremely difficult
reading for everyone.

01:20:23.550 --> 01:20:25.860
And for me it further reinforces

01:20:25.860 --> 01:20:28.260
the importance of listening to women

01:20:28.260 --> 01:20:31.950
and taking the appropriate
action in response.

01:20:31.950 --> 01:20:33.960
Improving maternity and neonatal care

01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:37.560
is one of the highest
priorities for us as an NHS,

01:20:37.560 --> 01:20:40.110
and despite the improvements
that's been made

01:20:40.110 --> 01:20:43.680
in maternity and neonatal
services over the last decade,

01:20:43.680 --> 01:20:46.470
we know that there's much more to do.

01:20:46.470 --> 01:20:49.440
Which is why we did develop
the three year delivery plan

01:20:49.440 --> 01:20:51.840
from maternity and neonatal services.

01:20:51.840 --> 01:20:54.420
And this does bring
together the recommendations

01:20:54.420 --> 01:20:59.340
from Donna Ockenden's reports
into Shrewsbury and Telford

01:20:59.340 --> 01:21:02.640
and Bill Kirkup into East Kent

01:21:02.640 --> 01:21:06.060
so that we do drive up
maternity and neonatal services.

01:21:06.060 --> 01:21:08.100
They're safer, they're more personable

01:21:08.100 --> 01:21:12.090
and of course more equitable
for women, babies and families.

01:21:12.090 --> 01:21:14.880
Now that delivery plan
included requirements

01:21:14.880 --> 01:21:17.160
for all levels within the NHS

01:21:17.160 --> 01:21:20.700
as well as there being a
national responsibilities.

01:21:20.700 --> 01:21:24.660
It's very clear about the
responsibility for every board

01:21:24.660 --> 01:21:27.960
and ICB to identify the
areas of improvement

01:21:27.960 --> 01:21:31.680
and take the action to improve
maternity and neonatal care.

01:21:31.680 --> 01:21:35.130
And I know around this
board we are also committed

01:21:35.130 --> 01:21:37.263
to ensuring that is achieved as well.

01:21:38.370 --> 01:21:40.500
So this week, as Amanda said,

01:21:40.500 --> 01:21:42.750
we too have welcomed the opportunity

01:21:42.750 --> 01:21:46.140
to work with the governments
to develop the cross government

01:21:46.140 --> 01:21:48.851
strategy to maximise the impact

01:21:48.851 --> 01:21:50.010
of the three year delivery plan

01:21:50.010 --> 01:21:52.290
and to address the wider determination

01:21:52.290 --> 01:21:54.963
determinants of health and inequalities.

01:21:56.100 --> 01:21:58.053
As I mentioned before, and,

01:21:59.315 --> 01:22:01.050
indeed you both did too in your remarks.

01:22:01.050 --> 01:22:03.060
Listening and responding to all women

01:22:03.060 --> 01:22:05.430
and families is an essential part

01:22:05.430 --> 01:22:07.980
of ensuring safe and high quality care.

01:22:07.980 --> 01:22:10.620
It improves the safety and the experience

01:22:10.620 --> 01:22:13.860
of those using maternity neonatal services

01:22:13.860 --> 01:22:16.980
and it addresses health inequalities.

01:22:16.980 --> 01:22:18.480
It's also vitally important

01:22:18.480 --> 01:22:22.350
to embed the service user
voice into the services.

01:22:22.350 --> 01:22:25.320
And that includes at a leadership level

01:22:25.320 --> 01:22:28.830
you will now hear from a very
important member of our team,

01:22:28.830 --> 01:22:32.550
Kelly, who is our neonatal
service user voice

01:22:32.550 --> 01:22:35.190
representatives and is an integral member

01:22:35.190 --> 01:22:37.350
of our team at all levels.

01:22:37.350 --> 01:22:39.060
Kelly, over to you.

01:22:39.060 --> 01:22:41.250
- Thank you Ruth. Good afternoon all.

01:22:41.250 --> 01:22:44.790
I'd like to thank you for the
opportunity to join today.

01:22:44.790 --> 01:22:46.980
So as Ruth said, I'm Kelly Phizacklea

01:22:46.980 --> 01:22:49.980
and I'm a neonatal service
user voice representative

01:22:49.980 --> 01:22:52.920
within the maternity
and neonatal programme.

01:22:52.920 --> 01:22:54.420
You may be more familiar with the term

01:22:54.420 --> 01:22:56.373
patient and public voice member.

01:22:57.510 --> 01:22:59.190
So my daughter was diagnosed

01:22:59.190 --> 01:23:02.340
with multiple congenital
anomalies, at our 12 week scan,

01:23:02.340 --> 01:23:04.650
which resulted in a complex pregnancy

01:23:04.650 --> 01:23:06.480
and a three and a half month stay

01:23:06.480 --> 01:23:08.253
in neonatal intensive care.

01:23:09.150 --> 01:23:11.970
So for us, although this
experience was traumatic,

01:23:11.970 --> 01:23:15.150
it was made as positive as
possible due to the competent

01:23:15.150 --> 01:23:18.330
and compassionate
multidisciplinary workforce

01:23:18.330 --> 01:23:21.570
and a dedication to
providing personalised care,

01:23:21.570 --> 01:23:25.560
not solely for our daughter
but for us as a family.

01:23:25.560 --> 01:23:27.120
Following her admission though

01:23:27.120 --> 01:23:29.910
I recognise the inequity of care provided

01:23:29.910 --> 01:23:34.710
and access to specialist
expertise across an ICB footprint,

01:23:34.710 --> 01:23:37.740
let alone regionally or nationally.

01:23:37.740 --> 01:23:41.070
Hence my desire to be
involved in improving outcomes

01:23:41.070 --> 01:23:45.093
and experiences for women
birthing people and families.

01:23:46.470 --> 01:23:47.940
Alongside me in my role,

01:23:47.940 --> 01:23:50.190
they're a group of strategic service user

01:23:50.190 --> 01:23:51.960
voice representatives,

01:23:51.960 --> 01:23:54.270
and we all have lived
experience of maternity

01:23:54.270 --> 01:23:58.620
and neonatal services,
both positive and negative.

01:23:58.620 --> 01:24:01.050
We are interwoven throughout
the whole maternity

01:24:01.050 --> 01:24:02.400
and neonatal programme

01:24:02.400 --> 01:24:06.360
and recognised as key
stakeholders at committee, board

01:24:06.360 --> 01:24:08.043
and leadership group level.

01:24:09.090 --> 01:24:11.220
We do recognise how advanced the embedding

01:24:11.220 --> 01:24:13.830
of service user voice
is within the maternity

01:24:13.830 --> 01:24:17.190
and neonatal programme
compared to some others.

01:24:17.190 --> 01:24:20.700
However, we must also acknowledge
the lack of role security

01:24:20.700 --> 01:24:24.123
and tenure we experience as PPV members.

01:24:26.040 --> 01:24:28.920
As we have said today, there
have been various inquiries,

01:24:28.920 --> 01:24:32.220
reports and recommendations
relating to maternity

01:24:32.220 --> 01:24:34.140
and neonatal services

01:24:34.140 --> 01:24:36.870
with the latest publication
being just this week

01:24:36.870 --> 01:24:41.640
on birth trauma and other
inquiries are yet to conclude.

01:24:41.640 --> 01:24:43.350
And sadly as we've said,

01:24:43.350 --> 01:24:46.560
a common theme is threaded
throughout all of these

01:24:46.560 --> 01:24:50.790
and an emerging duplication
of recommendation continues

01:24:50.790 --> 01:24:52.893
to listen to service users.

01:24:53.940 --> 01:24:55.950
So there are two essential
elements relating

01:24:55.950 --> 01:24:58.950
to service users and listening.

01:24:58.950 --> 01:25:01.620
Firstly, the importance
of listening to women,

01:25:01.620 --> 01:25:04.620
birthing people and their
families at the point of care

01:25:04.620 --> 01:25:07.980
to improve their outcomes and experiences.

01:25:07.980 --> 01:25:11.160
We cannot fulfil our
vision of providing safer,

01:25:11.160 --> 01:25:14.310
more equitable and more
personalised care for all

01:25:14.310 --> 01:25:15.663
if we do not listen.

01:25:16.500 --> 01:25:19.350
And remember, that what
we hear are not stories,

01:25:19.350 --> 01:25:21.630
these are people's lived experience

01:25:21.630 --> 01:25:24.990
and this will impact them
for the rest of their life.

01:25:24.990 --> 01:25:27.960
Plus, we know it isn't
enough to just listen,

01:25:27.960 --> 01:25:30.813
our actions must relate
to what we have heard.

01:25:32.700 --> 01:25:36.690
The second essential element
is how it's vital for maternity

01:25:36.690 --> 01:25:40.020
and neonatal service user
voice to be integrated

01:25:40.020 --> 01:25:44.280
within quality, safety,
governance and assurance processes

01:25:44.280 --> 01:25:46.680
and for service improvements
to be co-produced

01:25:46.680 --> 01:25:49.800
with those who have lived experience.

01:25:49.800 --> 01:25:50.850
So at a local level,

01:25:50.850 --> 01:25:53.910
we have maternity and
neonatal voice partnerships

01:25:53.910 --> 01:25:56.430
known as MNVPs,

01:25:56.430 --> 01:25:59.550
and they are the mechanism
for ensuring this is achieved.

01:25:59.550 --> 01:26:02.190
However, variation in resourcing

01:26:02.190 --> 01:26:05.130
and embedding of MNVPs remains.

01:26:05.130 --> 01:26:07.800
And that is why the
spring budget allocation

01:26:07.800 --> 01:26:11.583
for MNVPs will be a welcomed
additional funding stream.

01:26:12.420 --> 01:26:15.240
This funding will be utilised
to strengthen the work

01:26:15.240 --> 01:26:17.790
of the MNVP at a local level

01:26:17.790 --> 01:26:20.640
and particularly in
relation to the integration

01:26:20.640 --> 01:26:22.863
of neonatal service user voice.

01:26:24.390 --> 01:26:25.560
We know that maternity

01:26:25.560 --> 01:26:28.500
and neonatal services
are inextricably linked

01:26:28.500 --> 01:26:32.520
and neonatal families view
their journey as a continuum.

01:26:32.520 --> 01:26:35.880
They do not differentiate
between the two services.

01:26:35.880 --> 01:26:37.650
They are not aware that these services

01:26:37.650 --> 01:26:39.210
are commissioned differently,

01:26:39.210 --> 01:26:41.700
and for these families
their neonatal journey

01:26:41.700 --> 01:26:45.510
does not start or end
on the neonatal unit.

01:26:45.510 --> 01:26:47.430
Hence the need for us to ensure

01:26:47.430 --> 01:26:50.460
that feedback collection
mechanisms are equipped

01:26:50.460 --> 01:26:54.363
to support maternity and
neonatal service user voice.

01:26:55.380 --> 01:26:57.060
We must also acknowledge the need

01:26:57.060 --> 01:27:00.600
to support collaborative
work in across MNVPs

01:27:00.600 --> 01:27:03.060
and neonatal parent advisory groups

01:27:03.060 --> 01:27:06.630
and also VCSE organisations.

01:27:06.630 --> 01:27:09.840
And this is to ensure that
neonatal voices are represented

01:27:09.840 --> 01:27:13.140
at both a local and network level.

01:27:13.140 --> 01:27:15.870
Working in partnership will
support the continuation

01:27:15.870 --> 01:27:17.430
to influence decisions.

01:27:17.430 --> 01:27:19.710
Once neonatal commissioning arrangements

01:27:19.710 --> 01:27:22.620
have been delegated to ICBs,

01:27:22.620 --> 01:27:24.990
the additional funding
from the spring budget

01:27:24.990 --> 01:27:28.320
will enable MNVPs to
have dedicated capacity

01:27:28.320 --> 01:27:32.760
to focus on improving neonatal
outcomes and experiences

01:27:32.760 --> 01:27:35.043
for these vulnerable service users.

01:27:36.480 --> 01:27:39.360
I just want to end by
thanking all the service users

01:27:39.360 --> 01:27:41.700
who share their experiences

01:27:41.700 --> 01:27:44.370
and also the service user
voice representatives

01:27:44.370 --> 01:27:47.100
who work tirelessly at a local, regional

01:27:47.100 --> 01:27:50.670
and national level to
progress the implementation

01:27:50.670 --> 01:27:53.580
of embedding service user voice.

01:27:53.580 --> 01:27:56.430
I would also like to thank
those who work within the system

01:27:56.430 --> 01:27:59.220
that really see the
value of involving those

01:27:59.220 --> 01:28:01.050
with lived experience

01:28:01.050 --> 01:28:05.283
and ensure that it is ingrained
within their daily practise.

01:28:06.570 --> 01:28:09.630
We must remember that one avoidable death,

01:28:09.630 --> 01:28:13.350
one case of harm, one
experience of birth trauma

01:28:13.350 --> 01:28:16.110
and one parent being unable
to stay with their baby

01:28:16.110 --> 01:28:20.700
in neonatal care, these
are all one too many.

01:28:20.700 --> 01:28:24.360
And unfortunately funding to
increase workforce numbers

01:28:24.360 --> 01:28:26.820
is only part of the solution.

01:28:26.820 --> 01:28:30.090
We need the infrastructure
to foster a competent,

01:28:30.090 --> 01:28:32.760
confident, compassionate,
and well supported

01:28:32.760 --> 01:28:35.640
multidisciplinary workforce.

01:28:35.640 --> 01:28:37.170
But an even bigger requirement

01:28:37.170 --> 01:28:39.720
is the need for a change in culture,

01:28:39.720 --> 01:28:42.600
and a vital element to
support that transition

01:28:42.600 --> 01:28:44.490
is the need to listen to

01:28:44.490 --> 01:28:48.300
and work collaboratively
with service users.

01:28:48.300 --> 01:28:50.130
Because I really feel that together

01:28:50.130 --> 01:28:53.370
we can strive towards
safer, more equitable

01:28:53.370 --> 01:28:55.555
and more personalised care for all.

01:28:55.555 --> 01:28:57.120
Thank you.

01:28:57.120 --> 01:28:58.170
- Thank you Kelly.

01:28:58.170 --> 01:29:02.040
And just to pick up a few
other points I may chair?

01:29:02.040 --> 01:29:03.673
- I want you to be really...

01:29:03.673 --> 01:29:04.506
I mean it's a very important topic

01:29:04.506 --> 01:29:06.660
but I do want to give the board the chance

01:29:06.660 --> 01:29:08.094
to engage with it.

01:29:08.094 --> 01:29:10.500
So if you present to us for
the whole of the the time.

01:29:10.500 --> 01:29:13.440
- I'll just highlight
a few couple of areas.

01:29:13.440 --> 01:29:15.587
- That'd be great.
- Thank Kelly for all the work

01:29:15.587 --> 01:29:18.120
that she does does with us.

01:29:18.120 --> 01:29:19.350
I did just want to just pick out,

01:29:19.350 --> 01:29:21.960
you take the board as the paper as read,

01:29:21.960 --> 01:29:24.750
but just a couple of areas
to particularly highlight

01:29:24.750 --> 01:29:27.870
and actually feeds in some
of the other conversations

01:29:27.870 --> 01:29:29.010
that have been had this afternoon.

01:29:29.010 --> 01:29:31.260
But there is a growing
issue facing our maternity

01:29:31.260 --> 01:29:33.660
and neonatal services,

01:29:33.660 --> 01:29:36.300
which are factors which
were in influencing

01:29:36.300 --> 01:29:37.860
the complexity of care.

01:29:37.860 --> 01:29:39.840
So preexisting conditions,

01:29:39.840 --> 01:29:44.004
changes in maternal
BMI, age improving care

01:29:44.004 --> 01:29:45.810
for very premature babies is leading

01:29:45.810 --> 01:29:48.240
to much more complex care needs.

01:29:48.240 --> 01:29:51.330
And so we are working to
understand what that means

01:29:51.330 --> 01:29:54.000
for the future implications
for care pathways,

01:29:54.000 --> 01:29:57.000
clinical policy, staffing,
estates requirements.

01:29:57.000 --> 01:29:58.470
And that will continue to be part

01:29:58.470 --> 01:30:02.010
of our ongoing strategy discussions.

01:30:02.010 --> 01:30:04.110
Just a couple of
improvements to highlight.

01:30:04.110 --> 01:30:05.340
I think it's worth just noting

01:30:05.340 --> 01:30:07.180
that we have seen some improvement

01:30:08.310 --> 01:30:11.190
in our CQC survey over the last year.

01:30:11.190 --> 01:30:15.990
So 25 and a half thousand women
who use maternity services

01:30:15.990 --> 01:30:18.570
showed that there was
often a better experience

01:30:18.570 --> 01:30:21.270
in maternity care than the year before.

01:30:21.270 --> 01:30:24.150
However, we know, and as other
colleagues have rightly said,

01:30:24.150 --> 01:30:27.780
we've got a lot more to do
to improve that experience.

01:30:27.780 --> 01:30:30.330
We're making good progress on workforce

01:30:30.330 --> 01:30:32.790
and particularly you can see from the data

01:30:32.790 --> 01:30:35.580
we are making good progress in
reducing the gaps in staffing

01:30:35.580 --> 01:30:37.385
and particularly you'll see

01:30:37.385 --> 01:30:38.940
we've got a record number of midwives,

01:30:38.940 --> 01:30:41.220
and we've improved our
retention rates as well.

01:30:41.220 --> 01:30:42.240
And it's really important

01:30:42.240 --> 01:30:45.630
that we continue the work
on retention particularly

01:30:45.630 --> 01:30:46.500
given the fact that

01:30:46.500 --> 01:30:50.070
we've got a high level of public scrutiny

01:30:50.070 --> 01:30:51.900
around maternity and neonatal services

01:30:51.900 --> 01:30:54.102
and we need to under make sure

01:30:54.102 --> 01:30:58.140
that doesn't impact those
attracting those into

01:30:58.140 --> 01:31:01.203
work in our workforce and those
that already working there.

01:31:02.190 --> 01:31:05.590
But also just to say that we've also

01:31:07.200 --> 01:31:09.900
got work going on to tackle inequalities

01:31:09.900 --> 01:31:11.850
and I think it's really important
to just reflect the fact

01:31:11.850 --> 01:31:13.890
that we've got a
significant amount of work

01:31:13.890 --> 01:31:16.860
going on to address inequalities as well.

01:31:16.860 --> 01:31:18.990
And culture, as Kelly rightly said,

01:31:18.990 --> 01:31:20.310
culture is a really important part.

01:31:20.310 --> 01:31:23.550
We're making really good
progress on the leadership

01:31:23.550 --> 01:31:24.900
and culture programme

01:31:24.900 --> 01:31:29.370
and all of our 150 units are going through

01:31:29.370 --> 01:31:32.804
that programme currently and

01:31:32.804 --> 01:31:36.870
they'll all have gone through
that by January next year.

01:31:36.870 --> 01:31:38.910
Just to also highlight the fact that

01:31:38.910 --> 01:31:41.400
these aren't the APPG
isn't the only report

01:31:41.400 --> 01:31:43.110
that we will have over the next 18 months.

01:31:43.110 --> 01:31:45.210
You'll know that the impact in maternity

01:31:45.210 --> 01:31:47.190
and neonatal, the Thirlwall inquiries

01:31:47.190 --> 01:31:49.980
commenced it's preliminary hearings today

01:31:49.980 --> 01:31:51.780
and the Ockenden review of maternity

01:31:51.780 --> 01:31:55.530
and neonatal care at Nottingham
Universities Hospitals

01:31:55.530 --> 01:31:57.090
is ongoing.

01:31:57.090 --> 01:31:59.080
And I do want to just draw out the fact

01:32:00.186 --> 01:32:01.650
that we are working differently with Donna

01:32:01.650 --> 01:32:04.440
through the review of
Nottingham. I chair a group,

01:32:04.440 --> 01:32:06.750
a bimonthly group with
the trust executives,

01:32:06.750 --> 01:32:09.750
Donna, members of Emily's team

01:32:09.750 --> 01:32:11.909
in terms of improvement directors

01:32:11.909 --> 01:32:15.420
and others really to in real time

01:32:15.420 --> 01:32:17.790
understand the emerging
themes from the reviews.

01:32:17.790 --> 01:32:18.840
And I think that's really important.

01:32:18.840 --> 01:32:21.690
So it enables the trust
ahead of the final report

01:32:21.690 --> 01:32:24.720
to make improvement and
also for us nationally

01:32:24.720 --> 01:32:27.630
to get the learning out
and to disseminate that

01:32:27.630 --> 01:32:29.580
and work with partners where we need to,

01:32:29.580 --> 01:32:31.980
to make those improvements as well.

01:32:31.980 --> 01:32:34.080
So I'll stop there, but I
will just leave the board

01:32:34.080 --> 01:32:36.760
with one stat 'cause I think

01:32:38.130 --> 01:32:42.030
during this board meeting
this afternoon 156 babies

01:32:42.030 --> 01:32:45.360
will have been welcomed
into the world by the NHS,

01:32:45.360 --> 01:32:49.080
and I think that a stat
alone really signifies

01:32:49.080 --> 01:32:51.630
why the work that we are
doing is so important

01:32:51.630 --> 01:32:53.820
to make sure that every mum, every baby,

01:32:53.820 --> 01:32:58.770
every family member gets the
right safe personalised care

01:32:58.770 --> 01:33:01.020
and the best experience that they can.

01:33:01.020 --> 01:33:03.420
- So Duncan, thank you.
And Kelly, thank you

01:33:03.420 --> 01:33:07.290
by the way, very good
presentation and explanation.

01:33:07.290 --> 01:33:11.040
So thank you. But let's
open up to, so Mary,

01:33:11.040 --> 01:33:12.660
do you want to go?
- [Mary] Thank you.

01:33:12.660 --> 01:33:14.460
- Comments or questions to the team?

01:33:15.420 --> 01:33:17.310
- Firstly, congratulations

01:33:17.310 --> 01:33:20.580
because I think it's
being taken very seriously

01:33:20.580 --> 01:33:23.370
and I was particularly delighted to hear

01:33:23.370 --> 01:33:26.673
of your user representation involvement.

01:33:28.410 --> 01:33:29.790
I just wanted to look at

01:33:29.790 --> 01:33:33.450
whether you feel we've got the
preceptorship support right

01:33:33.450 --> 01:33:35.340
for newly qualified midwives

01:33:35.340 --> 01:33:40.110
because we really need to
retain them in midwifery

01:33:40.110 --> 01:33:44.520
and yet at the moment the
language is so poor in the press

01:33:44.520 --> 01:33:46.440
that there must be some
who think, you know,

01:33:46.440 --> 01:33:50.640
I'll get my qualification and
maybe not practise in Britain.

01:33:50.640 --> 01:33:52.470
So I just wanted to ask about that.

01:33:52.470 --> 01:33:55.590
- So can we back, we'll sort
of group the questions again

01:33:55.590 --> 01:33:59.730
to make it easier for
time management. Andrew.

01:33:59.730 --> 01:34:03.720
- Okay, so for me the most
important thing is around culture

01:34:03.720 --> 01:34:06.420
and I think Kelly, you hit
the nail on the head there,

01:34:07.734 --> 01:34:11.520
and not only have we got a
kind of leadership challenge,

01:34:11.520 --> 01:34:14.160
but we've got to make sure that boards are

01:34:14.160 --> 01:34:15.600
really focused on this.

01:34:15.600 --> 01:34:17.190
'Cause at the end of the day,

01:34:17.190 --> 01:34:19.500
NHS England can only do so much.

01:34:19.500 --> 01:34:22.410
It's the board of a trust basically

01:34:22.410 --> 01:34:24.510
that sets the culture and tone

01:34:24.510 --> 01:34:27.153
of how it wants to operate
within our hospital.

01:34:28.459 --> 01:34:30.120
And I'm just interested
in the work we are doing

01:34:30.120 --> 01:34:32.610
to support organisations on this

01:34:32.610 --> 01:34:35.490
and have we got some very
clear markers of improvement

01:34:35.490 --> 01:34:39.083
around culture as a
result of the programme

01:34:39.083 --> 01:34:42.780
because I think helping
organisations to move on,

01:34:42.780 --> 01:34:46.560
particularly the ones that
you are more concerned about

01:34:46.560 --> 01:34:49.950
is a really important
dimension here of success.

01:34:49.950 --> 01:34:52.470
And I'm just interested to know

01:34:52.470 --> 01:34:55.500
how much progress is being made.

01:34:55.500 --> 01:34:58.582
- And within that there's
quite a sort of subtle question

01:34:58.582 --> 01:35:02.340
if I can sort of add it,
which is and to what extent,

01:35:02.340 --> 01:35:04.860
so we're rolling out various
improvement programmes

01:35:04.860 --> 01:35:05.970
across all of the units,

01:35:05.970 --> 01:35:07.890
but to what extent are we also focused on

01:35:07.890 --> 01:35:11.970
really strong additional
support into those units

01:35:11.970 --> 01:35:13.620
where our data is showing us actually

01:35:13.620 --> 01:35:15.900
there may be an emerging vulnerability,

01:35:15.900 --> 01:35:20.418
or there's a greater risk? So Mark.

01:35:20.418 --> 01:35:22.680
- Can I add one thing to
your comment which is,

01:35:22.680 --> 01:35:25.140
given the number of reports at the moment

01:35:25.140 --> 01:35:28.320
into some very important issues,

01:35:28.320 --> 01:35:32.280
how are we extracting the
most important actions

01:35:32.280 --> 01:35:34.173
and making sure they're prioritised?

01:35:35.220 --> 01:35:37.200
- [Richard] Thank you.
- Which I think is an addition

01:35:37.200 --> 01:35:38.300
to what you've raised.

01:35:39.198 --> 01:35:41.803
- Okay, so do you want to have a go.

01:35:41.803 --> 01:35:44.943
- [Ruth] Duncan first and I'll finalise.

01:35:46.718 --> 01:35:48.123
- Okay, great.

01:35:48.123 --> 01:35:51.003
So Donald, do you want to
cover the culture element

01:35:51.003 --> 01:35:53.430
and what we're doing around culture,

01:35:53.430 --> 01:35:56.502
and then I'll come in
on the other two items?

01:35:56.502 --> 01:36:01.502
- Yeah, so the maternity
safety support programme we run

01:36:03.540 --> 01:36:07.230
and it's a group of 16 senior midwives

01:36:07.230 --> 01:36:08.560
and obstetricians

01:36:09.403 --> 01:36:13.380
who go into trusts often
alongside the recovery support

01:36:13.380 --> 01:36:16.860
programme as the maternity arm of it.

01:36:16.860 --> 01:36:18.090
We're getting much better

01:36:18.090 --> 01:36:20.100
I think at getting upstream.

01:36:20.100 --> 01:36:23.610
So not going in just when
things have gone wrong,

01:36:23.610 --> 01:36:27.960
but we're often invited by trust boards

01:36:27.960 --> 01:36:32.960
who have identified things that
they want some advice about.

01:36:33.060 --> 01:36:35.553
So we're getting much
more proactive about it.

01:36:37.020 --> 01:36:40.710
We really recognise this
business about trust boards

01:36:40.710 --> 01:36:42.870
and we're trying to help
in all sorts of ways.

01:36:42.870 --> 01:36:45.600
So for instance, packaging data,

01:36:45.600 --> 01:36:48.010
there's so much data
around maternity services

01:36:48.847 --> 01:36:50.880
and getting the right data to the board

01:36:50.880 --> 01:36:53.430
in the right way I think
is really important

01:36:53.430 --> 01:36:55.630
and we're doing that

01:36:56.790 --> 01:36:59.430
and also we've been
doing a lot of training

01:36:59.430 --> 01:37:01.729
for non-exec directors,

01:37:01.729 --> 01:37:04.650
safety board level safety champions,

01:37:04.650 --> 01:37:08.070
what do they need to know
about maternity services?

01:37:08.070 --> 01:37:10.600
I've taken part personally in 90

01:37:11.520 --> 01:37:14.430
sort of face-to-face meetings with boards

01:37:14.430 --> 01:37:17.220
around maternity services
talking about them

01:37:17.220 --> 01:37:19.140
and what do they need to know.

01:37:19.140 --> 01:37:20.290
- Very good, thank you.

01:37:21.466 --> 01:37:23.685
- [Donald] Great. Kelly, you
wanted to make a comment then?

01:37:23.685 --> 01:37:25.380
- Yeah. It was just say, and I
picked up on your point about

01:37:25.380 --> 01:37:27.930
there's only so much we
can do nationally as well

01:37:27.930 --> 01:37:30.750
and from a service user voice perspective

01:37:30.750 --> 01:37:32.400
and influencing that culture,

01:37:32.400 --> 01:37:35.670
we have published the
MNVP guidance which states

01:37:35.670 --> 01:37:36.960
our expectations.

01:37:36.960 --> 01:37:39.480
But also for me it's really
important as a national team

01:37:39.480 --> 01:37:42.300
that we are role modelling
that culture of involving

01:37:42.300 --> 01:37:44.370
and listening to service users.

01:37:44.370 --> 01:37:45.420
So as I said before,

01:37:45.420 --> 01:37:48.330
we are involved in committee
board leadership group,

01:37:48.330 --> 01:37:50.460
we co-chair some meetings,

01:37:50.460 --> 01:37:52.560
the maternity safety support programme

01:37:52.560 --> 01:37:53.760
that Donald's mentioned.

01:37:53.760 --> 01:37:56.370
We have service user voice
representation on that

01:37:56.370 --> 01:37:59.640
and also specialised
commissioning regional visits.

01:37:59.640 --> 01:38:01.620
So I think there's a
really important aspect

01:38:01.620 --> 01:38:04.320
of that role modelling.

01:38:04.320 --> 01:38:08.130
- Thank you. And I think just
to finally on that point,

01:38:08.130 --> 01:38:10.980
you see the difference
between those trusts

01:38:10.980 --> 01:38:12.725
that are on the MSSP

01:38:12.725 --> 01:38:13.650
and the improvement that they make

01:38:13.650 --> 01:38:16.186
with the engagement
that's done by the board.

01:38:16.186 --> 01:38:18.060
So that's why we've done
a lot of work to join up

01:38:18.060 --> 01:38:20.193
with the recovery support programme,

01:38:20.193 --> 01:38:22.176
which Emily's team runs.

01:38:22.176 --> 01:38:25.350
To your point, Mary, around preceptorship,

01:38:25.350 --> 01:38:26.370
you will probably know

01:38:26.370 --> 01:38:28.590
that we've put in retention midwives

01:38:28.590 --> 01:38:31.920
into all of our maternity
units over the last few years

01:38:31.920 --> 01:38:36.270
and that has enabled not only
just focus on those colleagues

01:38:36.270 --> 01:38:37.800
that have been there for a while,

01:38:37.800 --> 01:38:39.570
but certainly for those new midwives.

01:38:39.570 --> 01:38:41.190
And I think we've seen
the difference on that.

01:38:41.190 --> 01:38:43.230
But we know that there is
a lot of variation still

01:38:43.230 --> 01:38:46.950
between the experience of
students, and our student midwives

01:38:46.950 --> 01:38:48.660
and therefore, you know,
there's a lot of work

01:38:48.660 --> 01:38:51.450
that's been done around
perceptship standards

01:38:51.450 --> 01:38:53.280
and student learning charters, et cetera.

01:38:53.280 --> 01:38:55.260
But I think there is still more to do

01:38:55.260 --> 01:38:56.940
and to reduce down that variation,

01:38:56.940 --> 01:38:59.940
which I think speaks to the
work that we've got to do

01:38:59.940 --> 01:39:02.790
across the board around
maternity and neonatal services

01:39:02.790 --> 01:39:05.880
and reducing that variation
down still further.

01:39:05.880 --> 01:39:09.870
And then just to your point
around prioritising the reports,

01:39:09.870 --> 01:39:11.550
I think that was, you know, as Ruth said,

01:39:11.550 --> 01:39:13.440
that was one of the
fundamental reasons why

01:39:13.440 --> 01:39:15.330
we brought the three year
delivery plan together

01:39:15.330 --> 01:39:18.000
to try and clear some of the landscape

01:39:18.000 --> 01:39:19.860
and to make sure that the recommendation,

01:39:19.860 --> 01:39:21.810
the prioritisation of
these recommendations

01:39:21.810 --> 01:39:26.810
were really part of that
three year delivery plan

01:39:27.562 --> 01:39:28.590
so that it was clearer for our services.

01:39:28.590 --> 01:39:31.140
But I think we are going
to have to continue

01:39:31.140 --> 01:39:32.910
through the period of the
three year delivery plan

01:39:32.910 --> 01:39:35.550
and beyond, particularly
as we look at some of the

01:39:35.550 --> 01:39:39.930
change in demographics
and the impacts of that to

01:39:39.930 --> 01:39:43.920
continually look at the
prioritisation of those actions.

01:39:43.920 --> 01:39:45.957
But Ruth, shall I hand back to you?

01:39:45.957 --> 01:39:48.420
- My final point then
would be on that to Mark,

01:39:48.420 --> 01:39:51.990
which was, if there's only two things

01:39:51.990 --> 01:39:54.840
that a board need to think about is,

01:39:54.840 --> 01:39:57.810
listen to women and families.

01:39:57.810 --> 01:39:59.283
And the second one was,

01:40:00.510 --> 01:40:04.140
put some personal focus on it as a board.

01:40:04.140 --> 01:40:08.640
And I'm very grateful for this
board supporting, nudging,

01:40:08.640 --> 01:40:12.150
challenging us as a team
so that we can collectively

01:40:12.150 --> 01:40:14.073
across England get to our ambition.

01:40:15.870 --> 01:40:18.840
- Thank you. Amanda, any
comments you want to make

01:40:18.840 --> 01:40:22.440
before we move on to the next item?

01:40:22.440 --> 01:40:26.190
- Just to say, I think
again, what others have said,

01:40:26.190 --> 01:40:29.070
this is an incredibly
important conversation

01:40:29.070 --> 01:40:32.550
and actually Duncan, I
thought you are just bringing

01:40:32.550 --> 01:40:35.340
to life why it is with
the number of babies

01:40:35.340 --> 01:40:36.788
that have been born

01:40:36.788 --> 01:40:37.621
since we've been having this conversation.

01:40:37.621 --> 01:40:40.833
Really, it's just a
brilliant way of reminding us

01:40:40.833 --> 01:40:43.830
that actually we don't
have any time to waste.

01:40:43.830 --> 01:40:48.298
So, brilliant to see how
seriously everybody has taken it.

01:40:48.298 --> 01:40:49.830
Kelly, thank you for your work,

01:40:49.830 --> 01:40:54.830
but also for describing in
such a, again, a powerful way,

01:40:55.170 --> 01:40:58.470
the truth of, you know, we
talk a lot about patient

01:40:58.470 --> 01:41:00.120
and public involvement,

01:41:00.120 --> 01:41:03.150
but the truth of in this
programme, how real that is

01:41:03.150 --> 01:41:06.300
and how material it is to
everything that you do.

01:41:06.300 --> 01:41:09.270
And I think I would just
say again, there is so much

01:41:09.270 --> 01:41:11.670
that's already in the plan
that we know we need to do.

01:41:11.670 --> 01:41:14.826
But just to really welcome
the opportunity now

01:41:14.826 --> 01:41:17.430
for me personally to work
with the Secretary of State,

01:41:17.430 --> 01:41:19.650
to pull together that
cross government strategy

01:41:19.650 --> 01:41:22.380
to make sure that we
are taking this across

01:41:22.380 --> 01:41:26.280
with cross government support,
that we're really pushing

01:41:26.280 --> 01:41:29.700
forward in all of the ways
that we know really matter

01:41:29.700 --> 01:41:30.840
that we're doing anyway,

01:41:30.840 --> 01:41:33.030
but that we're also looking
for those other opportunities

01:41:33.030 --> 01:41:35.610
to get behind and support this agenda.

01:41:35.610 --> 01:41:37.123
- Amanda, thank you.

01:41:37.123 --> 01:41:39.224
And thank you also Ruth, thank you.

01:41:39.224 --> 01:41:41.970
And Donald and Kelly and and
Duncan. Thank you very much.

01:41:41.970 --> 01:41:43.260
So let's move on.

01:41:43.260 --> 01:41:44.790
We're going to go to item eight,

01:41:44.790 --> 01:41:48.630
which is the topic of research in the NHS,

01:41:48.630 --> 01:41:50.580
Vin, I think you are leading in for us.

01:41:50.580 --> 01:41:53.490
- Yeah. Thank you. Thank you chair.

01:41:53.490 --> 01:41:57.450
So firstly, I'll just note
that I think this board

01:41:57.450 --> 01:42:00.510
has got some of the most
distinguished clinical academics

01:42:00.510 --> 01:42:02.130
actually in the world on it.

01:42:02.130 --> 01:42:04.230
And the fact that we've
got several of them here

01:42:04.230 --> 01:42:05.760
I think is a measure of how seriously

01:42:05.760 --> 01:42:09.000
the board takes research actually
in terms of the skill mix.

01:42:09.000 --> 01:42:11.050
I do just think that's important to note.

01:42:12.540 --> 01:42:14.220
I always feel slightly
humbled by that as well

01:42:14.220 --> 01:42:15.570
by being in charge of research.

01:42:15.570 --> 01:42:17.340
So the second bit I think is that

01:42:17.340 --> 01:42:19.650
I'll just say a few words
about how we are working

01:42:19.650 --> 01:42:22.410
with universities, how
we work with patients,

01:42:22.410 --> 01:42:24.300
how we work with researchers,

01:42:24.300 --> 01:42:27.085
and then open it up to discussion chair

01:42:27.085 --> 01:42:28.530
on what else it is that NHS England can do

01:42:28.530 --> 01:42:30.060
to support research.

01:42:30.060 --> 01:42:32.040
So the first thing is, I
think inherently we all know

01:42:32.040 --> 01:42:33.360
that research is important,

01:42:33.360 --> 01:42:35.880
not just in creating new discoveries,

01:42:35.880 --> 01:42:38.490
but also we know that
staff retention's better

01:42:38.490 --> 01:42:41.310
if you're working in an
organisation which does research.

01:42:41.310 --> 01:42:44.160
And secondly, we know that
clinical outcomes are better

01:42:44.160 --> 01:42:47.130
if you are a patient and
you are in a research study.

01:42:47.130 --> 01:42:50.160
So it's important a number of levels.

01:42:50.160 --> 01:42:52.710
As I said NHS England works in partnership

01:42:52.710 --> 01:42:55.110
with the National Institute
of Health Research

01:42:55.110 --> 01:42:58.050
and the Medical Research Council
and other funding bodies.

01:42:58.050 --> 01:43:00.450
And actually we've got very distinct roles

01:43:00.450 --> 01:43:03.840
which are set out in the
paper in paragraph eight.

01:43:03.840 --> 01:43:06.720
And it is important to
remember we are not the sole

01:43:06.720 --> 01:43:09.360
national body responsible for research.

01:43:09.360 --> 01:43:11.730
Now there are three particular ways

01:43:11.730 --> 01:43:14.070
that I think we just want
to reflect on in the ways

01:43:14.070 --> 01:43:18.930
that we in NHS England are
working with to promote research.

01:43:18.930 --> 01:43:22.710
So the first thing is that
the NHS digi trials service

01:43:22.710 --> 01:43:25.440
supports academics with
recruiting patients.

01:43:25.440 --> 01:43:28.410
So 140,000 participants

01:43:28.410 --> 01:43:30.453
recruited into the Galleri trial,

01:43:31.650 --> 01:43:34.050
thanks to NHS digi trials.

01:43:34.050 --> 01:43:38.130
Secondly, volunteers who wish
to participate in research

01:43:38.130 --> 01:43:40.260
can register through the NHS app.

01:43:40.260 --> 01:43:42.780
So 80% of people who've registered

01:43:42.780 --> 01:43:44.850
to be part of research

01:43:44.850 --> 01:43:46.380
have registered through the app.

01:43:46.380 --> 01:43:49.500
And then thirdly, we
also specifically work

01:43:49.500 --> 01:43:51.754
with local communities through our

01:43:51.754 --> 01:43:54.930
trusted engagement networks

01:43:54.930 --> 01:43:57.420
to particularly work
with community leaders

01:43:57.420 --> 01:43:59.954
in local underserved communities

01:43:59.954 --> 01:44:01.290
to make sure that they fully participate

01:44:01.290 --> 01:44:02.220
in research as well.

01:44:02.220 --> 01:44:04.290
So that's the way in which we are working

01:44:04.290 --> 01:44:07.170
with patients and members
of the public on it.

01:44:07.170 --> 01:44:09.300
Secondly, how do we work
with academics with,

01:44:09.300 --> 01:44:12.480
of course there are longstanding
partnerships in the NHS

01:44:12.480 --> 01:44:14.700
through things such as
biomedical research centres

01:44:14.700 --> 01:44:18.600
and others through clinical
research facilities

01:44:18.600 --> 01:44:21.390
with which we've worked and
created some of the best

01:44:21.390 --> 01:44:23.310
research facilities in the world actually.

01:44:23.310 --> 01:44:26.670
And so it's to make the UK
good place to do research.

01:44:26.670 --> 01:44:28.680
But very practically speaking,

01:44:28.680 --> 01:44:30.960
some of the work we've
undertaken more recently

01:44:30.960 --> 01:44:35.190
recognising that prior to the pandemic

01:44:35.190 --> 01:44:36.840
the number of patients being recruited

01:44:36.840 --> 01:44:39.990
into commercial clinical
trials was dropping,

01:44:39.990 --> 01:44:43.020
which has now started
increasing are for example,

01:44:43.020 --> 01:44:46.890
we have developed a single
costing and contracting mechanism

01:44:46.890 --> 01:44:48.660
which has taken more than 100 days

01:44:48.660 --> 01:44:52.860
off the time it takes to
create a contract for research

01:44:52.860 --> 01:44:56.610
that reduces the setup
time for a clinical trial.

01:44:56.610 --> 01:44:59.580
Secondly, we're building
research data infrastructure

01:44:59.580 --> 01:45:03.360
through our secure data
environments across England,

01:45:03.360 --> 01:45:05.970
which will provide a
much richer set of data

01:45:05.970 --> 01:45:09.240
in which research can happen.

01:45:09.240 --> 01:45:11.670
And thirdly, we demand
signal out to industry

01:45:11.670 --> 01:45:14.040
and out to academia about
the sorts of studies

01:45:14.040 --> 01:45:16.140
we think are important for our studies.

01:45:16.140 --> 01:45:17.970
And so for example,

01:45:17.970 --> 01:45:22.530
Ruth's team have produced
a nursing research strategy

01:45:22.530 --> 01:45:24.840
and we've also put out calls for example,

01:45:24.840 --> 01:45:27.930
for mental health research
and stroke research

01:45:27.930 --> 01:45:31.860
which have led to an increasing
number of funding calls.

01:45:31.860 --> 01:45:33.900
So those are all the
good things that we do,

01:45:33.900 --> 01:45:36.090
I think in research where
we've shown real evidence

01:45:36.090 --> 01:45:38.220
of progress and of teamwork.

01:45:38.220 --> 01:45:41.070
But nevertheless, there
are some key challenges

01:45:41.070 --> 01:45:42.330
that I think are important.

01:45:42.330 --> 01:45:44.340
We've set those out in the paper.

01:45:44.340 --> 01:45:45.590
So I'll pause there chair

01:45:46.514 --> 01:45:47.790
and let the board discuss perhaps

01:45:47.790 --> 01:45:50.077
some of the key questions in the paper

01:45:50.077 --> 01:45:51.416
that members may wish to raise.

01:45:51.416 --> 01:45:53.550
- So let's go Robert,
then Steve, then Mark,

01:45:53.550 --> 01:45:57.660
and then we'll batch those
up then. Okay, so Robert.

01:45:57.660 --> 01:45:58.680
- Yeah, well thanks very much,

01:45:58.680 --> 01:46:00.090
and thanks very much for the paper

01:46:00.090 --> 01:46:03.850
and it's great to have
it on the board agenda

01:46:04.710 --> 01:46:07.650
and there's a lot of
great stuff in the paper.

01:46:07.650 --> 01:46:10.560
The risk when the health services

01:46:10.560 --> 01:46:11.790
under such intense pressure

01:46:11.790 --> 01:46:14.760
is that research can be
seen as a nice to have.

01:46:14.760 --> 01:46:17.520
You've made some of the
reason points as to why

01:46:17.520 --> 01:46:19.413
it should be seen as essential.

01:46:20.460 --> 01:46:23.070
I just add to that,

01:46:23.070 --> 01:46:27.360
that actually if we capitalise
on the opportunities

01:46:27.360 --> 01:46:28.440
that research provides,

01:46:28.440 --> 01:46:30.960
we can address some of the
pressures in the longer term.

01:46:30.960 --> 01:46:34.503
So we can get much smarter
and better at prevention.

01:46:35.580 --> 01:46:37.950
We can use engineering skills

01:46:37.950 --> 01:46:40.020
and technologies to re-engineer pathways

01:46:40.020 --> 01:46:42.870
to become more productive
back to productivity

01:46:42.870 --> 01:46:45.090
and using discovery,

01:46:45.090 --> 01:46:48.120
we can take some chronic
diseases and cure them.

01:46:48.120 --> 01:46:50.160
So that's the ambition that we should have

01:46:50.160 --> 01:46:51.883
with our research agenda

01:46:51.883 --> 01:46:53.490
and I think that's why it's so important.

01:46:53.490 --> 01:46:55.170
So the only thing I would say,

01:46:55.170 --> 01:46:56.580
and you mentioned universities,

01:46:56.580 --> 01:46:58.890
I think making university partnerships

01:46:58.890 --> 01:47:00.750
with the NHS work really well

01:47:00.750 --> 01:47:03.660
is key to realising on our potential

01:47:03.660 --> 01:47:05.640
and likewise life sciences industries

01:47:05.640 --> 01:47:07.260
who really want to partner with us

01:47:07.260 --> 01:47:09.480
because we've got such fantastic resources

01:47:09.480 --> 01:47:11.520
to facilitate drug discovery.

01:47:11.520 --> 01:47:13.320
So really positive,

01:47:13.320 --> 01:47:14.910
but I would just emphasise

01:47:14.910 --> 01:47:16.983
those two partnerships in particular.

01:47:18.030 --> 01:47:19.050
- Thank you Robert. Steve.

01:47:19.050 --> 01:47:20.490
- Well it was really to
build on Robert's point,

01:47:20.490 --> 01:47:21.810
so again, I really welcome

01:47:21.810 --> 01:47:25.470
that we are discussing
this at the board today.

01:47:25.470 --> 01:47:27.850
But I think it's important
to recognise that

01:47:28.890 --> 01:47:30.030
it's the same staff

01:47:30.030 --> 01:47:32.550
that have delivered all those improvements

01:47:32.550 --> 01:47:35.670
that we've seen in care
and performance indicators

01:47:35.670 --> 01:47:38.910
such as the faster diagnosis
standard earlier in the board

01:47:38.910 --> 01:47:42.060
that are also driving the research

01:47:42.060 --> 01:47:43.530
and supporting the clinical trials

01:47:43.530 --> 01:47:45.781
and all the research
that goes on in the NHS.

01:47:45.781 --> 01:47:46.614
And as Robert says,

01:47:46.614 --> 01:47:48.150
that is particularly
challenging at the moment

01:47:48.150 --> 01:47:51.240
for all the reasons we know
about pandemic recovery

01:47:51.240 --> 01:47:53.580
and recovering waiting lists.

01:47:53.580 --> 01:47:57.450
So it's important that
we recognise that balance

01:47:57.450 --> 01:47:58.950
is difficult to achieve at the moment,

01:47:58.950 --> 01:48:00.180
but it's also important

01:48:00.180 --> 01:48:02.070
that we recognise it should be a both end,

01:48:02.070 --> 01:48:05.640
and not an either or because
for all the reasons you said,

01:48:05.640 --> 01:48:08.160
it's important that we undertake research

01:48:08.160 --> 01:48:09.090
and support research.

01:48:09.090 --> 01:48:10.710
And the final thing I'd say,

01:48:10.710 --> 01:48:13.860
is that clinicians, and
it's not just doctors

01:48:13.860 --> 01:48:15.460
in my experience, really enjoy

01:48:16.881 --> 01:48:18.630
and get a huge amount
of personal satisfaction

01:48:18.630 --> 01:48:20.190
out of undertaking research

01:48:20.190 --> 01:48:23.130
and seeing the benefits that
derive from that research.

01:48:23.130 --> 01:48:26.460
So, giving clinicians
the time and the space,

01:48:26.460 --> 01:48:27.960
the resource to do this

01:48:27.960 --> 01:48:30.630
also has benefits in
terms of energising them,

01:48:30.630 --> 01:48:34.050
enthusing them for all the
other great work they do.

01:48:34.050 --> 01:48:35.246
- Thank you.

01:48:35.246 --> 01:48:36.630
- I think just to add to all of that,

01:48:36.630 --> 01:48:39.270
the pattern of disease is not a constant.

01:48:39.270 --> 01:48:42.240
So there are constantly
new diseases emerging.

01:48:42.240 --> 01:48:44.460
We are learning more about rare diseases.

01:48:44.460 --> 01:48:47.190
So diseases such as
spinal muscular atrophy

01:48:47.190 --> 01:48:50.250
are potentially curable
by genetic therapies.

01:48:50.250 --> 01:48:53.760
And rare diseases
collectively are very common

01:48:53.760 --> 01:48:56.760
and it tends to be academic research staff

01:48:56.760 --> 01:49:00.000
that work on these and help to understand.

01:49:00.000 --> 01:49:02.820
And so for all those reasons,

01:49:02.820 --> 01:49:04.800
research has got to be
absolutely intrinsic

01:49:04.800 --> 01:49:05.940
to the health service.

01:49:05.940 --> 01:49:07.830
And you could look at Covid

01:49:07.830 --> 01:49:11.040
and say that the UK, the recovery trial

01:49:11.040 --> 01:49:12.510
was the best trial in the world.

01:49:12.510 --> 01:49:14.610
It was the largest trial in the world.

01:49:14.610 --> 01:49:16.950
It showed drugs that
did work, Dexamethasone

01:49:16.950 --> 01:49:20.403
and drugs that didn't work,
Hydroxychloroquine, Ivermectin.

01:49:21.420 --> 01:49:23.940
And I think in many ways that
could only have been done

01:49:23.940 --> 01:49:25.680
in a system like the
National Health Service.

01:49:25.680 --> 01:49:28.080
So the opportunities are enormous

01:49:28.080 --> 01:49:30.000
and we've just got to make
sure we've got a workforce

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:32.853
that's equipped and able to carry this on.

01:49:34.560 --> 01:49:35.970
- You want to react to those?

01:49:35.970 --> 01:49:38.010
- Yeah, so three points I think.

01:49:38.010 --> 01:49:40.110
So first I think on Robert's point,

01:49:40.110 --> 01:49:44.010
we do work very closely with
NIHR to make absolutely certain

01:49:44.010 --> 01:49:47.700
that particularly we signal
to them that both operational

01:49:47.700 --> 01:49:50.220
and systems research is as important

01:49:50.220 --> 01:49:53.520
to the service as is biomedical research.

01:49:53.520 --> 01:49:56.130
To talk to Steve's point,

01:49:56.130 --> 01:49:58.470
we have just published finance guidance

01:49:58.470 --> 01:50:00.090
for finance directors of trust

01:50:00.090 --> 01:50:02.430
to make sure that particularly
where there's income,

01:50:02.430 --> 01:50:03.810
either from funding bodies

01:50:03.810 --> 01:50:06.510
or from commercial clinical trials,

01:50:06.510 --> 01:50:09.030
that those actually find
their way into job plans,

01:50:09.030 --> 01:50:11.367
for example, for consultants
and for nursing staff

01:50:11.367 --> 01:50:13.860
and for others involved in research.

01:50:13.860 --> 01:50:15.090
And so their times released

01:50:15.090 --> 01:50:17.970
and I've seen certainly
been to Coventry recently

01:50:17.970 --> 01:50:19.350
where the chief finance officer

01:50:19.350 --> 01:50:22.170
is also the chief research
officer for the organisation.

01:50:22.170 --> 01:50:24.420
So they're absolutely
embedded as part of it.

01:50:25.620 --> 01:50:27.540
The third bit is I
absolutely agree with Mark

01:50:27.540 --> 01:50:29.670
and particularly on
rare diseases research,

01:50:29.670 --> 01:50:32.070
particularly having worked
at places like Birmingham

01:50:32.070 --> 01:50:33.390
Children's and Great Ormond Street

01:50:33.390 --> 01:50:35.610
where we treat patients
with rare diseases.

01:50:35.610 --> 01:50:37.620
One of the key points behind that,

01:50:37.620 --> 01:50:40.680
and I briefly mentioned our
research data infrastructures,

01:50:40.680 --> 01:50:42.120
of course you need large data sets

01:50:42.120 --> 01:50:47.120
in order to identify and
understanding the root causes

01:50:47.250 --> 01:50:51.360
of rare diseases,
particularly the genomic ones.

01:50:51.360 --> 01:50:53.670
One of the important things
about creating large data sets

01:50:53.670 --> 01:50:55.020
is that we take the public with us

01:50:55.020 --> 01:50:57.870
'cause there's understandable
public ask questions

01:50:57.870 --> 01:50:59.820
about the security of their data

01:50:59.820 --> 01:51:02.730
and to make sure it's
properly de-identified.

01:51:02.730 --> 01:51:04.140
But we can still conduct research.

01:51:04.140 --> 01:51:07.920
In fact, I spent Saturday working

01:51:07.920 --> 01:51:09.600
with citizens and jury,

01:51:09.600 --> 01:51:11.700
and members of the public in Sheffield,

01:51:11.700 --> 01:51:14.100
but there were simultaneous
events being to Plymouth

01:51:14.100 --> 01:51:16.020
and Canterbury and to London

01:51:16.020 --> 01:51:18.600
where we are working with
the public to understand

01:51:18.600 --> 01:51:21.210
both their attitudes to research data

01:51:21.210 --> 01:51:26.040
and also working with
them on how we can best

01:51:26.040 --> 01:51:28.050
optimise their participation in research

01:51:28.050 --> 01:51:30.150
and their trust in research data.

01:51:30.150 --> 01:51:33.750
And in fact, one of the members
of the public I spoke to

01:51:33.750 --> 01:51:35.850
had opted out of her data being used

01:51:35.850 --> 01:51:37.890
and having heard all
the benefits of research

01:51:37.890 --> 01:51:40.980
and participating in the data,
decided to opt back in again

01:51:40.980 --> 01:51:42.063
'cause she had a rare disease

01:51:42.063 --> 01:51:43.500
and wanted to participate in it.

01:51:43.500 --> 01:51:44.640
And so we can make a real difference

01:51:44.640 --> 01:51:45.840
with this kind of engagement.

01:51:45.840 --> 01:51:48.750
- Ruth, you want to...
- Make just a very, very quick

01:51:48.750 --> 01:51:52.500
just saying that was part
of the nursing strategy

01:51:52.500 --> 01:51:54.420
for research,

01:51:54.420 --> 01:51:57.060
we wanted to focus on nurses themselves

01:51:57.060 --> 01:51:59.850
being the primary investigator,

01:51:59.850 --> 01:52:01.890
but also clinical academic careers

01:52:01.890 --> 01:52:05.070
and working with partners
across in the university.

01:52:05.070 --> 01:52:07.560
So Joseph Manning at
Nottingham for example,

01:52:07.560 --> 01:52:10.950
is a great example of a
paediatric nurse consultant

01:52:10.950 --> 01:52:12.959
also working in university.

01:52:12.959 --> 01:52:15.620
And we should encourage more
of that type of approach.

01:52:15.620 --> 01:52:16.697
Thank you chair.
- Thank you.

01:52:16.697 --> 01:52:19.920
So I'm going to close this topic down now

01:52:19.920 --> 01:52:22.770
for the purpose of this board,

01:52:22.770 --> 01:52:24.703
but we know it's going to come back

01:52:24.703 --> 01:52:25.950
and we had a very good
discussion on it this morning

01:52:25.950 --> 01:52:27.420
at the private board,

01:52:27.420 --> 01:52:29.640
and one of these we agreed
then was to bring it back

01:52:29.640 --> 01:52:32.760
to make sure we keep in
fact increase our focus

01:52:32.760 --> 01:52:35.100
on just making sure that
our research engines

01:52:35.100 --> 01:52:36.630
and our research collaborations

01:52:36.630 --> 01:52:41.630
and our energies are continuing
to improve and increase.

01:52:42.420 --> 01:52:43.253
Thank you.

01:52:44.130 --> 01:52:47.010
So we are going to go on to

01:52:47.010 --> 01:52:49.997
the item which is actually just
reporting on the completion

01:52:49.997 --> 01:52:51.960
of the new NHS England programme.

01:52:51.960 --> 01:52:54.110
And in this one I'm
actually asking the board

01:52:54.110 --> 01:52:55.610
at the end of the conversation

01:52:56.460 --> 01:52:58.590
to approve I think four things.

01:52:58.590 --> 01:53:01.410
First that we're going to close down

01:53:01.410 --> 01:53:04.590
the NHS oversight committee

01:53:04.590 --> 01:53:06.090
that has been actually running

01:53:06.090 --> 01:53:07.920
as a subcommittee of the board

01:53:07.920 --> 01:53:09.450
governing for the past two years,

01:53:09.450 --> 01:53:12.090
actually the merger of
essentially five entities,

01:53:12.090 --> 01:53:17.090
four entities and the rationalisation

01:53:17.490 --> 01:53:21.273
of the programmes and
the workforce and so on.

01:53:22.110 --> 01:53:24.097
The second approval actually is

01:53:24.097 --> 01:53:25.620
that we retain a very small programme team

01:53:25.620 --> 01:53:28.110
at the centre of reporting to the CDO,

01:53:28.110 --> 01:53:30.690
and that's to deliver
ongoing areas of work.

01:53:30.690 --> 01:53:32.340
It isn't completely finished,

01:53:32.340 --> 01:53:34.380
but we think it's in a state now where

01:53:34.380 --> 01:53:39.150
it should go back into
direct executive management

01:53:39.150 --> 01:53:41.790
without needing a separate subcommittee.

01:53:41.790 --> 01:53:43.620
And then that's the third approval,

01:53:43.620 --> 01:53:45.960
which we agree that the NHS execs

01:53:45.960 --> 01:53:49.323
will assume accountability
for the areas of ongoing work,

01:53:50.550 --> 01:53:52.440
board committee oversight will apply

01:53:52.440 --> 01:53:56.670
as normal through the normal
board committees subcommittees

01:53:56.670 --> 01:53:59.220
that exist, but without
this particular one.

01:53:59.220 --> 01:54:00.210
But that would include

01:54:00.210 --> 01:54:03.330
and we should make sure
that we bring it out.

01:54:03.330 --> 01:54:07.260
It includes the continued
work on organisation design

01:54:07.260 --> 01:54:08.733
and culture programme,

01:54:09.780 --> 01:54:12.600
as well as the
establishment of an internal

01:54:12.600 --> 01:54:14.465
improvement team.

01:54:14.465 --> 01:54:16.980
So whilst the initial merger,
et cetera has finished

01:54:16.980 --> 01:54:19.170
actually this continuing momentum

01:54:19.170 --> 01:54:20.880
and within the programme
programme just to make sure

01:54:20.880 --> 01:54:23.050
that NHS England continues as a model

01:54:24.090 --> 01:54:26.520
to work to its best endeavours.

01:54:26.520 --> 01:54:27.870
And then actually we should just note

01:54:27.870 --> 01:54:29.727
it's less than approval actually.

01:54:29.727 --> 01:54:31.560
We just want to note planned internal

01:54:31.560 --> 01:54:35.340
and external communications on
the closure of the programme

01:54:35.340 --> 01:54:36.777
and the learning we've got from it

01:54:36.777 --> 01:54:39.510
and the next steps that will be pursued.

01:54:39.510 --> 01:54:41.460
So those will be the approvals

01:54:41.460 --> 01:54:45.510
that I'm asking for
the board's support for

01:54:45.510 --> 01:54:47.280
when we be in the conversation to a close.

01:54:47.280 --> 01:54:48.930
But Steve, to you I think.

01:54:48.930 --> 01:54:50.220
- Thank you Chair.

01:54:50.220 --> 01:54:52.293
You've done my pitch for me actually.

01:54:53.430 --> 01:54:55.769
- [Richard] Just the approval, Steve.

01:54:55.769 --> 01:54:57.720
- So just to reflect
on a couple of points.

01:54:57.720 --> 01:55:00.270
So we talked through the meeting about

01:55:00.270 --> 01:55:05.270
this being one of the largest
UK public sector changes

01:55:05.700 --> 01:55:10.590
and it has undoubtedly
touched every single member

01:55:10.590 --> 01:55:14.073
of NHS England, Health Education England,

01:55:15.150 --> 01:55:18.390
and NHS Digital over quite some time

01:55:18.390 --> 01:55:21.300
and in quite profound
ways in many respects.

01:55:21.300 --> 01:55:22.980
And that's partly because
of the size of the task,

01:55:22.980 --> 01:55:26.760
bringing five organisations
together at the same time

01:55:26.760 --> 01:55:29.940
as redesigning and downsizing

01:55:29.940 --> 01:55:32.490
and changing how we plan to work.

01:55:32.490 --> 01:55:35.100
So doing that all in one go

01:55:35.100 --> 01:55:37.020
has meant that it has been quite a long

01:55:37.020 --> 01:55:39.330
and quite a challenging process

01:55:39.330 --> 01:55:42.153
for colleagues in the organisation.

01:55:43.050 --> 01:55:47.160
And that, to be fair, a very
big difference from many other

01:55:47.160 --> 01:55:51.840
industries is that, people
in those organisations,

01:55:51.840 --> 01:55:54.480
this is not the first reorganisation.

01:55:54.480 --> 01:55:59.480
So this has been a relatively
constant set of restructuring

01:56:00.150 --> 01:56:02.040
that has happened over many years.

01:56:02.040 --> 01:56:03.390
So this was not the first time

01:56:03.390 --> 01:56:05.520
that colleagues have
had to go through this

01:56:05.520 --> 01:56:07.720
and that has quite a disruptive effect

01:56:08.790 --> 01:56:11.220
for colleagues working
in the organisations,

01:56:11.220 --> 01:56:13.200
but also in terms of the work that we do

01:56:13.200 --> 01:56:15.870
to try and support the NHS.

01:56:15.870 --> 01:56:17.850
And of course it's worth
just calling out that

01:56:17.850 --> 01:56:20.970
as we have done this over
the last couple of years,

01:56:20.970 --> 01:56:21.990
integrated care boards

01:56:21.990 --> 01:56:25.560
have also had to go through
changes across the country

01:56:25.560 --> 01:56:27.363
to reduce their running costs.

01:56:28.560 --> 01:56:31.200
So to not to repeat what
colleagues have said,

01:56:31.200 --> 01:56:34.740
but perhaps to say that

01:56:34.740 --> 01:56:37.500
although it has been a
long and difficult road,

01:56:37.500 --> 01:56:39.120
a number of the commitments

01:56:39.120 --> 01:56:41.700
that we made at the
beginning of the programme

01:56:41.700 --> 01:56:46.700
in terms of reducing our
complexity, our silo working,

01:56:47.280 --> 01:56:49.980
releasing money to go
back to the front line

01:56:49.980 --> 01:56:52.884
and trying to create, as
Richard as you described,

01:56:52.884 --> 01:56:54.600
a more streamlined organisation,

01:56:54.600 --> 01:56:56.640
we've got the possibility to achieve that.

01:56:56.640 --> 01:56:57.930
Much of the work has been done

01:56:57.930 --> 01:57:00.000
to put the foundations in place

01:57:00.000 --> 01:57:02.700
and as colleagues have
said the structural change

01:57:02.700 --> 01:57:05.670
has come to an end,
although it is important

01:57:05.670 --> 01:57:07.920
to recognise there's a very
small group of colleagues

01:57:07.920 --> 01:57:11.370
who are still going through
the final redeployment

01:57:11.370 --> 01:57:13.233
part of the process.

01:57:14.100 --> 01:57:14.970
All of those changes

01:57:14.970 --> 01:57:19.080
have had quite an impact
on morale for people

01:57:19.080 --> 01:57:20.700
because alongside that restructuring,

01:57:20.700 --> 01:57:25.350
we've had recruitment freezes
in place for quite some time.

01:57:25.350 --> 01:57:28.170
But, I think it's also important to say,

01:57:28.170 --> 01:57:31.140
and Amanda made this point
more eloquent than I will,

01:57:31.140 --> 01:57:33.600
that throughout the board discussions

01:57:33.600 --> 01:57:36.030
we've talked about really
positive improvements

01:57:36.030 --> 01:57:37.230
that have been secured.

01:57:37.230 --> 01:57:39.120
Now lots of that has been down

01:57:39.120 --> 01:57:41.670
to the hard work of people in the service,

01:57:41.670 --> 01:57:42.930
but it's also true to say

01:57:42.930 --> 01:57:45.210
that lots of that has been supported

01:57:45.210 --> 01:57:48.300
by the hard work of
colleagues in NHS England.

01:57:48.300 --> 01:57:52.740
And there has been rather a
lot of discretionary effort

01:57:52.740 --> 01:57:54.130
that has gone alongside

01:57:55.560 --> 01:57:58.020
the engagement in the change programme.

01:57:58.020 --> 01:58:03.020
So, there is also...

01:58:03.105 --> 01:58:03.938
Is that the rain?

01:58:03.938 --> 01:58:07.410
It's also a really important
opportunity in front of us

01:58:07.410 --> 01:58:11.250
because the structural
change puts responsibility

01:58:11.250 --> 01:58:14.970
at a national level for people, for policy

01:58:14.970 --> 01:58:17.913
and for technology in one organisation.

01:58:18.810 --> 01:58:20.950
And to bring those things together

01:58:21.900 --> 01:58:24.950
gives us a really good
opportunity to lead the NHS

01:58:24.950 --> 01:58:27.300
and support the NHS in
a very different way

01:58:27.300 --> 01:58:29.340
and in a much simpler and more joined way.

01:58:29.340 --> 01:58:32.700
And I think, Richard, you've
previously called out examples

01:58:32.700 --> 01:58:34.230
like the long term workforce plan

01:58:34.230 --> 01:58:36.510
and the digital investment
that has been secured

01:58:36.510 --> 01:58:39.393
as examples of the
benefits that we can see.

01:58:40.440 --> 01:58:44.070
So the second part I think just to say is

01:58:44.070 --> 01:58:47.850
although the structural change
has come largely to an end,

01:58:47.850 --> 01:58:50.730
we have got, and colleagues in NHS England

01:58:50.730 --> 01:58:53.670
and in the service have been
really clear about this,

01:58:53.670 --> 01:58:56.045
we've got to go quite significantly

01:58:56.045 --> 01:58:58.290
beyond the structural change
to realise the benefits

01:58:58.290 --> 01:59:02.100
that is all about focusing
on our organisation,

01:59:02.100 --> 01:59:03.960
our ways of working,

01:59:03.960 --> 01:59:06.030
the culture that we have
in the organisation,

01:59:06.030 --> 01:59:09.330
and making sure that
what we do adds value.

01:59:09.330 --> 01:59:11.370
And as you say, the intention

01:59:11.370 --> 01:59:13.350
in the way we intend to do that

01:59:13.350 --> 01:59:15.363
is largely through NHS Impact.

01:59:17.010 --> 01:59:19.500
So, Richard, I'll not say anymore

01:59:19.500 --> 01:59:22.620
other than a very final quick word to say,

01:59:22.620 --> 01:59:24.870
we have done a very
extensive lessons learned.

01:59:24.870 --> 01:59:26.820
And I think it's important

01:59:26.820 --> 01:59:30.750
for colleagues in the
organisation, our stakeholders,

01:59:30.750 --> 01:59:32.430
to know that we have done that.

01:59:32.430 --> 01:59:36.240
It has been quite a challenging process

01:59:36.240 --> 01:59:38.790
in terms of listening to absorbing

01:59:38.790 --> 01:59:41.430
and considering the feedback
that has been given.

01:59:41.430 --> 01:59:44.310
And there are some quite
profound lessons in there

01:59:44.310 --> 01:59:45.510
if you were to enter

01:59:45.510 --> 01:59:49.770
into a restructure of that nature again.

01:59:49.770 --> 01:59:52.920
And of course there are
different ways to maintain

01:59:52.920 --> 01:59:54.660
ongoing agile deployment

01:59:54.660 --> 01:59:57.330
and incrementally improving
the way the organisation works

01:59:57.330 --> 02:00:00.540
rather than throwing
everything up in one go.

02:00:00.540 --> 02:00:04.290
So I think it's important to
say that we have done that

02:00:04.290 --> 02:00:06.480
and we'll continue to
reflect on some of the points

02:00:06.480 --> 02:00:07.313
that we've heard.

02:00:07.313 --> 02:00:09.210
- Thank you. Let's open up comments.

02:00:09.210 --> 02:00:11.613
Mike, I think you wanted
to ask a question.

02:00:12.639 --> 02:00:14.417
- Yeah, I was just
looking around the table.

02:00:14.417 --> 02:00:16.380
I think Andrew and I are the two non-execs

02:00:16.380 --> 02:00:17.213
who are left standing on
the original committee,

02:00:17.213 --> 02:00:18.513
two and a half years on.

02:00:19.360 --> 02:00:22.200
And that reflects on the scale of time

02:00:22.200 --> 02:00:23.730
and change that you've gone through.

02:00:23.730 --> 02:00:26.700
So I would start by
congratulating you and your team

02:00:26.700 --> 02:00:29.130
and indeed the rest of
the exec on seeing through

02:00:29.130 --> 02:00:33.660
what has been a very long
and disruptive process.

02:00:33.660 --> 02:00:36.720
And that inevitably has an
impact on the individuals

02:00:36.720 --> 02:00:37.920
at the frontline who are affected

02:00:37.920 --> 02:00:39.470
by the changes that we've made.

02:00:40.680 --> 02:00:44.340
I think it's a fair
comment on the committee

02:00:44.340 --> 02:00:46.143
that we're talking about,

02:00:46.143 --> 02:00:48.000
that it has set out to do the right thing.

02:00:48.000 --> 02:00:50.340
And we have had many robust discussions

02:00:50.340 --> 02:00:52.200
during the committee about
what the right thing is.

02:00:52.200 --> 02:00:54.000
Because always in these situations

02:00:54.000 --> 02:00:56.040
there's a degree of ambiguity,

02:00:56.040 --> 02:00:58.020
and you're making choices
about people's lives

02:00:58.020 --> 02:01:00.723
and livelihoods, which are never easy.

02:01:02.280 --> 02:01:04.440
You've reflected already
on some of the changes

02:01:04.440 --> 02:01:05.273
for the future.

02:01:05.273 --> 02:01:07.530
So to some extent it is the foundations

02:01:07.530 --> 02:01:08.910
and really building from this

02:01:08.910 --> 02:01:11.523
is the challenge of the
organisation going forward.

02:01:12.379 --> 02:01:13.320
And the last point I made,

02:01:13.320 --> 02:01:15.780
which is a point you made the end was

02:01:15.780 --> 02:01:16.890
you now have a handbook

02:01:16.890 --> 02:01:19.210
that you can hand to
any large organisation

02:01:20.160 --> 02:01:23.400
who will need to go
through this form of change

02:01:23.400 --> 02:01:27.653
and ultimately reorganisation disruption

02:01:27.653 --> 02:01:30.750
as a template that can
be used in the future.

02:01:30.750 --> 02:01:33.720
I hope that the NHS
won't go through the same

02:01:33.720 --> 02:01:35.070
process in the future.

02:01:35.070 --> 02:01:36.510
I hope that it will be done through

02:01:36.510 --> 02:01:39.090
a series of iterations
rather than wholesale change.

02:01:39.090 --> 02:01:39.923
But equally,

02:01:39.923 --> 02:01:42.480
if anybody else goes
through this kind of change,

02:01:42.480 --> 02:01:43.590
you've got the template.

02:01:43.590 --> 02:01:45.630
So, but thank you for your work
and for the work of the team

02:01:45.630 --> 02:01:47.601
and indeed your predecessor

02:01:47.601 --> 02:01:50.550
who certainly did quite
a lot in the beginning.

02:01:50.550 --> 02:01:52.561
- I mean, what I would
add actually is I would,

02:01:52.561 --> 02:01:57.561
is quite often easy to
forget that actually

02:01:57.750 --> 02:01:59.950
what we now do is the NHS England board

02:02:01.080 --> 02:02:02.370
and through the board

02:02:02.370 --> 02:02:04.770
and the subcommittee structure
below us is actually,

02:02:04.770 --> 02:02:07.680
we are now overseeing and challenging

02:02:07.680 --> 02:02:09.600
and working with the executives

02:02:09.600 --> 02:02:12.453
on what were the agendas of five entities.

02:02:13.320 --> 02:02:17.460
So before that there were
five different organisations

02:02:17.460 --> 02:02:20.040
running with boards,
running with objectives,

02:02:20.040 --> 02:02:23.910
running with agendas, running
with their own subcommittees.

02:02:23.910 --> 02:02:27.180
And actually we've combined in all those

02:02:27.180 --> 02:02:29.100
and it's only been possible to do that

02:02:29.100 --> 02:02:31.860
because actually the way that you,

02:02:31.860 --> 02:02:35.730
Steve with Amanda's
leadership and all the execs

02:02:35.730 --> 02:02:39.390
you've reorganised the way NHS England

02:02:39.390 --> 02:02:42.030
absorbing those other entities operate.

02:02:42.030 --> 02:02:43.800
And I've got the numbers
wrong now in my head,

02:02:43.800 --> 02:02:44.910
but there's some like

02:02:44.910 --> 02:02:48.379
over 100 different
programmes we're running

02:02:48.379 --> 02:02:50.700
and they're now sort of consolidated into

02:02:50.700 --> 02:02:52.712
but rationalised into

02:02:52.712 --> 02:02:55.380
and thematically into about 13 programmes.

02:02:55.380 --> 02:02:57.390
And the benefit of that,
when we think about

02:02:57.390 --> 02:02:58.980
how does NHS England

02:02:58.980 --> 02:03:01.638
representing now all of
these different entities

02:03:01.638 --> 02:03:02.940
engage with the systems,

02:03:02.940 --> 02:03:06.758
is that there are now five
different head office entities

02:03:06.758 --> 02:03:07.710
engaged with the systems,

02:03:07.710 --> 02:03:10.470
there is one consolidated system

02:03:10.470 --> 02:03:12.630
and also we have created,

02:03:12.630 --> 02:03:15.993
you have created a far
better coordination,

02:03:16.950 --> 02:03:21.060
interconnectivity
structure with NHS England

02:03:21.060 --> 02:03:24.620
as it reaches out into either
regions or into the ICBs

02:03:24.620 --> 02:03:28.200
and or into the trust and
elsewhere in the system.

02:03:28.200 --> 02:03:30.480
And that allows us to
form a much better use

02:03:30.480 --> 02:03:32.220
instead of having over
a hundred programmes

02:03:32.220 --> 02:03:35.070
communicating out request
information reports and so on.

02:03:35.070 --> 02:03:37.590
Actually they're more rationalised.

02:03:37.590 --> 02:03:39.210
I'm sure we'll still be often seen.

02:03:39.210 --> 02:03:40.830
I'm here from head office and here to help

02:03:40.830 --> 02:03:42.750
'cause that's always the burden.

02:03:42.750 --> 02:03:44.670
But actually in a way

02:03:44.670 --> 02:03:47.580
it's lost how fundamentally restructuring

02:03:47.580 --> 02:03:48.990
you have achieved at the same time

02:03:48.990 --> 02:03:52.770
as actually shrinking the
organisation by over a third.

02:03:52.770 --> 02:03:54.030
And it is one of the largest,

02:03:54.030 --> 02:03:56.250
if not the largest public sector,

02:03:56.250 --> 02:03:59.100
I think restructuring reorganisation

02:03:59.100 --> 02:04:01.020
that's been achieved
within an organisation

02:04:01.020 --> 02:04:04.127
that's continuing then
the centre to operate

02:04:04.127 --> 02:04:06.000
in that head office function.

02:04:06.000 --> 02:04:07.710
So it's a huge compliment to everybody

02:04:07.710 --> 02:04:09.867
and I think it does make already,

02:04:09.867 --> 02:04:11.700
and it will continue to
make a real difference,

02:04:11.700 --> 02:04:13.170
but it's not over.

02:04:13.170 --> 02:04:14.610
And so when we have this approval

02:04:14.610 --> 02:04:19.020
to pass now the continuing
work and the oversight

02:04:19.020 --> 02:04:21.270
and the governance and
how the machine runs,

02:04:21.270 --> 02:04:22.920
Amanda back into, you know,

02:04:22.920 --> 02:04:25.560
the execs without this specific committee,

02:04:25.560 --> 02:04:28.380
you know, I'm sure you'll
continue to make improvements

02:04:28.380 --> 02:04:32.670
and changes to the way that
the structure operates.

02:04:32.670 --> 02:04:33.503
Chris, do you want to?

02:04:33.503 --> 02:04:36.346
- Yeah, I just want you to
make one observation, Richard,

02:04:36.346 --> 02:04:37.530
just to thank a group of
people from my perspective

02:04:37.530 --> 02:04:39.750
as one of the executive members
that's been leading this,

02:04:39.750 --> 02:04:40.830
and just to mention a group

02:04:40.830 --> 02:04:43.050
that we haven't actually
pulled out specifically,

02:04:43.050 --> 02:04:47.100
which is we've been incredibly
reliant on our team leaders

02:04:47.100 --> 02:04:50.490
to effectively lead teams through

02:04:50.490 --> 02:04:54.540
a difficult kind of period
with considerable uncertainty

02:04:54.540 --> 02:04:56.310
for the staff that they lead.

02:04:56.310 --> 02:04:58.740
And just to make the observation that

02:04:58.740 --> 02:05:01.770
I know certainly in my area
I've been incredibly grateful

02:05:01.770 --> 02:05:03.930
for the work that the
team leaders have done.

02:05:03.930 --> 02:05:05.910
And just one specific group, which again,

02:05:05.910 --> 02:05:08.130
Steve has done some amazing work

02:05:08.130 --> 02:05:09.900
leading on behalf of the executive,

02:05:09.900 --> 02:05:12.030
but he's been working
with a group of design

02:05:12.030 --> 02:05:15.300
and implementation leads for
each individual directorate.

02:05:15.300 --> 02:05:18.450
And certainly mine has
actually had a day job to do

02:05:18.450 --> 02:05:21.330
alongside that really busy piece of work.

02:05:21.330 --> 02:05:22.590
And Amanda, you were talking about

02:05:22.590 --> 02:05:23.640
the work of the greener team.

02:05:23.640 --> 02:05:25.440
So in fact it's been the
leader of the greener team

02:05:25.440 --> 02:05:28.786
who's been our design
and implementation lead.

02:05:28.786 --> 02:05:30.120
So I just think it's
important to just recognise

02:05:30.120 --> 02:05:32.280
how reliant we've been
on our team leadership

02:05:32.280 --> 02:05:34.050
to take us through this process.

02:05:34.050 --> 02:05:36.210
- Thank you. Well said Chris.

02:05:36.210 --> 02:05:39.210
And so as we move on, can I just assume

02:05:39.210 --> 02:05:41.010
that the board is giving the approval?

02:05:41.010 --> 02:05:42.030
You'll see it in the paper

02:05:42.030 --> 02:05:45.600
and I read them out earlier
on, so thank you very much.

02:05:45.600 --> 02:05:48.186
Let's go on to miss item ten,

02:05:48.186 --> 02:05:51.069
I think Ruth, are you leading in on this?

02:05:51.069 --> 02:05:51.930
So it's specific equality duties.

02:05:51.930 --> 02:05:54.780
- [Ruth] I'm good.
- And it's a 2024 said review.

02:05:54.780 --> 02:05:57.960
- Yeah. On behalf of other
executive colleagues,

02:05:57.960 --> 02:05:59.959
I'm just going to say one sentence

02:05:59.959 --> 02:06:02.310
and hand over for just two
or three sentences more

02:06:02.310 --> 02:06:03.990
to Neil at the end.

02:06:03.990 --> 02:06:07.200
So it's a legal
requirement for NHS England

02:06:07.200 --> 02:06:11.280
to publish annually and how
we meeting our quality duties

02:06:11.280 --> 02:06:13.860
and improving the outcomes
for all is central

02:06:13.860 --> 02:06:17.010
obviously to our strategy. Neil.

02:06:17.010 --> 02:06:18.699
- Thank you very much.

02:06:18.699 --> 02:06:20.490
So briefly, hello. Good to see you again.

02:06:20.490 --> 02:06:22.530
The purpose of annual reporting is really

02:06:22.530 --> 02:06:25.020
to drive transparency and accountability.

02:06:25.020 --> 02:06:27.159
It gives us a chance to set out

02:06:27.159 --> 02:06:29.100
what we see the issues as being,
what we've done about them

02:06:29.100 --> 02:06:30.840
and what we're planning to do.

02:06:30.840 --> 02:06:34.170
I'd want you to be cited as
a board on the priorities

02:06:34.170 --> 02:06:38.430
of our regulator, the Equality
and Human Rights Commission,

02:06:38.430 --> 02:06:41.460
which is particularly focused
on black maternal health,

02:06:41.460 --> 02:06:43.290
on inappropriate detention,

02:06:43.290 --> 02:06:45.440
people with learning
disability and autism,

02:06:46.503 --> 02:06:47.336
or people with from ethnic minorities

02:06:47.336 --> 02:06:48.930
and under the Mental Health Act

02:06:48.930 --> 02:06:51.840
and also on the experiences
of ethnic minority staff.

02:06:51.840 --> 02:06:54.900
You'll see quite a bit set
out in the report there.

02:06:54.900 --> 02:06:56.760
We feel that progress has been made

02:06:56.760 --> 02:06:58.950
in a year where there
has been a lot of change,

02:06:58.950 --> 02:07:01.440
but to see real impact, it does take time.

02:07:01.440 --> 02:07:03.870
We don't need to set new objectives yet.

02:07:03.870 --> 02:07:07.590
So we are recommending that
those be extended until '25/'26,

02:07:07.590 --> 02:07:09.960
at which point we will
need to set new objectives.

02:07:09.960 --> 02:07:12.300
But on the whole, some good progress.

02:07:12.300 --> 02:07:16.350
But this is quite a thorough exercise

02:07:16.350 --> 02:07:17.760
across the whole organisation

02:07:17.760 --> 02:07:19.560
and it's important to see,

02:07:19.560 --> 02:07:22.781
for you to see the work
that's been happening.

02:07:22.781 --> 02:07:25.350
- Actually, I misunderstood that.

02:07:25.350 --> 02:07:27.720
Why will we need, I mean
we may, we could do,

02:07:27.720 --> 02:07:32.119
but why will we need to set
new objectives in '25/'26?

02:07:32.119 --> 02:07:33.117
- It's a requirement of the EHRC

02:07:33.117 --> 02:07:35.910
and the reporting that the
objectives are reviewed

02:07:35.910 --> 02:07:37.683
at least once every four years.

02:07:38.730 --> 02:07:40.414
- We could lead the same ones.

02:07:40.414 --> 02:07:41.340
Yeah. I'm not saying we
should by the way, but...

02:07:41.340 --> 02:07:44.370
- Yes, we need to do a more
further going exercise.

02:07:44.370 --> 02:07:45.540
- I understand. Yeah. Very good.

02:07:45.540 --> 02:07:49.830
So any comments or
questions are very clear.

02:07:49.830 --> 02:07:52.399
I think report, it sets out clearly

02:07:52.399 --> 02:07:55.320
our performance here on equality.

02:07:55.320 --> 02:07:56.850
Any comments or questions,

02:07:56.850 --> 02:07:58.653
reactions from members of the board?

02:08:01.740 --> 02:08:03.240
I think it's a very straightforward,

02:08:03.240 --> 02:08:05.130
it is actually a very well written report

02:08:05.130 --> 02:08:06.799
and very, very clear.

02:08:06.799 --> 02:08:08.399
So thank you very much for that.

02:08:10.020 --> 02:08:13.680
Let's go then on to, I think it's, yeah,

02:08:13.680 --> 02:08:14.910
it's not quite the last item yet.

02:08:14.910 --> 02:08:16.920
No, it's item number 11.

02:08:16.920 --> 02:08:20.815
So it's the oversight
and assessment framework.

02:08:20.815 --> 02:08:23.580
And I think, Steve, are you? Okay.

02:08:23.580 --> 02:08:28.580
- Thank you chair. So Adam
and I will cover this.

02:08:28.860 --> 02:08:31.710
We'll do so relatively briefly.

02:08:31.710 --> 02:08:34.230
So again, I hope the paper is helpful.

02:08:34.230 --> 02:08:36.150
So just to do a little bit about the why

02:08:36.150 --> 02:08:37.830
and the what with this.

02:08:37.830 --> 02:08:40.620
So again, big theme through
the board discussion

02:08:40.620 --> 02:08:45.063
just demonstrates how important
the NHS is to everybody.

02:08:46.260 --> 02:08:49.920
It has a profound impact on
people's health and wellbeing.

02:08:49.920 --> 02:08:53.880
It helps us keep mentally
and physically well

02:08:53.880 --> 02:08:56.403
and it's there for us when we get ill.

02:08:57.450 --> 02:09:02.200
So one of the things that
we do is to try and help

02:09:03.540 --> 02:09:05.940
identify success for the NHS

02:09:05.940 --> 02:09:09.453
and identify areas where
improvement is needed.

02:09:10.440 --> 02:09:14.190
And this is a sort of area that
has evolved over the years.

02:09:14.190 --> 02:09:18.540
So when ICBs were put
on a statutory footing,

02:09:18.540 --> 02:09:22.590
we published the operating
framework, which largely sets out

02:09:22.590 --> 02:09:26.580
how we try and work with each
other in pursuit of the goal

02:09:26.580 --> 02:09:29.070
of NHS England, which is to lead the NHS

02:09:29.070 --> 02:09:31.143
to secure high quality care for all.

02:09:33.210 --> 02:09:37.260
We also have what's known
as the oversight framework,

02:09:37.260 --> 02:09:40.770
which sets out how we determine success.

02:09:40.770 --> 02:09:44.190
So the kind of how we look at
what does success look like,

02:09:44.190 --> 02:09:47.100
where are the areas that we
might want to improve on,

02:09:47.100 --> 02:09:49.600
where are there areas we
might want to learn from?

02:09:50.940 --> 02:09:53.040
The oversight framework was last updated

02:09:53.040 --> 02:09:54.630
a couple of years ago.

02:09:54.630 --> 02:09:56.940
And at the time we made a commitment that

02:09:56.940 --> 02:10:00.030
as the new system
architecture came into place,

02:10:00.030 --> 02:10:04.230
we would reflect on the changes
that we might want to make

02:10:04.230 --> 02:10:07.440
to reflect the duties of
integrated care boards

02:10:07.440 --> 02:10:09.630
and integrated care systems

02:10:09.630 --> 02:10:12.573
across a much broader
range of responsibilities.

02:10:13.710 --> 02:10:17.430
And we went through a process
of talking to NHS leaders

02:10:17.430 --> 02:10:20.820
about what they would like to
see from that piece of work.

02:10:20.820 --> 02:10:21.930
And there were three or four things

02:10:21.930 --> 02:10:24.630
that came through in those conversations.

02:10:24.630 --> 02:10:27.870
There was requests for
clarity in terms of roles

02:10:27.870 --> 02:10:31.260
and responsibilities between
all of the consistent parts

02:10:31.260 --> 02:10:33.210
that make up the NHS.

02:10:33.210 --> 02:10:37.590
There was a request that we view success

02:10:37.590 --> 02:10:41.430
for both things that
are short term measures

02:10:41.430 --> 02:10:45.210
and also those things that
are more medium term measures.

02:10:45.210 --> 02:10:46.620
And Jane's example earlier

02:10:46.620 --> 02:10:49.830
is a good example of that
request about balance

02:10:49.830 --> 02:10:50.970
so that it we're concentrating

02:10:50.970 --> 02:10:52.830
on really important access issues,

02:10:52.830 --> 02:10:55.470
but we're also concentrating on prevention

02:10:55.470 --> 02:10:58.270
and improving outcomes and
reducing health inequalities.

02:10:59.400 --> 02:11:02.370
So there's a request for looking

02:11:02.370 --> 02:11:05.760
a broader set of success measures

02:11:05.760 --> 02:11:09.360
and also to make the assessments
slightly less subjective

02:11:09.360 --> 02:11:10.770
than perhaps it's been in the past

02:11:10.770 --> 02:11:12.370
because Emily's smiling already.

02:11:13.350 --> 02:11:16.563
But where there is a need for improvement,

02:11:16.563 --> 02:11:18.240
that the framework is underpinned

02:11:18.240 --> 02:11:22.623
by strong mature relationships
and an improvement focus.

02:11:23.820 --> 02:11:26.610
So we've developed on the
back of that engagement

02:11:26.610 --> 02:11:28.080
and update to the oversight framework

02:11:28.080 --> 02:11:29.613
which seeks to address that.

02:11:30.803 --> 02:11:33.870
And again, we've tested
that update with NHS leaders

02:11:33.870 --> 02:11:38.853
and key stakeholders over
the last four to five weeks.

02:11:40.530 --> 02:11:42.240
To try and pull all that together,

02:11:42.240 --> 02:11:44.850
we think that there are three key pillars.

02:11:44.850 --> 02:11:48.090
So the first is about
leadership development,

02:11:48.090 --> 02:11:51.540
the second is about
the oversight framework

02:11:51.540 --> 02:11:53.880
and the third is about NHS impact,

02:11:53.880 --> 02:11:56.510
which as we've said a couple
of times through this meeting

02:11:56.510 --> 02:11:59.670
is the mechanism by which
we're hoping the NHS

02:11:59.670 --> 02:12:01.860
will continuously improve.

02:12:01.860 --> 02:12:04.110
This pillar, this discussion is about

02:12:04.110 --> 02:12:06.570
the second of those things,

02:12:06.570 --> 02:12:08.220
which is the oversight framework.

02:12:09.720 --> 02:12:10.650
I won't go through the details

02:12:10.650 --> 02:12:11.790
'cause it's hopefully clearing the paper.

02:12:11.790 --> 02:12:15.630
But basically, the paper
sets out the approach

02:12:15.630 --> 02:12:19.290
that we'll take, which is
to consider the capability

02:12:19.290 --> 02:12:22.170
of providers and integrated care boards

02:12:22.170 --> 02:12:25.980
against their key functions.

02:12:25.980 --> 02:12:30.180
And we'll also assess a
range of measures and metrics

02:12:30.180 --> 02:12:33.660
that we call the delivery assessment

02:12:33.660 --> 02:12:35.670
which reflect the unique responsibility

02:12:35.670 --> 02:12:39.210
of each of those organisations
across the four purposes

02:12:39.210 --> 02:12:43.470
that we've set, which is
improving access and outcomes,

02:12:43.470 --> 02:12:45.300
reducing health inequalities,

02:12:45.300 --> 02:12:47.550
improving productivity and value

02:12:47.550 --> 02:12:50.163
and supporting wider economic development.

02:12:51.180 --> 02:12:54.693
That will lead to a rating
for each organisation.

02:12:55.710 --> 02:12:57.150
But because we are very keen

02:12:57.150 --> 02:13:00.510
to make sure that people
collaborate at a system level

02:13:00.510 --> 02:13:03.183
and take responsibility
for the whole population,

02:13:04.380 --> 02:13:06.540
we've introduced something
that's described in the paper

02:13:06.540 --> 02:13:09.832
as a double lock and that
will basically consider

02:13:09.832 --> 02:13:12.090
the total system performance

02:13:12.090 --> 02:13:17.090
across the key recovery
priorities that we set

02:13:17.640 --> 02:13:21.330
and where an organisation
is doing really well.

02:13:21.330 --> 02:13:24.300
If the broader system is struggling,

02:13:24.300 --> 02:13:25.710
then we will take that into account

02:13:25.710 --> 02:13:28.510
through the double lock process
to bind people together.

02:13:29.790 --> 02:13:31.650
A couple of final things just to say is,

02:13:31.650 --> 02:13:35.220
this framework focuses on the NHS,

02:13:35.220 --> 02:13:37.752
that's important to draw out,

02:13:37.752 --> 02:13:41.340
to think that is not because
we think that the NHS

02:13:41.340 --> 02:13:43.800
doesn't play a very important part

02:13:43.800 --> 02:13:46.560
in the wider integrated care partnership,

02:13:46.560 --> 02:13:51.000
but it wouldn't be for NHS
England to assess the progress

02:13:51.000 --> 02:13:53.670
for integrated care
partnerships priorities

02:13:53.670 --> 02:13:55.491
'cause they are developed

02:13:55.491 --> 02:13:57.117
between local government and the NHS.

02:13:58.800 --> 02:14:01.140
The assessment of
integrated care partnerships

02:14:01.140 --> 02:14:03.960
is much more the territory of the CQC

02:14:03.960 --> 02:14:06.873
who have been developing their approach.

02:14:08.130 --> 02:14:09.690
We've heard probably four things

02:14:09.690 --> 02:14:11.670
through the engagement that we've done,

02:14:11.670 --> 02:14:14.910
there's been broad
support for the approach.

02:14:14.910 --> 02:14:17.580
People have welcomed the
attempt to bring clarity

02:14:17.580 --> 02:14:19.200
around roles and responsibility.

02:14:19.200 --> 02:14:20.880
There's been a request
for us just to continue

02:14:20.880 --> 02:14:22.440
to reflect on whether
we've got the balance

02:14:22.440 --> 02:14:25.620
of the short term and medium term right

02:14:25.620 --> 02:14:29.100
and a request that although
it is very complicated

02:14:29.100 --> 02:14:31.710
as a system that we try
and make sure the framework

02:14:31.710 --> 02:14:33.150
is not overly complex

02:14:33.150 --> 02:14:35.700
and doesn't add too much burden

02:14:35.700 --> 02:14:37.620
and that we make sure there's alignment

02:14:37.620 --> 02:14:41.793
with other regimes such as
the CQC as far as possible.

02:14:43.080 --> 02:14:47.850
So the next steps for us are
to undertake a short period

02:14:47.850 --> 02:14:52.530
of public consultation,
which we intend to start

02:14:52.530 --> 02:14:54.300
at the back end of May.

02:14:54.300 --> 02:14:56.850
And then we will subject
to board approval,

02:14:56.850 --> 02:15:01.850
publish the final framework
and go live in July.

02:15:02.220 --> 02:15:04.560
During that time, we will be preparing

02:15:04.560 --> 02:15:07.200
how we need to change
within NHS England to work.

02:15:07.200 --> 02:15:09.750
This will be quite a significant change

02:15:09.750 --> 02:15:11.270
in the way that we approach it.

02:15:11.270 --> 02:15:14.823
It builds on the conversation
that we just had before.

02:15:15.660 --> 02:15:18.120
And the final part to this is to say

02:15:18.120 --> 02:15:20.760
alongside the oversight framework,

02:15:20.760 --> 02:15:22.860
we will publish three documents

02:15:22.860 --> 02:15:23.880
because the oversight framework

02:15:23.880 --> 02:15:26.130
isn't trying to solve all problems.

02:15:26.130 --> 02:15:28.170
So we'll publish what is known as

02:15:28.170 --> 02:15:30.360
the insightful board series,

02:15:30.360 --> 02:15:32.700
which will give a bit more information

02:15:32.700 --> 02:15:35.280
about the sorts of things
that highly performing,

02:15:35.280 --> 02:15:37.410
well governed boards should be looking at

02:15:37.410 --> 02:15:40.650
through their governance
on an ongoing basis.

02:15:40.650 --> 02:15:43.710
There'll be one for the provider sector,

02:15:43.710 --> 02:15:45.840
there'll be one for integrated care boards

02:15:45.840 --> 02:15:47.850
and importantly there'll
be one for NHS England

02:15:47.850 --> 02:15:50.880
to set out how we should govern ourselves

02:15:50.880 --> 02:15:54.450
in line with our responsibilities.

02:15:54.450 --> 02:15:56.340
So, the board doesn't need to approve

02:15:56.340 --> 02:15:58.611
the start of consultation,

02:15:58.611 --> 02:16:01.440
the board will need to
approve the framework

02:16:01.440 --> 02:16:03.780
once we've consulted on it,

02:16:03.780 --> 02:16:05.040
which will be during July.

02:16:05.040 --> 02:16:06.690
But I'll pause there Richard

02:16:06.690 --> 02:16:08.610
and happy to take any questions.

02:16:08.610 --> 02:16:10.585
- And you've got Adam in case

02:16:10.585 --> 02:16:11.592
of any difficult questions.
- [Steve] Absolutely.

02:16:11.592 --> 02:16:13.710
- So we've got...
- [Steve] I think

02:16:13.710 --> 02:16:14.910
you should maybe give him a really

02:16:14.910 --> 02:16:16.170
difficult question Richard.

02:16:16.170 --> 02:16:20.040
- Yes, we've got Mary, then
Mark, then Robert I think.

02:16:20.040 --> 02:16:20.873
So Mary.

02:16:22.020 --> 02:16:25.290
- Just two fairly straightforward
questions I think.

02:16:25.290 --> 02:16:29.179
When we say NHS executive,

02:16:29.179 --> 02:16:32.910
are we including in that
the regional offices?

02:16:32.910 --> 02:16:36.000
'Cause I wasn't quite sure when I read it.

02:16:36.000 --> 02:16:38.490
And then secondly,

02:16:38.490 --> 02:16:43.470
where do providers who
aren't part of the NHS fit

02:16:43.470 --> 02:16:45.720
in terms of monitoring?

02:16:45.720 --> 02:16:47.153
'Cause there are quite
a few mental health.

02:16:49.260 --> 02:16:51.810
- We'll capture. Let's go around Mark.

02:16:51.810 --> 02:16:53.842
- So thank you very much indeed.

02:16:53.842 --> 02:16:55.080
I suppose the thing I'm
slightly concerned about is

02:16:55.080 --> 02:16:56.430
that you've got on the left,

02:16:56.430 --> 02:16:57.810
I'm looking at the figures in the annex,

02:16:57.810 --> 02:16:58.950
the four core purposes.

02:16:58.950 --> 02:17:02.190
So improved population
health tackle inequalities

02:17:02.190 --> 02:17:03.750
and what I can't see

02:17:03.750 --> 02:17:06.750
is sort of sufficient
clinical measures of outcome.

02:17:06.750 --> 02:17:08.310
So you map them across

02:17:08.310 --> 02:17:10.590
and then I can't quite see, you know,

02:17:10.590 --> 02:17:13.399
how can you measure improvement
in population health

02:17:13.399 --> 02:17:15.000
and healthcare if you don't know

02:17:15.000 --> 02:17:16.470
what the baseline population health is

02:17:16.470 --> 02:17:18.810
on a sort of ICB by ICB basis

02:17:18.810 --> 02:17:20.580
and simply 'cause they will differ

02:17:20.580 --> 02:17:22.437
and similarly in inequalities.

02:17:22.437 --> 02:17:24.360
And so I'm just sort of slightly concerned

02:17:24.360 --> 02:17:26.918
that what's on the right
isn't going to map across

02:17:26.918 --> 02:17:27.751
to the left

02:17:27.751 --> 02:17:29.850
to actually give us measures on whether

02:17:29.850 --> 02:17:31.260
health outcomes are being improved.

02:17:31.260 --> 02:17:33.723
- That might be a question
for Adam. I dunno.

02:17:37.530 --> 02:17:38.700
Robert.

02:17:38.700 --> 02:17:41.607
- Yeah, I mean, I think
this is really helpful

02:17:41.607 --> 02:17:43.140
and I mean it's fundamental

02:17:43.140 --> 02:17:47.340
to the functioning of this
board, it seems to me.

02:17:47.340 --> 02:17:48.810
I mean in shorthand it,

02:17:48.810 --> 02:17:51.390
it's to oversee the
performance of the ICBs

02:17:51.390 --> 02:17:54.690
having delegated
responsibility for performance

02:17:54.690 --> 02:17:56.073
and financial management.

02:17:57.360 --> 02:18:01.560
Just returning to an earlier
topic of this board discussion,

02:18:01.560 --> 02:18:04.380
the research thing, would
I be right in assuming

02:18:04.380 --> 02:18:06.600
that the research responsibility

02:18:06.600 --> 02:18:08.910
of ICBs will be captured in securing

02:18:08.910 --> 02:18:10.353
transformation and learning?

02:18:12.150 --> 02:18:15.278
- So do you want to have a go at those

02:18:15.278 --> 02:18:16.718
or bring Adam in on those.

02:18:16.718 --> 02:18:18.048
- I'll have a quick go

02:18:18.048 --> 02:18:20.238
and then Adam can correct
all the bits I get wrong.

02:18:20.238 --> 02:18:22.020
So Mary, just on your
point about NHS England,

02:18:22.020 --> 02:18:24.003
I think as opposed to NHS executive.

02:18:26.460 --> 02:18:29.103
No, it was actually
called that 25 years ago.

02:18:30.480 --> 02:18:34.650
So we've deliberately not
drawn a distinction between

02:18:34.650 --> 02:18:36.930
regional teams and national teams

02:18:36.930 --> 02:18:38.430
because we are trying to work

02:18:38.430 --> 02:18:40.950
as a single integrated organisation.

02:18:40.950 --> 02:18:43.200
That's been something the
services asked us to do.

02:18:43.200 --> 02:18:45.330
So we're just saying NHS England.

02:18:45.330 --> 02:18:46.410
- I think that's wonderful.

02:18:46.410 --> 02:18:49.380
It just might need to be made
clear in the eventual paper

02:18:49.380 --> 02:18:51.099
so that everybody knows.

02:18:51.099 --> 02:18:53.881
And I have been in the health
service more than 25 years,

02:18:53.881 --> 02:18:57.590
so I'm going excuse myself.

02:18:57.590 --> 02:18:59.640
- So on the non NHS providers,

02:18:59.640 --> 02:19:01.290
you're absolutely right,
there's a number in place.

02:19:01.290 --> 02:19:04.770
We won't oversee them
through this framework,

02:19:04.770 --> 02:19:07.230
but they will be overseeing
through the contracts

02:19:07.230 --> 02:19:09.280
that they hold with their commissioners

02:19:11.670 --> 02:19:13.440
so that will also apply to primary care

02:19:13.440 --> 02:19:16.173
and dentistry and and so on at this point.

02:19:17.040 --> 02:19:19.324
Very quickly, just on the
clinical measures Mark,

02:19:19.324 --> 02:19:21.000
so you are absolutely right to say

02:19:21.000 --> 02:19:25.023
population health priorities
will differ through each ICB.

02:19:25.950 --> 02:19:30.510
So, what we have done is
select some that we think

02:19:30.510 --> 02:19:32.520
are going to likely to
be relevant everywhere.

02:19:32.520 --> 02:19:34.893
So for example, blood pressure control,

02:19:36.630 --> 02:19:38.940
and population health
in its broadest sense,

02:19:38.940 --> 02:19:40.470
the way that you are describing it,

02:19:40.470 --> 02:19:41.703
that would be tested

02:19:41.703 --> 02:19:43.860
through the annual ICB
capability assessment.

02:19:43.860 --> 02:19:45.758
So we would be saying,

02:19:45.758 --> 02:19:48.330
how have you done your
joint needs assessment?

02:19:48.330 --> 02:19:50.520
How do you understand your
population health need?

02:19:50.520 --> 02:19:53.160
What are the priorities,
what progress has been made?

02:19:53.160 --> 02:19:55.230
'Cause that will be different for each

02:19:55.230 --> 02:19:57.150
integrated care board.

02:19:57.150 --> 02:19:58.950
It's also help maybe helpful just to say

02:19:58.950 --> 02:20:02.730
that we've got Duncan and Claire Fuller

02:20:02.730 --> 02:20:03.990
leading a piece of work

02:20:03.990 --> 02:20:05.400
with the national clinical directors,

02:20:05.400 --> 02:20:07.320
with medical directors, chief nurses

02:20:07.320 --> 02:20:09.810
and others to give us
advice on the measures

02:20:09.810 --> 02:20:11.880
that we've developed

02:20:11.880 --> 02:20:14.880
and to say how could you make
those more clinically relevant

02:20:14.880 --> 02:20:16.830
and more outcomes focused.

02:20:16.830 --> 02:20:18.960
We may not be able to change
all of them in the first year

02:20:18.960 --> 02:20:20.850
'cause we might not collect the data,

02:20:20.850 --> 02:20:22.680
but we'll create ourselves effectively

02:20:22.680 --> 02:20:24.753
a list for further development.

02:20:25.710 --> 02:20:28.980
And then finally on the research point,

02:20:28.980 --> 02:20:31.737
yes it will be in that bucket.

02:20:31.737 --> 02:20:33.510
We actually have a statute of duty

02:20:33.510 --> 02:20:36.240
to assess ICBs on research

02:20:36.240 --> 02:20:37.890
and we're just looking at
whether there's a measure

02:20:37.890 --> 02:20:41.340
that we could use in
the framework as well.

02:20:41.340 --> 02:20:44.310
- So, Adam, have you got
to correct any of that?

02:20:44.310 --> 02:20:45.143
Is that okay?

02:20:46.020 --> 02:20:48.270
- That was a point an
a plus. That's alright.

02:20:49.140 --> 02:20:50.760
The only key part I
would add there, chair,

02:20:50.760 --> 02:20:52.980
is I think it's really
important just to amplify

02:20:52.980 --> 02:20:55.920
this is a developmental
journey for the service now.

02:20:55.920 --> 02:20:58.650
So I think there are areas
where we have got good data

02:20:58.650 --> 02:21:00.450
and areas where we need to improve it.

02:21:00.450 --> 02:21:02.160
And particularly when
you look at, for example,

02:21:02.160 --> 02:21:04.320
the clinical measures,
what we really struck by

02:21:04.320 --> 02:21:08.340
has been the level of clinical
engagement and excitement

02:21:08.340 --> 02:21:11.610
to start to look at what are
the better integrated measures

02:21:11.610 --> 02:21:14.580
by which we can therefore
measure ourselves more robustly.

02:21:14.580 --> 02:21:17.641
And so I think what we're
trying to do as part of this

02:21:17.641 --> 02:21:20.130
is to launch it, there will
be areas we'll have to tweak,

02:21:20.130 --> 02:21:21.750
there will be things you won't have right,

02:21:21.750 --> 02:21:25.230
both in terms of what we are
assessing ICBs for, providers

02:21:25.230 --> 02:21:27.000
and also how we're working ourselves.

02:21:27.000 --> 02:21:28.980
But I think if we keep
that under regular review,

02:21:28.980 --> 02:21:29.850
I think the key part,

02:21:29.850 --> 02:21:32.280
if we can get the right
outcomes based data

02:21:32.280 --> 02:21:33.870
and we start to measure
that in the right way,

02:21:33.870 --> 02:21:36.360
we can look at the things
we need to do for the future

02:21:36.360 --> 02:21:39.060
but see the operational
benefit in the here and now.

02:21:39.060 --> 02:21:41.400
And lastly, yes, research and innovation

02:21:41.400 --> 02:21:42.690
will be tested through that domain

02:21:42.690 --> 02:21:46.213
as we go through the oversight mechanisms.

02:21:46.213 --> 02:21:49.560
- Thank you. Adam thank
you, Steve, thank you. So...

02:21:49.560 --> 02:21:51.270
- One second.
- Of course.

02:21:51.270 --> 02:21:55.560
- Just to conclude this bit,

02:21:55.560 --> 02:22:00.560
I think the next step,
which is as Steve said,

02:22:00.660 --> 02:22:04.440
the formal consultation
is really important

02:22:04.440 --> 02:22:05.650
but it builds on,

02:22:05.650 --> 02:22:08.040
and I just really want to say again

02:22:08.040 --> 02:22:12.480
it's a big thank you to Adam,
to Steve and their teams,

02:22:12.480 --> 02:22:15.750
what, six months a year now of co-creation

02:22:15.750 --> 02:22:19.860
with colleagues across the
NHS to get to this stage.

02:22:19.860 --> 02:22:22.740
Adam, the fact that you
are also a jobbing ICB

02:22:22.740 --> 02:22:26.190
chief executive, you
know, has been invaluable.

02:22:26.190 --> 02:22:28.512
But Steve, the number of people
who've been involved in this

02:22:28.512 --> 02:22:29.640
'cause we are trying to do something

02:22:29.640 --> 02:22:33.330
really quite sophisticated
and it is recognising

02:22:33.330 --> 02:22:36.600
that we're what, two years on
now from the creation of ICBs,

02:22:36.600 --> 02:22:39.210
the time is right to be clearer

02:22:39.210 --> 02:22:43.980
about what the role is of ICBs
as distinct from providers,

02:22:43.980 --> 02:22:46.560
but find a way, as you've
described through the double lock,

02:22:46.560 --> 02:22:48.253
that means that actually

02:22:48.253 --> 02:22:50.700
we're still all aiming for
the same set of outcomes

02:22:50.700 --> 02:22:52.710
and we don't want to set something up

02:22:52.710 --> 02:22:54.900
that pulls in in different directions.

02:22:54.900 --> 02:22:57.840
So I do think Adam's right
to say, as Steve has said,

02:22:57.840 --> 02:22:59.490
this is not going to be a we'll consult,

02:22:59.490 --> 02:23:02.010
suddenly it will be the
perfect finished article

02:23:02.010 --> 02:23:04.020
and nothing will ever change again.

02:23:04.020 --> 02:23:05.760
But it's significant move on from

02:23:05.760 --> 02:23:07.673
where we are at the moment.

02:23:07.673 --> 02:23:09.330
And the thing for me that has been

02:23:09.330 --> 02:23:13.020
so encouraging has been the
level of engagement in this

02:23:13.020 --> 02:23:14.760
and people's desire to get something

02:23:14.760 --> 02:23:16.560
that actually feels like
it is the right balance

02:23:16.560 --> 02:23:19.410
between being, you know, workable, useful,

02:23:19.410 --> 02:23:21.960
actually measuring the
right things that matter,

02:23:21.960 --> 02:23:23.130
but also the work that colleagues

02:23:23.130 --> 02:23:24.330
in this organisation have done

02:23:24.330 --> 02:23:26.610
to really challenge
themselves on, you know,

02:23:26.610 --> 02:23:29.430
as it says here, genuinely
what are the things

02:23:29.430 --> 02:23:33.630
that then really recognise
and reward high performance

02:23:33.630 --> 02:23:36.180
as well as the things
that are better rehearsed,

02:23:36.180 --> 02:23:38.190
if I'm honest, sometimes in this forum

02:23:38.190 --> 02:23:40.530
about the things that
we're doing to support

02:23:40.530 --> 02:23:43.800
those whose performance
is more challenged.

02:23:43.800 --> 02:23:45.273
- Thank you. Very good.

02:23:46.170 --> 02:23:48.120
There's one item which is a starred item

02:23:48.120 --> 02:23:50.956
just for information
only, which you can see,

02:23:50.956 --> 02:23:54.330
which is a sum of the outcomes
and registries directions

02:23:54.330 --> 02:23:56.473
which were issued to NHS signal England by

02:23:56.473 --> 02:23:59.250
the Secretary of State in March, 2024.

02:23:59.250 --> 02:24:01.900
So if we just note those,
that would be very helpful.

02:24:03.030 --> 02:24:05.860
And then lastly, it's any other business

02:24:07.199 --> 02:24:08.220
and so is there any other business?

02:24:08.220 --> 02:24:10.410
I'll just look around the table going left

02:24:10.410 --> 02:24:12.860
and any other business
from anybody going around,

02:24:14.310 --> 02:24:18.660
in which case let me call
this board meeting to a close

02:24:18.660 --> 02:24:20.688
to thank everyone for attending to that

02:24:20.688 --> 02:24:22.740
and the executives for the papers to thank

02:24:22.740 --> 02:24:24.150
the presenters actually very good

02:24:24.150 --> 02:24:26.460
and very clear presentations and good Q&amp;A.

02:24:26.460 --> 02:24:27.600
Thank you very much.

02:24:27.600 --> 02:24:29.130
And just to say to the board

02:24:29.130 --> 02:24:32.408
that the debrief session
on the private board

02:24:32.408 --> 02:24:33.983
and the public board
is on the third floor.

02:24:35.460 --> 02:24:37.103
So thank you very much everybody.

